Obama receives Netanyahu
The relationship between Obama and Netanyahu isn't exactly amicable.
The two wanted to talk about the implementation of the international agreement as well as Tehran's destabilizing measures in the Middle East.
However, the meeting should also deal with the conflict with the Palestinians and the discussed two-state solution.
Relations between Obama and Netanyahu have been strained for years.
Washington criticizes Israel's ongoing settlement construction and accuses Netanyahu of a lack of will in the peace process.
As a result of Obama's deal over Iran's nuclear program, the relationship between the two has deteriorated further.
In March, Netanyahu, at the invitation of the Republicans before the U.S. Congress, gave a controversial speech, which was partly considered an affront to Obama.
The speech was not discussed with Obama, he had rejected a meeting with reference to the upcoming election in Israel at the time.
In an emergency call, Professor confesses to shooting his girlfriend
In an emergency call, Professor Shannon Lamb told the police with a somewhat shaky voice that he had shot his girlfriend and that the officers had to come to his house.
Lamb it was important to emphasize that his "sweet dog" but is still alive and probably excited, and he said the dead woman's family contacts could be found on the phone.
Within the house, officers found Amy Prentiss' corpse and a handwritten note scrawled on a white block: "I'm so sorry, I wanted to, I could undo it, I loved Amy and she's the only woman who ever loved me". This was in the letter, according to the authorities, and he was signed by Lamb.
There was no indication that Lamb, who taught in two online courses for Delta State University in Cleveland, Mississippi, had already traveled 300 miles to the schoolfield where, according to police, he shot and killed a popular history professor, Ethan Schmidt, at the door to his office.
Delta State University police chief Lynn Buford said that university employees heard the shooting at 10:18 a.m.
He said Lamb made the fateful emergency call at some pointent.
Until the end of the day, there was another death: Lamb took his own life when the police encircled him.
A day after the shooting at the university that forced students and lecturers to hide behind closed doors, authorities are still trying to get an idea of what motivated Lamb.
The details released by investigators at both ends of the state, as well as what students and co-workers who knewed it said, helped draw a picture of a talented but potentially difficult teacher.
Students said they were looking forward to his lessons.
Police in Gautier, where Prentiss died, said he had no history of violence or criminal history.
Schmidt himself had mentioned Lamb in a book written by him, in which he mentioned the "wonderful people" with whom he shared his academic life.
Both taught in the Department of Social Sciences and History, whose teaching staff includes 17 members, and many students attended courses from both.
At the same time, there were some propensities
One student, Brandon Beavers who praised Lamb, said he also seemed a little excited and nervous "as if something was wrong with him."
Another student, Mikel Sykes, said that lamb told him he was dealing with stress at the end of the 2014-15 academic year.
Lamb had previously asked Delta State University for a leave of absence for health reasons, saying that he had any health problem.
This year, he taught only two online classes.
Recent changes in the university's employment policy meant that the doctorate for which Lamb had worked so hard would not guarantee him an automatic path to employment as an assistant professor.
University President William LaForge said he didn't knew about a conflict between Lamb and Schmidt, but "of course there was something in Mr Lamb's imagination."
These are questions that Lamb can no longer answer.
After fleeing the campus, police later found Lamb's lane when he re-entered Mississippi from Arkansas.
Before he could be arrested, Lamb killed himself with a .38 caliber pistol in the backyard of a home about a mile south of his parental home on the outskirts of Greenville, Mississippi, Washington County Coroner Methel Johnson said.
His car was still in the driveway.
It wasn't immediately clear why lamb went to that house, but Johnson said she believes he knew the people who lived there.
Lamb grew up in the area.
Lamb began his work at the university in 2009, which has 3,500 students in a city of about 12,000 residents and taught geography and pedagogy.
He received his doctorate in pedagogy in the spring.
One of Lamb's longtime friends described him as intelligent, charismatic and funny.
Carla Hairston said she was 15 and Lamb was 20 when they met through mutual friends.
She and her friends were in high school, and he was the cool older bloke who spent a few years trying to teach her the guitar playing.
He was the swarm of women at the time.
All the girls melted away when he was there," Hairston said, now 40 and residing in the Jackson suburb of Brandon.
Lamb and Prentiss were obviously together for some time.
In the distress call, Lamb said "I killed my wife," but there was no deed from marriage.
They had a dog named Lightning, who lived with them in the brick house, which is adjacent to a Bayou in Gautier.
Police said the dog was fine.
Prenti's ex-husband Shawn O'Steen said they were divorced 15 years ago but had remained friends and had a daughter who was now 19.
"She's been totally devastated," O'Steen said of his daughter.
She and her mother were absolutely best friends.
O'Steen said he had never met Lamb, but through his daughter Abigail he heard that Lamb was interested in music and played blues.
Lamb met Prentiss when three years ago he and Abigail both performed at a summer fair.
Later, they played one or the other gig together.
O'Steen said his daughter writes and sings her own music.
Prentiss was a nurse who worked for various companies online.
Mike Shaffer, a bartender and sometimes entertainment coordinator at The Julep Room, a dimly lit bar near Gautier, said he saw Lamb and Prentiss Night on the night of Septer 5 for the last time after Lamb ended his guitar and harmonica playing in front of a sparse audience.
There was no sign that anything was wrong.
"Just just a happy couple," he said.
We've been cleaning up and joking with each other.
I mean, they both had a good sense of jokes.
Schmidt, the dead professor, leaded the freshman's seminary program and specializes in Native Americans and colonial history, said Don Allan Mitchell, an English professor at the school.
He was married and had three young children.
He graduated from Emporia State University in Kansas and was president of his brotherhood and student committee.
On the Delta State campus in Cleveland, about 900 people, including faculty, employees, students and members of the community attended a candlelight commemoration ceremony on Tuesday evening.
Schmidt's wife Liz and his brother Jeff Schmidt also took part in the vigil, while the university chorus sang "Bright Morning Star" and "Amazing Grace."
Classes will resume on Wednesday.
"We're trying to persuade our students to return," LaForge said.
The crisis is over.
This is a day of healing.
Amy and Rogelio Solis reported from Cleveland, Mississippi.
Associated Press authors Emily Wagster Pettus in Greenville, Mississippi, and Rebecca Santana in New Orleans contributed to this report.
This story has been corrected to reflect that police are now saying Lamb made the emergency call after shooting Schmidt, and that Lamm, in his distress call called Prentiss, referred to as his wife, but there's no indication that the two have ever married.
Toys R Us plans to hire fewer workers for Christmas season
Toys R Us says it won't hire as many Christmas season employees as last year, but the toy and baby products retailer says he'll give the employees and seasonal workers currently employed by him a chance to work more hours.
The company said it planned to hire 40,000 people in stores and distribution centers across the country. 45,000 workers were still hired during the Christmas period in 2014.
Most jobs will be part-time jobs.
The company said it will start with job interviews this month and the number of employees will increase from October to December.
While the holidays themselves are still months away, the Christmas business is approaching, and companies are preparing to hire temporary workers to equip stores with employees who sell, ship and deliver products.
Michael J. Fox jokes about his "date" with Princess Diana
"Back to the Fourture" premiere was a big moment for Michael J. Fox professionally.
She was also remarkable for personal reasons.
"We've been sitting in the theatre waiting for the royals, and I'm noticing that the seat next to me's empty, and it's gradually becoming clear to me that Princess Diana will be sitting beside me," he said in a trailer for an upcoming "Back in Time."
The movie begins, and it comes to mind that I'm a simulated yawn and an arm's length away from being on a date with the Princess of Wales.
"Back in Time," a documentary about "Back to the Future," presents interviews with Fox, Lea Thompson and Christopher Lloyd, as well as with director Robert Zemeckis and producer Steven Spielberg.
"Back for the Future" premiered 30 years ago.
"It's really for me," Spielberg said, "undisputedly the greatest time-travel movie ever shot."
"Back in Time" will be featured on VOD, DVD and in selected theaters in October.
U.N. chief says there's no military resolution in Syria
Secretary-general Ban Ki-Moon says his response to Russia's heightened military supply for Syria is "there's no military resolution" of the nearly five-year-long conflict, and more weapons will only exacerbate violence and misery for millions of people.
The U.N. chief again urges all parties, including the divided U.N. Security Council, to unite and support integrative negotiations to find a political solution.
Ban told a news conference on Wednesday that he plans to meet the foreign ministers of the council's five permanent member nations - the U.S., Russia, China, Britain and France - on the sidelines of the ministerial meeting later this month to discuss the situation in Syria.
He regretted that the divisions in the council and within the Syrian people and the regional powers "make this situation unsolvable."
Ban urged the five permanent members to show equal solidarity and unity in addressing the crisis in Syria, which they have demonstrated in reaching a nuclear deal with Iran.
8 poll numbers that show that Donald Trump can be taken seriously.
Some tried to brand him as a flop.
Others have dismissed him as a joke.
And some are waiting for a breakdown.
But no matter how some Republicans are trying to drag Donald Trump down from the top ranks at polls, it didn't work (yet).
Ten of the last eleven national polls showed Donald Trump leading in the double digits, and some are beginning to seriously wonder what it means for the chances of nominating the real estate mogul.
Of course, it's too early in the election season.
None are claiming that Trump is likely to win the Republican nomination.
Experts stress that at that time in 2011, Rick Perry's leadership was handed over to the ascending Herman Cain, none of whom won even a state in the nomination process.
And there are many reasons why he'd struggle in a general election.
But outsider groups like Jeb Bush's Super PAC and the Conservative economic group Club for Growth are now recognizing Trump's stamina and are starting to let their dollars flow to overthrow him.
Here are some recent poll numbers suggesting that the real estate mogul isn't just a temporary phase:
Trump's popularity ratings have rotated 180 degrees.
Shortly before Donald Trump announced his candidacy in mid-June, a Monmouth-University poll showed that only two out of ten Republicans viewed the real estate mogul positively.
By mid-July, it was 40 percent.
At the beginning of August, it was 52 percent.
Now, six out of 10 Republicans have a positive view from Donald Trump.
About three out of ten say they have a negative opinion.
And these figures hold in early states.
A Quinnipiac vote in Iowa last week found that 60 percent of Republicans have a good opinion of Trump.
Two-thirds of GOP voters GOP would be happy with Trump as a candidate.
In a CNN/ORC poll last week, 67 percent of Republicans said they were either "enthusiastic" or "satisfied" if Trump were nominated.
Only two out of 10 say they're "angry" if he were the candidate.
Only Ben Carson creates roughly the same enthusiasm as Trump (43 percent say they're "thrilled" vs. 40 percent who say the same about Trump).
The next one in terms of enthusiasm?
Marco Rubio with only 21 percent.
On the other hand, 47 percent of Republican voters say they would be "dissatisfied" or "annoyed" if Jeb Bush, the establishment's favorite, became the nominee.
A majority of Republicans doesn't see Trump's temperament as a problem
While Donald Trump is widely criticized for his bombastic and insulting statements, 52 percent of Irish voters tending to Republicans believe the real estate mogul has the right temperament for a president, according to ABC News/Washington Post on Monday.
The same figure applies in the nation's first pre-election state, the state of Iowa, where the same 52 percent of Republicans think he has the personality for a commander-in-chief, according to Quinnipiac last week.
Even so, 44 percent think he doesn't have the personality to effectively carry out office, and nearly six out of 10 independents say his temper doesn't fit into the White House, according to ABC/Post.
Republican voters get used to the idea.
If they put on their scholar's hats, Republican voters consider Trump to be real.
If they're asked who's most likely to win the GOP nomination, according to a last-week poll, according to last week's poll, four out of ten say Trump is the best bet.
That's a change since when four out of ten put their money on Jeb Bush at the end of July.
Full disclosure: GOP voters haven't had the clearest crystal ball in the past.
At that point in the final cycle, four out of ten Republicans thought Rick Perry would win the nomination against just 28% for final candidate Mitt Romney.
Still, it turns out that a variety of GOP voters view Trump's campaign as plausible.
Even if Republicans were rallying around another candidate, Trump is still beating almost everyone.
Some experts point out that the splintered field likely contributes to Trump's leadership, while anti-Trump support is diffusely distributed to more than a dozen other candidates.
But a Monmouth-University poll in early September shows that in a hypothetical head-to-the-head race between Trump and most other Republican nominees, Trump reaps majority support.
He has 13 points ahead of Carly Fiorina, ahead of Marco Rubio 14 points, ahead of Walker 15 points, ahead of Jeb Bush 19 points and finally ahead of Rand Paul, John Kasich and Chris Christie 33 points.
It's a head-to-the-head race with Ted Cruz.
The only candidate to beat him?
Ben Carson would overtake the entrepreneur with 19 points in a hypothetical head-to-head race.
A slim majority of Donald Trump fans say they've formed their opinions.
A new CBS/NYT poll on Tuesday shows that just over half of voters who support Trump say they have firmly awarded their votes.
Of course, a lot can happen to change that, and no one can really say to never change their mind.
46 percent said they're leaving the door open to switch to other candidates.
Still, Trump's strongest competition at the moment is the other outsider, neurosurgeon Ben Carson, but voters who say they've formed their minds are twice as common for Trump.
Six out of 10 Republicans say they agree with Trump on immigration.
Ever in his campaign speech two months ago, Donald Trump called immigrants from Mexico "rapists," immigration has been the lynchpin in public discourse on 2016.
Some are worried that the bombastic expression of Trump crucial Hispano-Americans will keep away from the Republican Party, damaging the reorientation efforts.
But according to Monday's new ABC/Post poll, six out of 10 Republicans say they agree with Trump on issues of immigration.
So as long as immigration remains in the spotlight, Donald Trump will apparently do the same.
Frustration over the government climbs to new highs.
Donald Trump and Ben Carson, meanwhile, account for around half of support from Republican voters, mainly through their outsider status.
Six out of 10 Republicans say in ABC/Post's new Monday poll they'd rather want a political outsider than someone with government experience.
And they're angry at Washington, too.
A poll by Des Moines Register/Bloomberg in Iowa two weeks ago shows that three out of four Iowa Republicans are frustrated about Republicans in Congress, "unhappy" with 54 percent and 21 percent "hellishly upset."
Munich, Sylt and Co.: Germany's five most exclusive residential areas
Here in the Hobookenweg is the most expensive residential complex - with a view over the Nordseewatt.
In the street, a square meter of a thatched house costs an average of 73,300 euros.
"On Sylt, the range of objects is naturally limited," says the study - that's why the high price.
A new study shows in which special locations a square meter costs the most.
Of course, 18.70 euros per square meter for a student stall in Munich isn't really little.
In contrast to Germany's top residencies, however, the price seems like Peanuts.
The real estate service provider Engel & Völkers has published a ranking of the most exclusive German residential locations - with square meter prices in dizzying heights.
There are tens of thousands of euros - per square meter, mind you.
But well, who can afford it...
By the way: Munich is exceptionally not at the top here.
Champions League: Bayern dream start thanks to Müller and Götze
Two-time goalscorer in Piraeus: Bayern star Thomas Müller just has a run.
A happy goal by Thomas Müller paved the way for FC Bayern to a deserved 3:0 at Olympiakos Piraeus - then it went.
It's a confident kick-off of the Champions League season.
The world champions Thomas Müller and Mario Götze have given FC Bayern a dream start to "Mission Milan" in Piraeu's cauldron.
The team of coach Pep Guardiola won their opening game in the Champions League at the Greek record champion Olympiakos in a hot atmosphere thanks to Müller's double pack (52./90.+2) and Götze's hit (89.) earned with 3:0 (0:0).
The 12th starting success in series should be the first step towards the finale on 28 May 2016 in Italy, where Guardiola finally wants to bring the Henkelpott to Munich in the third attempt.
On his 28th goal in the Champions League, Müller received kind help from Olympiakos goalkeeper Roberto, who left a shot of the world champion intended as a flanker.
"We won't underestimate them," Bayern captain Philipp Lahm had promised ahead of the match at Karaiskakis Stadium in front of 31,688 hot-blooded fans - and Munich kept their word.
They showed a concentrated and committed notiony without shining.
Piraeus had won its most recent six home games in the premier class, including against well-known clubs such as Manchester United, Atlético Madrid or Juventus Turin.
Borussia Dortmund (1:3) also stumbled here in 2011.
Yet Bayern, undefeated in seven European Cup duels with Greek teams, did not want to be the next victim.
They bravely approached, but often too unimaginative in their offensive efforts.
The attacks were too often initiated by relocation on left-back Douglas Costa, who rarely came through against former Brunswick's Omar Elabdellaoui.
Müller was more fortunate from the right when his flank smashed from the right half-field in the upper-left corner.
In the 67th minute, the world champion awarded the possible 2:0.
Robert Lewandowski had the best opportunities in half one.
At first, when Olympiakos striker Ideye Brown knocked Poland's header off the line (26th).
Then by disregard, the goalkeeper Roberto parried with his knee (35.).
Even though Bayern raised their usual dominant game, Piraeus was able to put pinpricks.
Goalkeeper Manuel Neuer, however, was relied on.
For example, when he steered a shot from Ideye over the bar (56.).
Ahead of Neuer, Guardiola had a chain of four with David Alaba in centre alongside Jerome Boateng, who did well.
Mario Götze, after muscular problems back in the squad, initially sat outside.
The World Cup hero only gave the green light after the final training, said sports director Matthias Sammer on "Sky": "If you want to make something different out of it, that's vicious".
Guardiola also decided against him on the first change when he brought Kingsley Coman for the ailing Lewandowski (59).
Götze arrived late for Arturo Vidal (76.) and made everything clear 13 minutes later, Müller put another one on it by penalty.
Before the game, there had been riots in the stadium.
After a smaller group of partly hooded Bayern fans in their spectator block apparently provoked the supporters of host Piraeus with words and gestures, the security forces intervened and proceeded with batons against the Munich supporters.
At least one Bayern fan was transported injured from the stadium.
If you smoke, you have a higher risk of losing teeth
Those who smoke not only harm the lungs, but also the teeth - this is suggested by a long-term Potsdam study.
The good news is that if given up, the risk decreases quickly.
Smokers have a significantly increased risk of losing their teeth early.
This is evident from the long-term study of an international team of researchers, including several employees of the German Institute of Nutritional Research (DIfE) in Potsdam.
Compared to non-smokers, the risk of losing their teeth prematurely was 2.5- to 3.6 times higher.
Scientists had observed more than 23,300 participants.
The main causes of tooth loss are tooth decay and inflammation of the gums, emphasizes the DIfE.
For these periodontitis, smoking is a risk factor, the authors emphasize.
The connection between smoking and tooth loss is therefore due to more frequent gum inflammation in smokers.
It is not clear to what extent smoking also increases the risk of tooth decay.
However, people who quit smoking could reduce their risk within a short period of time, the researchers write in the "Journal of Dental Research".
However, it could take more than ten years before the danger drops back to the level of non-smokers, explained first author Thomas Dietrich from the English University of Birmingham.
You have to convince people to become non-smokers, explained study leader Heiner Boeing of the DIfE: "Smoking shortens the lifetime."
Not to be smoked is good for lungs and vessels and according to our findings also leads to good dental health into old age.
In Germany, over 20 percent of the population between the ages of 65 and 74 is affected by toothlessness, the DIfE said.
Earlier studies had already pointed out that smoking increased the risk of early tooth failure.
The new long-term study underscores the findings.
Archaeologists come across leftovers from late antiquity on the banks of the Rhine in Cologne
Once again, archaeological finds have been made on the right bank of the Rhine in Cologne.
Near the construction site for the new Rhine Boulevard, archaeologists from the Roman-Germanic Museum have excavated skeletons and remnants of historical buildings, museum director Marcus Trier said Tuesday.
Since the beginning of the construction work on the Rhine, historians have been looking for so-called ground monuments.
For all construction projects in the district of Cologne, the museum is always consulted as a specialist office for archaeological soil monument preservation.
In the third construction phase of the Rheinboulevard, the archaeologists now found what they were looking for.
It had already been known from text documents that there had been a fortress at the site in the 4th century and a church in the 8th century, Trier explained.
In addition to remains of the fortress and the church tower, a cemetery with about 350 graves was discovered, which probably belonged to the church.
Young woman captured by freight train
After a young woman at the train station in Geilenkirchen-Lindern, the federal police asks the population for help.
Early Wednesday morning around 1.40 a.m., the woman crossed the tracks coming from the direction of the station building at the end of the platform and was captured by a freight train, which came from Mönchengladbach.
By recording the train, according to the federal police, she has sustained serious injuries from it.
She was treated at the scene and taken to a hospital by rescue workers.
There are no findings about the reason for crossing the nocturnal track of the young woman.
The incident may have been witnessed by witnesses or local residents have heard acoustic perceptions.
Residents or passers-by from Linderner Bahn, Ziegelbäckerweg, Thomashofstraße and Leiffarther Straße (L364) are eligible.
Information, which provides information about the cause of the accident, can be given under round-the-clock and free hotline of the Federal Police on telephone 0800/6888000 or any other police department.
E-Commission proposes new court in dispute over TTIP
In order to defuse the dispute over the planned transatlantic free trade agreement TTIP, the EU Commission has proposed an extensive reform of the current arbitration system.
The controversial private conciliation facilities for disputes between corporations and states are therefore to be replaced by a more transparent system, which corresponds to significantly more traditional courts in its functioning.
"We want to set up a system that citizens trust," European Commissioner Cecilia Malmström said.
Swede intends to introduce the proposal as a European negotiating position in the free trade talks with the USA.
The German government announced support.
TTIP aims to create the world’s largest free trade zone with 800 million people.
The plans to reform the current arbitration proceedings provide concretely that the EU states and the U.S. jointly select independent judges for a new investment tribunal.
They are said to come in equal parts from the EU, the US and third countries.
For the first time, a second instance is also planned.
It would allow it to object to verdicts.
So far, the parties to the proceedings have agreed among themselves on arbitrators, there has been no possibility of opposition to their judgments.
In the discussion of the past few months, it became clear that citizens did not trust the old ISDS system in terms of fairness and justice, commented Malmström.
In the long term, she wants to work towards an international investment tribunal.
There, disputes between Chinese companies and EU states, for example, could also be resolved.
The investor-state arbitration (ISDS), which is mainly demanded by large corporations, are considered one of the main reasons for the great resistance in Europe against TTIP.
The traditional arbitratorial courts are criticized by opponents as a kind of parallel justice, through which corporations can claim damages at the expense of taxpayers, derail national laws or enforce a reduction in consumer and environmental standards.
In initial opinions, anti-TTIP organisations such as Campact described the EU Commission's proposals as inadequate.
Criticulated above all that they should not apply to the already negotiated free trade agreement with Canada (Ceta).
The German Secretary of State for Economic Affairs Matthias Machnig (SPD), on the other hand, described the plans as great progress.
The Commission takes into account key points on the reform that German Economy Minister Sigmar Gabriel drafted in the spring with other EU trade ministers.
The old system of private arbitral tribunals is off the table.
The French government also welcomed Malmström's plans.
The European business federation BusinessEurope warned against restricting the economy's lawsuits through too many new rules.
Especially small and medium-sized companies could be disadvantaged by this, it said.
When talks on the Transatlantic Trade and Investment Partnership (TTIP (Transatlantic Trade and Investment Partnership) agreement can be concluded remained unclear on Wednesday.
Negotiations have been ongoing since mid-2013.
However, the topic of investor protection has not yet been discussed with the United States.
Originally, a framework for the agreement was supposed to stand as early as the end of this year
This date is no longer tenable.
Delicator Folk by Sufjan Stevens in Hamburg
Sufjan Stevens sits on stage at Cologne's Prime Club eleven years ago (today: Luxor).
Next to him is a flipchart on which the shy-looking folk singer has recorded the picturesque US state of Michigan with felt-tip pen.
The entire audience, about 40 people, is almost gripped by Steven's talk.
He talks about various places that he draws again and again on the map, about which his meticulously and delicately arranged songs report.
Where they originated and what it looks like there, in his home
Since then, Stevens has turned out to be a marvelously creative lunatic.
Released in 2003 with the third album "Greetings from Michigan: The Great Lake State", cleverly composed songs appear, which snuggle harmoniously close to American folk of the late sixties, but can drift into great opulente at any time.
The rumour arises that "Michigan" would be the first part of a daring series: Each of the 50 US states should be sung in succession.
But as early as 2004 and near the Rhine, "Seven Swans" has already appeared and breaks positive expectations.
Reduced, Stevens performs here.
Live guitar and banjo suffice.
A year later, the musician, who lives in New York, returns to the series.
"Sufjan Stevens Invites You To: Come on Feel The Illinoise") is a widely acclaimed masterpiece released in 2005.
Exuberant of ideas and stylistic finesse.
The album enters the American Billboard charts and is not missing from any annual leaderboard of the influential music media.
After extremely prolific experiments, Stevens then uses "The Age of Adz" (2010) very extensively electronic offset pieces.
This sixth decent studio album is still broadly orchestrated, but worn by programmed sequences or beats and plays with all sorts of effect and reverberation.
Long since the smaller clubs, the stage shows here become a colorful spectacle of neon forehead bands and large fluorescent stage decorations.
Sufjan Stevens is currently touring Germany as part of a five-piece band.
In the dark hamburger More!
Theater mainly presents the "Carrie & Lowell", which has been available since March.
Although almost 2000 guests now sit in their chairs here, the much more mature Stevens returns to intimacy and folk.
Without addressing a word to the audience, the group of excellent musicians plays through the widely enriched album.
From the audience you can hear: The lecture is "opulent" and you can "basically throw away the plate".
It's only for an encore that Stevens expresses modest gratitude, acoustically plays few older songs and finally bows to thunderous applause.
Jeremy Corbyn's debut at prime minister's question time
Since his election, Mr Corbyn's debut has been eagerly awaited at the prime minister's Question Time
The new Labour chairman Jeremy Corbyn is to make his debut at the prime minister's Question Time and line up with David Cameron for the first time.
Mr Corbyn will rise up to ask the first of his six predetermined questions shortly after noon, with his feat likely to be scrutinised by the media and the Labour MP up close.
He called for "fewer theatres and more facts" at this weekly event.
He has also said he could skip some meetings to leave them to colleagues.
The encounter will be the first parliamentary examination for Mr Corbyn's leadership because it comes after his appointment as a member of the shadow cabinet and after his speech at the TUC annual meeting on Tuesday.
In the meantime, the Labour leader's decision to persevere in silence on Tuesday while singing the national anthem at a commemoration on the 75th anniversary of the Battle of Britain has provoked criticism from a number of Tory MPs and is at the heart of several front-page stories in the newspapers.
Mr Corbyn's decision not to sing the national anthem has attracted attention
A spokesman for Mr Corbyn said he had been "in respectful silence" and had acknowledged the "heroism of the Royal Air Force in the aerial battle for England."
But a member of Mr Corbyn's shadow cabinet, Owen Smith, recounted on the BBC Two Newsnight's programme that he had advised the Labour chairman to sing the national anthem, "independent" of his belief that the monarchy should be abolished.
Nearly a dozen shadow ministers have refused to serve in Mr Corbyn's top team, invoking differences in the economy, defence and foreign policy, while less than one-sixth of the parliamentary party originally backed him as a leader.
BBC political correspondent Robin Brant says that political differences are also "stackingling" within Labour, according to Mr Corbyn's remarks on the position on the European Union and the limit on profits.
Mr Corbyn told the TUC conferencing that Labour is putting forward changes to remove the whole idea of a limitation overall.
Hours later, Mr Smith, the shadow-labour and pension minister, said the party was "very clear" that it was only against the government plans to lower the limit from �£26,000 to £23,000.
Mr Corbyn will be the fifth Labour chairman to face David Cameron ever since he has been Tory's chief executive across the dispatch box in parliament in the last decade.
The Labour chairman, who has promised a different policy approach, says he has "crowd-sourced" ideas for questions to Mr Cameron and given him more than 30,000 suggestions.
The Islington North MP has said the prime minister's question time is too confrontational, and that he will refrain both "fatality" and arguing with barbs and instead set out to focus on serious issues such as poverty, inequality and the challenges for young people.
Mr Corbyn has said Angela Eagle, the shadow economy minister will represent him if he doesn't participate - for example, when Mr Cameron is travelling abroad.
He has also brought up the idea that other colleagues occasionally take the floor, and that he addressed John Bercow, the speaker of the lower house, to discuss the issue.
When he became a leader in 2005, Mr Cameron said he wanted to move away from politics in the style of a "Kasperle theatre," which is often linked to the prime minister's Question Time. But a few years later, he conceded that he had failed in it.
Since it was first broadcast on television in 19990, Question Time with the prime minister was viewed as the most important barometer of a leader's verdict and his leadership in the House of Commons and his position among his parliamentary colleagues, although critics have argued that it has become a caricature and needs far-reaching reforms.
'Floors in Joburg': Homeless youngars trained as photographers
Johannesburg city centre is a hard-hitting place when you're homeless.
But a group of former street children have found a way to learn a skill and earn a living.
"I was shot in Joburg" is a non-proficient studio that teaches homeless young people how they take photos of their surroundings and make a profit from it.
BBC News has met one of the first graduates of the project.
How to share your stories, pictures and videos with BBC News
Every day BBC news - on TV, on the radio and online - brings you the latest stories from all over the world..., but what we're trying to hear are the topics that matter to you.
The role that you play in creating the messages is very important.
Whether it’s news or a topic you’re presenting, your post can make a difference.
Have you seen an event or have you been affected?
Is something significant, bizarre or unusual happening in your environment?
Do you have a story to tell, or is there anything you should pursue in your view?
Are there topics that you would like to initiate conversations about?
What would you like to comment on?
Or do you want to find out what other people are talking about?
Terms and conditions for sending contributions to the BBC.
Here are the different ways to send your posts:
Contact us on Twitter
We can take advantage of your tweets by using your Twitter username on BBC productions.
Send us a message on WhatsApp
Send us a picture, video or a message to our WhatsApp number + 44-7525-900971
In some cases, your text, as well as your pictures, can be used for BBC production.
We’ll publish your name as you provide it (if you don’t ask us to refrain), but we’ll never publish your phone number.
You send us your pictures and videos or an SMS to 61124.
Or if you are located outsiding the UK, to international number +44 7624 800 100.
You can download the BBC News app on iOS or Android devices and send us pictures or posts with the help of the buttons in the app.
Send us your stories and comments by e-mail to haveyoursay@bbc.co.uk
We strive to read all of your emails, but due to the quantities we receive every day, it’s not always possible to answer everyone individually.
Send us your pictures, video or audio recordings by e-mail to yourpics@bbc.co.uk
If we use your material in BBC programs or online, we will publish your name as you indicate it (if you don’t ask us to refrain), but we’ll never post your email address.
You can also upload your video or pictures.
We’ll publish your name as you provide it if you don’t ask us to refrain from doing so.
Pound rises due to accelerated wage growth in UK
The pound skyrocketed according to data showing an increase in UK wage growth. In parallel, the FTSE 100 was boosted by SABMiller's bidding target.
Between May and July, earnings excluding bonuses in the UK grew 2.9% from the same period last year.
This percentage has been the fastest since 2009 and yielded estimates about the timing of an increase in interest rates.
The pound rose more than 1% against the dollar to $1.5505 and had climbed to €1.3730 by 0.84% against the euro.
The FTSE 100 index closed with an increase of 1.49% to 6.229.21.
SABMiller was the outstanding stock in the market and made a 20% jump aftend takeover offer from rival AB InBev.
A transaction would combine the world's two largest breweries, bringing together brands such as Budweiser, Stella Artois, Peroni and Grolsch.
Burberry shares were 0.9% higher after encouraging news from the competing group for luxury goods Richemont.
Richemont's latest sales figures beat all expectations and also showed that sales in China - a key market for Burberry - is reporting growth again.
Shares of Glencore rose 5% after the mining giant said it had taken $2.5bn through a share sale as part of its strategy of debt cutting.
Shares of the packaging and paper group Mondi were the biggest losers on the index, down 4.3% after Goldman Sach's estimate for "selling."
JD Sports rose 3.1% after the sportswear company reported pre-tax earnings of 46.6 million for the half-year.
JD Sport's boss says higher wages jeopardize expansion.
The managing director of JD Sports Peter Cowgill says a higher minimum wage for UK workers could mean "more purchasing power in the pockets of potential customers."
But it is unlikely that this purchasing power will offset the higher labor costs at his company, he says.
Costhes could hit JD Sports's expansion plans, he added, and that could mean there are less additional jobs.
