Pegida poster puts Lebanese on potato döner idea
Potatoes or kebabs?
Both can be done in the potato kebab developed by the Oldenburg takeaway owner Hani Alhay.
Inspired by the saying "potato instead of doner" on a poster of the Pegida movement, Alhay put potato and meat together on a skewer.
He came up with the idea for a poster at a Pegida demonstration with the inscription "Potatoes instead of Döner", which he had accidentally seen on television.
Since then, a barbecue skewer has been spinning in his shop, equipped with meat and potatoes.
"That's multiculture for the stomach," says Alhay.
In the meantime, Lebanese has registered the idea at the patent office in Munich.
According to the authority, it has recently been protected as a utility model.
Alhay therefore does not want to reveal how much meat and how much potatoes the skewer contains.
Just so much: "It tastes even better together," he finds.
The culinary international understanding is also well received by its customers.
Metcash launches down-to-earthness campaign to combat Aldi incursion
Metcash is trying to protect the IGA stores from an Aldi march in South Australia and Western Australia.
Grocery wholesaler Metcash has launched a down-to-earthrough campaign to defend IGA retailers' market share in South Australia and Western Australia in the run-up to a $700 million Aldi invasion.
As Aldi prepares the opening of two distribution centers and the first 120 stores in South Australia and Western Australia early next year, Metcash and IGA supermarkets strengthen their relationships with local food suppliers, building the IGA brand through mainstream marketing and marketing via social media, as well as through events such as Adelaide show.
IGA distributors and suppliers are jumping on board, with brands such as ice maker Simmo from Dunsbourg distributing rehearsals in IGA stores and shopkeepers selling discounted tickets for the South Australian Football League to loyal customers.
Metcash is expected to release more details of its defense strategy on September 29, when the wholesaler of food, alcohol and equipment runs its annual investor strategy day.
There's a lot at stake, as South Australia and Western Australia account for nearly 30 per cent of Metcash's IGA stores and generating higher profit margins than Metcash's IGA network in the eastern states.
Buyers in Souch Australia and Western Australia are notoriously provincial and IGA retailers have a much stronger market share than in New Souch Wales, Victoria and Queensland - more than 30 per cent in South Australia and 24 per cent in Western Australia compared to around 14 per centre in the eastern states.
However, analysts such as Morgan Stanley believe Aldi will snatch away about five percent of the food market in these two states twelve months after opening of its first markets, taking away sales from all existing players, but mostly self-employed retailers.
According to a report released this week, analyst Tom Kierath of Morgan Stanley estimated that IGA retailers could lose $360 million in revenue a year and Metcash $250 million by the end of 2016.
This forecast is based on the assumption that Aldi will open 25 stores in SA and 30 in WA next year and generate $500 million and $600 million revenue in each market.
Loss in the food and grocery business, and in terms of Metcash's high fixed-cost basis, could cost Metcash around $13 million profit before interest and taxes, Mr Kierath said.
At the same time, Metcash is losing market share in the eastern states, mainly to Aldi and Coles, although a recent survey by Roy Morgan found that IGA buyers are the most loyal.
Morgan Stanley assumes that metcash's food and grocery sales will fall by 3.8 per cent in 2016, down 4.1 per cent in 2017 and 2.1 per cent in 2018, reducing market share from 14 per cent in 2015 to 10.3 per cent by 2020.
Last week, leading fund leader Hamish Douglass, the founder of Magellan Financial Group, recounted at a business lunch in Melbourne that Metcash is likely to disappear within a decade.
Metcash declined to publicly respond to Mr Douglass' comments.
But it is expected that a Metcash board member made contact with Mr Douglass last week.
IGA's market share has fallen from 18 per cent to 14 per cent since 2010, and income from Metcash's food and food trade, which supplies about 1,200 IGA retailers, has fallen 43 per cent since 2012, reflecting operating debt reduction with simultaneous pressure on sales and margins.
However, the wholesaler is fighting back and investing more than $150 million to align sales prices with Coles and Woolworths on hundreds of products every week and improve IGA retailers in improving their stores through faster-growing fresh foods and delivering a better range of private-label food.
Metcash's chief executive Ian Morrice says the price adjustments and store refurbishment programs will increase to Elan and hopes the wholesaler will return to profit growth for the first time in four years in 2017.
Thanasi Kokkinakis is backed by the president of Australia's tennis federation, Steve Healy
Thanasi Kokkinakis deserves praise rather than criticism for his behaviour.
Thanasi Kokkinakis was one of the "collateral damages" in the recent storm surrounding his friend Nick Kyrgios and, according to the president of the Australian tennis federation Steve Healy, deserves praise rather than criticism for his own behaviour.
Kokkinakis, the younger one of the talented duo repeatedly referred to as "Special Ks," came into the spotlight through Kyrgios' infamous altercation with Stan Wawrinka at the Montreal Masters.
In a fiery qualifier in Cincinnati soon after, Kokkinakis and his opponent Ryan Harrison had to be separated twice by the referee, with Harrison exclaiming: "Wawrinka should have beaten Kyrgios, and I should beat the child."
While he was last seen smashing a tennis racket after a spasm thwarted a potential U.S. U.S. Open victory by Richard Gasquet, Kokkinakis was steadily defended by Healy.
The teenager is currently in Glasgow, competing with Sam Groth for second place in the individual behind Bernard Tomic in the Davis Cup semi-finals against Great Britain.
"One thing I'm quite jealous about is the fact that, in my view, Thanasi was drawn a little into the dispute," Healy said.
He shattered when he lost because of a spasm in the U.S. Open, but he's not the first player to break a racquet out of frustration with himself, and I know Thanasi well enough to know he's not proud.
But, really, he behaved extremely well in the U.S. Open, even under the pretty provocative circumstances with Harrison and so on, and I think he deserves a lot of recognition.
He's got a great team around himself, he's got a great family around himself, and I think he's been treated a little too hard.
He's a very mature 19-year-old, he works very hard, and he fits into the team and so on, he's never had a problem with the other players, he's respected in the locker room, he's training hard, he's in good shape, he's a very stable and mature young guy, and I believe he deserves to be judged by his own merits.
Healy also supported the decision not to consider Kyrgios in the important preliminary decision, which begins on Friday, and he describes the "common decision" as the right one for everyone, but he assures the man from Canberra to return to the team as a long-term permanent component."
"At first, we wish that the team is completely focused on the competition and not on all the controversies around [Kyrgios]," Healy said.
And I believe that Nick managed the rise very quickly, what people like Federer and Murray pointed out. He rises in the eyes of the public, and he has made some bad mistakes and deserves all the criticism he gets, but we're trying to support him, we're trying to make him understand the consequences of his actions. Some time out of the spotlight where he can only think about it, I think it's right, and it's best
Europe's refugee crisis: Hungary declares state of emergency, closes the border, withholds refugees
Hungary declares a state of emergency and sealed off the southern border with Serbia on Tuesday and detained all those who tried to enter the country illegally. All with the aim of stopping the influx of migrants.
Chasis followed at the border when hundreds of migrants accumulated in a no-man's land and Serbian officials reacted with outrage.
For an unknown time, the people who had fled violence were stuck in a strip of land on the road between the two checkpoints of the countries and built tents and settled there.
Frustration increased.
When a police helicopter hovered over them, the migrants chanted "Opens the border!" and shouted insults against Hungarian riot police.
Some rejected food and water in protest.
Serbia's foreign minister declared it was "unacceptable" that migrants would be sent back from Hungary as more and more arrive from Macedonia and Greece.
(Serbia) wants to be part of the solution rather than collateral damage.
Talks with Brussels and other countries will have to take place in the coming days," Ivica Dacic said in Prague.
The turbulence on the Hungarian-Serbian border failed to reconcile a common immigration policy a day after the 28-nation bloc at a contentious meeting in Brussels.
However, ministers agreed to share responsibility for 40,000 people seeking refuge in surprised Italy and Greece. They expressed their hopes that - next month or by the end of the year - an agreement would be reached that EU states would take in an additional 120,000 refugees, including some from Hungary.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Austria called for a special summit of the European Union on Tuesday to discuss the continent's immigration crisis.
Hungary, however, did not pin its hopes on an early action by Brussels or its neighbours.
The state of emergency declared on Tuesday in two southern regions gave authorities more powers to deal with the crisis, allowing them to block off roads and speed up asylum procedures.
Hungarian Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto, meanwhile, said that his country was also planning the construction of a barbed wire fence along its border with Romania to stop the influx of migrants through the country - after ending a fence on the Serbian border.
CBS news correspondent Charlie D'Agata reported that even prison inmates were deployed during the race to create the barbed-wire fence between Hungary and Serbia.
In recent months, Hungary has become a significant bottleneck for many war-refugees from Syria and elsewhere in the Middle East and entry point into the European Union.
However, Prime Minister Viktor Orban has insisted that most incoming arrivals are economic refugees looking for a better life, rather than war refugees who are entitled to protection - a view strongly at odds with other EU states, including Germany.
The new laws, which came into force at midnight in Hungary, make crossing the border or damaging the 4-metre (13-foot)-high fence built by the government along the southern border with Serbia into a crime. These laws also establish higher prison sentences for convicted traffickers.
"Due to the situation created by mass migration, the Hungarian government declares a state of emergency," government spokesman Zoltan Kovac's reporters in the southern city of Szeged said.
We say it very clearly: Illegal border crossing is a crime.
Parliament has yet to agree procedurally to the deployment of the military, which is expected for next week, but Associated Press reporters have already seen heavy-armed soldiers with vehicles and dogs on the border for days.
Gyorgy Bakondi, country's security adviser to Orban, said that authorities caught 45 people trying to cross the border, 15 others had already penetrated deeper into the country.
They come over by damaging the fence, they are now in police custody and are accused of committing crimes under the new laws.
Authorities are in the process of repairing the fence quickly.
Hungarian officials also closed two of the seven border crossings with Serbia on Tuesday morning after a trailed carriage with barbed wire to close the borders arrived.
Chaos surrounded the most important border crossing near Roszke, Hungary, when the Hungarians opened a small office in a metal container to edit the data of people and squeezed a crowd to push themselves in there.
About 20 managed to get in, but thousands stayed outside.
Another group of migrants blocked Serbia's and Hungary's main highway connection, saying they would deny food and water until they were allowed to enter Hungary.
Election-2016: Hillary Clinton's lead over Bernie Sanders halved in national poll
Former U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton continues to hold a double-digit lead in the Democratic race for national-level nomination, but Senator Bernie Sanders is gaining ground over her.
Clinton now has 47-percent support of Democratic voters in the primaries (58 percent previously), while Sanders ranks second, with 27 percent (up 17 percent).
Vice President-elector Joe Biden, who hasn't yet announced if he's running for the presidency, is receiving support from 15 per cent of Democratic voters in the primaries.
More than five months before the primaries began, most Democratic voters say it's too early to say that their opinion is determined which candidate they're going to support.
But Clinton's supporters are more consistent in her election than those of other candidates.
Since the CBS news poll, Clinton has lost at a number of demographic groups, while Sanders made some gains.
Clinton holds a big lead in women and moderates, but those protrusions have diminished.
Their support among the men has dropped significantly, and Sanders is only 5 points behind here.
Clinton's advantage among Democratic voters under the age of 50 has evaporated, and she and Sanders are now at the same height.
Clinton, however, still has a big lead among senior voters.
Last month Clinton was ahead of Sanders with a double-digit lead among the Liberals, but Sanders now has a 5-point lead in this group.
About half of Democratic voters in the primaries nationwide say they're enthusiastically supporting Clinton if nominated by the party.
A quarter would support her with some caveats and another 15 per cent would only support her because she is the candidate.
Six percent won't support them.
Perhaps unsurprisingly that Clinton's current supporters are especially enthusiastic about her.
Views are more mixed among those who currently don't support Clinton - only about a quarter would enthusiastically support them.
While 48 per cent of Democratic voters in the primaries say they support Clinton with enthusiasm, fewer Republican voters say this about their front-runner Donald Trump (35 percent).
If Clinton's supporters are asked on an open-free question about why they want her to be the nominee, the most common answer is that she has the right experience (16 percent), followed by the view that it's time it's time that a woman becomes president (13 percent) and that she's the best contestant for the job (10 percent).
By Clinton's supporters, her connection with Bill Clinton (9 percent) is also mentioned.
Approval of dealing with the problems and electability are also cited.
With the vice-presidentiance throwing a potential for presidential office into the balance, 57 percent of Democratic voters of the primaries would like to see him in the race - though a third believe he shouldn't.
If Democratic voters of the primaries are asked who would be their second choice for the party, Biden is at the forefront.
Of Clinton's supporters, more than half say Biden would be her second choice far ahead of Sanders.
If Biden decides not to run for Democrats' nomination, Hillary Clinton's lead over Sanders increases, from 20 points with Biden in the race to 30 points without him in the race.
Fifty-five percent of Democratic voters see Clinton as a nominee with the best chances of winning the election; however, that figure has dropped from 78 percent last month.
Biden is by far a second in that measurement, but the percentage that sees it as the "most electable" has risen.
Nevertheless, when asked what is more important for a candidate, Democratic voters cite questions about electability.
Similar to last month, not a single Democratic nominee, or potential nominee for these voters of the primaries, stands out as unacceptable.
The Democratic candidates, or potential candidates, produce good results on some key features among voters of their party's primaries.
Majorities regard Clinton, Sanders and Biden as honest and trustworthy and endowed with strong leadership qualities.
Biden scores best on honesty among the three nominees, while Clinton's strength is the lead.
Thirty percent of Democratic voters don't believe Clinton is honest and trustworthy, twice as many say that about Sanders (15 percent), but 27 percent don't have an opinion of him.
Democratic voters of the primaries generally also see Clinton, Sanders and Biden ideologically in accordance with their views.
With all these measurements, about a quarter of Democratic voters don't have an opinion of Sanders.
Since questions about Hillary Clinton's use of a personal email address and a server will continue to be asked as secretary of state during tenue, most Democratic voters of the primaries are satisfied with their explanation of the matter, saying it has not affected their opinions overall.
But it's a different story in the American public overall.
Half the Americans are now unhappy with their explanation, while only about a third are satisfied.
Publican opinion on this was divided in March, shortly after the problem came to light.
Most Americans said the email controversy had no impact on her general opinions from Clinton, though 36 percent said her opinion of her had still deteriorated.
It's mostly Republicans, more than six out of 10 who say their views have gotten worse from Clinton.
Although Clinton is seen as honest and trustworthy by her party's voters, this continues to be a problem for her among registered voters overall: only 32 percent see her as honest and trustworthy, eight percent less than last month and 15 points less since May.
62 percent of eligible voters don't think they're an honest and trustworthy person.
At the same time, 55 percent of eligible voters nationwide also see Trump as unhonest and untrustworthy.
However, Clinton and Trump receive good marks on leadership.
Of the six candidates tested, Biden, Carson and Bush fare best on honesty.
A majority sees Sanders rather than honest, but, as with Carson, many don't have an opinion of him.
The error rate for the sample of 351 Democratic voters in the primaries is 6 percentage points.
Ben Carson pulls up close behind Donald Trump at nationwide poll
Ben Carson is close behind Donald Trump in the latest CBS News/New York Times poll
The two candidates, meanwhile, account for half of voters' support in the new poll, and others are dramatically losing grip
Republican voters nationwide continue to support Trump in large numbers. Support grows 23% in the latest CBS poll conducted ahead of Fox News debate last month, by 27% in Tuesday's poll.
Carson shot from 6% to 23% over the same period.
The poll also found Carson is well received by demographic groups, outperforming Trump among Republican voters with higher education.
The rest of the field, with the exception of Carly Fiorina and Ohio Governor John Kasich, is knocked off over with Wisconsin Gov
Former Florida Governor Jeb Bush, Former Arkansas Governor Mike Huckabee and Florida Senator Marco Rubio are in third place with 6%.
The margin of error in the sample of 376 people from Republican voters in this latest poll is plus or minus 6% and was carried out in September.
The latest CNN/ORC poll, released last week, shows Carson rising in the polls - landing at 19% support among Republicans, behind Trump with 32% support.
ABC News/Washington Post's most recent poll also showed Trump well ahead of Carson.
Looking ahead to a last-week ascendant Carson, Trump found some side blows to the former neurosurgeon, even after Carson apologised for questioning Trump's belief.
"We need energy," Trump said last Saturday during a campaign appearance in Iowa.
Pleasant bathing temperatures: The eastern Mediterranean is currently up to 29 degrees warm.
Even if top values are only measured in the distance, a trip to the Mediterranean is enough for beach holidaymakers to find warm water.
A bath in the North Sea or Baltic Sea should be a rather short pleasure at a maximum of 17 degrees.
Some people no longer go into the sea: A maximum of 18 degrees has the water in the North and Baltic Seas this week, the German Weather Service (DWD) announces.
Bathing temperatures, on the other hand, continue to offer the Mediterranean: in the east it even comes up to 29 degrees.
In the Adriatic it is 25 degrees in the top, in France 23 degrees.
The Red Sea in Egypt comes to 28 degrees.
Five members of the Brotherhood burdened with the death of a Baruch college freshman
Nearly two years after a student died during a harassing ritual of a student ligament in Pocono Mountains, Pennsylvania, murder charges are filed against five people.
Members of New York's Baruch College Pi-Delta-Psi Brotherhood brutally attacked Chun "Michael" Deng during an introductory ritual in September 2013 and delayed his transport to the hospital, which contributed to his death, police said.
Officials said he had been blindfolded with a 30-pount backpack full of sand, and was repeatedly attacked as he tried to walk across a frozen field towards a target.
Mr. Deng eventually complained of headaches, then fell unconscious.
Members of the Pi-Delta-Psi Brotherhood did not call 911 after Deng fainted and allegedly waited an hour before taking him to a hospital, according to police.
However, they have had the time to change Deng's clothes, google his symptoms, and Andy Meng, to contact Brotherhood's national president to seek advice, police chief Chris Wagner said at a news conference.
"At this point, the members began to hide utensils and fundamentallyly placed the welfare of the brotherhood above that of Michael Deng," police chief Wagner said.
He couldn't be revived, and he died of his injuries the next morning.
After an autopsy report, Deng was repeatedly inflicted with blunt force injuries to the head, trunk and thigh.
"Too many families were destroyed by the harassments of the Brotherhood. At least one student has died of harassment every year since 1970," Douglas Fierberg, a lawyer representing Deng's family, said in a statement.
Brotherhoods and their members must be held accountable, and this step is very important.
A county grand jury of Monroe recommended a third-degree murder indictment for the first five members.
Thirty-two members of the Brotherhood, including Mr Meng, are also charged with harassing attacks and forming a criminal association.
"We don't just want to blame the people involved, but if there is an organization that - officially or unofficially - sanctioned this, so we're going to hold those accountable as well," Wagner said at a news conference on Tuesday.
Baruch College has banned the Brotherhood, and the National Brotherhood has revoked its affiliation with the local group.
That will happen when the Fed raises interest rates
An interest rate hike is coming, and the home is swerving, yields on bonds will rise, and the economy will slide into a recession.
That's what we know.
What we don't know is how long all this will last and how long it will last.
In particular, the history offers little guidance for the economy.
A recession came within just 11 months of the first rate hike and lasted 86 months.
The aggressivity of the Federal Reserve in raising interest rates is often, if not always, a factor in how the economy and financial assets react.
That's whhere U.S. central bank representatives have emphasized so fiercely that investors shouldn't focus on when it's starting to raise interest rates, but rather on the period needed for normalization.
In fact, several variables play a role.
In the end, however, market participants might find that all the excitement may have been exaggerated because of a rate hike.
"The Fed's first increase since the global financial crisis will inevitably be interpreted by some as a sign for the end of the era of "cheap money,"" Julian Jessop, chief economist at Capital Economics, said in a statement to clients.
In contrast, we do not expect the gradual return of U.S
But that doesn’t mean there won’t be an impact.
Here’s a look based on historical trends at how some areas of the economy might react:
As the market saw over the past month, anticipating interest rate hikes can make things volatile for a while.
As soon as the increase arrives, the influence isn't as dramatic.
"There's apparently a tendency that asset earnings are choked off 12 to 24 months after the initial increase, which perhaps again reflects the delay in monetary policy," Deutsche Bank analysts said in a more recent study on what's happening after interest rate hikes by the Fed.
More specifically, in the past 35 years, the market is mostly on an upward trend - about 14 per cent - on its way to raising interest rates, quite flat in the 250 days thereafter (average profit of 2.6 per cent), then back to normal after 500 days, with an average return in the past six past cycles of 14.4 per cent, according to a recent analyss published by Bob Doll at Nuveen Asset Management
Deutsche Bank said that the impact on equities tends to occur later in the increase cycle and the return begins to decrease.
Recessions are a fact of economic life, but interest rate hikes often encourage them.
In the current case, the Fed is facing some conditions that did not exist before and that could accelerate a recession.
It is extremely noteworthy that the gross domestic product will be close to its lowest point in a Fed interest rate hike.
According to Deutsche Bank's 118 interest rate hikes since 1950, nominal GDP was below 4.5 percent.
Although the second quarter of 2015 stood at 3.6 percent, few expect this to last and expect only 1.5 percent for the third quarter, according to the Atlanta Fed.
Interest rate hikes on such a fragile economic backdrop could be risky and raise the question "whether it's different this time," Deutsche Bank said.
Market participants have been gearing up all year for a Fed's rate hike, and all eyes have been on the Federal Open Market Committee meeting this week, which ends on Thursday.
While many strategists and economists believe the FOMC could authorize an increase in this session, futures trading only indicates a 25 percent probability.
"In our study, all increase cycles from 1950 to the present took place in a super-cycle with increasing momentum in GDP, dwarfing pre-recession heights very quickly after the start of the recovery," the report said.
In contrast, this is a clearly slow recovery from what was the worst recession in the observed phase.
The current cycle is by far the longest the Fed has waited since the end of last recession; the record has been 35 months, and that's 74 months so far.
Fixed income was also volatile as the market anticipates an interest rate hike, and the pattern is quite similar to that on the capital market.
The main difference is that the effect on bonds is faster than for equities, if they change their strategic course.
For bonds, yields appear to change direction as soon as the first increase/reduction occurs in the cycle.
At the end of the increase cycle, bond yields fall immediately, Deutsche Bank said.
Charles Schwab's strategists believe the increase in interest rates will result in yields moving closer together between longer and shorter bonds and leading to a flattening of the curve.
High-interest bonds often generate better returns in such a climate, though "we're still cautious about expecting higher yields," Kathy Jones, fixed income analyst at Schwab, said in an analysis.
It's interesting, says Jones, that the markets have already adapted to Fed strategy expectations.
"In addition to the strength of the dollar and the decline in inflation expectations, there are some indications that the market has adapted to the prospect of stricter monetary policy," she wrote.
Short-term interest rates have risen, the yield curve is flatter, credit margins have widened and volatility has risen - all features of the market if the Fed tightens its strategy.
By and large, companies handling the greater share of their operations in the U.S
Multinational companies with high debts will be worse off as a rising dollar makes their products more expensive in the global market, as well as financing their debts.
"History shows that "quality" stocks tend to react extremely positively three months after an initial rate hike," Goldman Sachs analysts said in a report for customers.
Companies with a strong balance sheet have surpassed companies with a weak balance sheet by an average of 5 percentage points after each of the interest rate hikes in 1994, 1999 and 2004.
Companies with high capital yields as well as low volatility in their shares also outperformed their low-quality counterparts by an average of 4 (percentaries) and 3 (percentaries).
Debt is going to be a big problem.
Companies with a high share of flexible interest rates will lose the most, Goldman said.
Outside the pure stock games, consumers will also benefit from the rising dollar.
Savers could see profits through higher yields, even if experts disagree about how quickly that will take place.
BBC Radio 3 is a copy of the Classic FM, says House member Andrew Bingham
The Conservative member of parliament criticised the broadcaster because of its "dumbing down"
He argues he is increasingly indistinguishable from commercial rivals.
He also targeted the BBC Music Awards and said they only imitated The Brits
Parliament colleague Damien Collins attacked the show The Voice and said that this was not very original.
BBC Radio 3 "converts" into Classic FM, claims a leading member of parliament announcing new pressure on the broadcaster to prove he is doing something the private broadcasters don't.
Conservative MP Andrew Bingham also told BBC bosses that the public radio station was "dumming down" and increasingly indistinguishable from its commercial competitors.
"Radio 3 seems - I don't like to use the word "dumb" - but it seems to turn into Classic FM," he said.
He was also targeting the BBC Music Awards, which launched last December.
The BBC Music Awards, launched last year, seem to be The Brits just under a different name.
So if you're trying to do something else - these are two spontaneously cited examples where you're actually imitating only what's already on the market," Mr Bingham said.
BBC director-general dismissed the accusations, arguing that BBC Radio 3 was far ahead of its commercial competitors when it comes to live music and public enlightenment.
What I learn from Radio 3, I won’t learn about classical music anywhere else.
And there's a commitment to live music and making music that's really unmatched.
"And the commitment of Radio 3 to find new music and commissioning new works, I also consider really important," he said.
Earlier this year, BBC Radio 3 controller Alan Davey argued that it needed to work harder to engage the audience than it did in the past because the Britons are less aware of classical music.
Radio 3, however, wasn't the only element of the BBC's production that came under fire for kicking on commercial rivals during the hearing in committee for the selection of culture, media and sport.
Tory MP Damien Collins attacked The Voice - the BBC's musical talent show bought by Dutch producer Talpa Medie at an estimated cost of £22 million.
Do you see why this program is often discussed because it isn't original and not particularly distinctive?
Lord Hall insisted that the BBC 1 show - starring Paloma Faith and Boy George as a judge - was produced "in a special BBC way."
He added he wishes the company "found a hit from our in-house stable" the next time it launches an entertainment series.
However, he refused to exclude the ordering of further rows of The Voice in the future, or to spend money from fee payers on other entertainment formats.
Internet revenues rose the slowest in August and Black Friday may be responsible
In the first three months of 2015, there was a seven percent increase in online sales.
Last month, there was the lowest result since records began in 2000
Online sales intensified three years ago by using cell phones for shopping
Experts believe Black Friday in November curbed spending
For over a decade, virtual shopping has boomed, and many of Britain's main shopping streets have turned into ghost towns.
Yet experts say there are now signs that online sales are stagnant after new figures showed just seven per cent in the first three months of 2015.
Total expenditure for August presented the lowest increase in growth since records began in 2000, with only five per cent increase over the previous year.
Experts say there are signs that online sales are stagnating after new numbers indicated just seven per cent growth in the first three months of 2015
"If you look at growth rates for the previous years and go back to 2000, then that's always evolved over the course of time," Andy Mulcahy, publisher of the IMRG sales index, told the Guardian.
He explained that online sales intensified three years ago, when shoppers started making purchases with their phones.
It meaned that more people could shop online and that people could do so in different environments, e.g. at home on the sofa.
But now this is commuting, and we will potentially see growth decrease.
The Black-Friday phenomenon imported from America is cited as a reason for the declining sales.
Experts believe that buyers will withhold their purchases prior to this event, which takes place on the last Friday in November.
Last year, the average number of shoppers gathered around their computers five times to make their bargains, and some merchant websites crashed.
Experts believe Black Friday, on which shoppers battled each other because of bargains, could stop shoppers from making purchases.
A study by the British Retail Consortium found that from £5 issues, just over £1 was spent online.
For some products such as televisions and washing machines, more than a third of purchases are now carried out via web shops.
At the same time, more than a quarter of people buy clothes and footwear online rather than in the fashion chains in the inner
Muslims bury those killed in Mecca in crane accident - Bin Laden's family is sanctioned
The bodies of 29 dead are carried through the streets by Muslims at the first burials in Mecca
Saudi Arabia blames the collapse in part of the huge Saudi bin-shop group
The health ministry also said a total of 394 people were injured after the crane crashed
Hundreds of Muslims gathered as 29 of the victims were carried through the streets to bury those who were killed when a crane collapsed on Mecca's grand mosque.
Mourners, dressed largely in white, filled the track to pay their respects when the first of the dead embarked on their last trip to Al-Moaissem Cemetery.
The funeral procession passed at the Holy Site, where only days earlier a construction crane fell through the ceiling of the mosque and then onto the prayers.
The bodies were cleared for the burial when the Saudi king imposed a sanction over the Bin Laden family's construction company because of the disaster, which also left behind 394 injured.
The bodies of 29 prayers who were killed when a crane crashed through Mecca's Great Central Mosque were carried through the streets.
Among the dead was Qasim Akram from Bolton, Greater Manchester, father of four who was on his first pilgrimage when the crane crashed down.
Mr Akram was with his parents before the beginning of the annual Hajj in the Grand Mosque.
Saudi officials have already firmly pledged that Hajj will move on to Mecca with thousands who will continue to come to the city.
Today King Salman said the bin-shop group shouldn't have left the arm of the crane when it wasn't used.
An Arabic version of the decree released by the state-owned Saudi Press Agency said the bin-shop group was co-responsible for the collapse.
The news agency's English-language service did not report this detail, but said that the company's leading foreign travel was prohibited.
Earlier this week, Salman vowed to reveal what caused the fall of the crane that plunged into an inner courtyard of the Grand Mosque, where hundreds of thousands of Muslims gathered in front of Hajj, which takes place later this month.
The Bin-Laden Group has not issued any statements about the collapse of the crane, and its representatives were unavailable for comment.
The Royal Decree also rebuked the group because of non-current safety measures and because it had not cooperated with meteorologists.
Dozens of cranes surround Mecca's grand mosque as part of the massive construction project led by the bin-laden group.
The Bin Laden family has been affiliated with Saudi Arabia's ruling family for decades and undertakes larger construction projects.
Al Qaeda's late leader, Osama bin Laden, was a renegade son expropriated by the family in the 1990's.
The crash of the crane came amid strong winds and rain, just days before millions arrived in the kingdom for the pilgrimage required at least once in the life of every healthy Muslim.
Officials said 158 people remain in hospital.
Government officials ordered that 1 million rial (£174,000) must be paid to the relatives of those killed and the same sum of permanently injured.
Other injuries will receive half of that amount.
B&Q Boss says the period of cheap Eastern European craftsmen is over
Britons increasingly employ craftsmen, says Veronique Laury
The number of skilled craftsmen from Eastern Europe has seen an increase
As a result, 200 more Screwfix stores targeting craftsmen will be opened
At the same time, Kingfisher will close 60 B&Q outlets nationwide
By Rupert Steiner, City chief correspondent of the Daily Mail
It is alleged that Britons lay down their hammers and brushes in favour of the use of qualified Eastern European craftsmen carrying out the work in their home
If you can skilfully deal with a hammer or brushes for painting walls, you seem to belong to a minority these days.
Because Britons are increasingly employing craftsmen to carry out do-it-yourself jobs in their homes, according to the company's chief executive behind B&Q.
Véronique Laury, new managing director of Kingfisher, who owns the hardware store chain, said that a large increase in the number of skilled artisans from Eastern Europe offering services at lowest prices has now become obsolete.
That's why Kingfisher plans to open 200 more Screwfix shops aimed at craftsmen and close 60 B&Q sales outlets.
Mrs Laury said she wanted to employ more women in B&Q shops because eight out of ten purchasing decisions in the store are made by women.
At the moment, the men predominate among the employees.
She said it was necessary to face the growing demographic trends that feature more female single households making more renovations than in the past.
Mrs Laury said: "It's still working a majority of men in our stores.
We need to shift this more towards a balance.
In principle, half of our customers consist of women with 80 percent of decisions about the purchase of items by women.
Women play a big role in the decision-making
Mrs Laury, who is one of only five women at the head of a UK leader listed in the FTSE100 index, has also restructured their top team to reflect on an equal number of men and women in leadership positions.
Kingfisher is almost the only company that has staffed exactly 50 percent of the team with women, including its board of directors and chief financial officers.
Mrs Laury said: "This has been a deliberate decision - diversity is an important issue here.
It wasn't difficult to find good women for our supervisory board.
Kingfisher reported a decline in half-yelf profit and sales after the group shouldered the cost of closing the stores.
Because of the trend, Kingfisher, which owns Screwfix and B&Q, will open 200 new stores.
Aich/Dob officials on trial for infidelity
At the regional court in Klagenfurt on Wednesday, two officials of the Austrian volleyball runner-up Aich/Dob had to answer for infidelity and levy evasion.
The defendant pleaded not guilty.
One of them had also been charged with abuse of office.
He was acquitted of this allegation in May.
The current trial has been adjourned.
According to Chief Prosecutor Robert Riffel, it had emerged in the preliminary investigation that the defendants used funds from association accounts, about which they were allowed to dispose in their functions, for private purposes in the years 2006 to 2011.
He estimates the damage at around 400,000 euros.
Sports officials are also charged with financial offenses.
Judge Michaela Sanin, the chairwoman of the Schöffensenate, ruled out this fact.
It is to be negotiated at a later date.
In order to discount the infidelity, the defense stipulated that the association had at no time incurred a financial disadvantage.
The sports officials would only have billed expense allowances and the sports club, for which they worked from 1982 to 2011 and in which they had invested a lot of time and money, out of the debt - the liabilities amounted to around 600,000 shillings (around 44,000 euros) in 2001.
"At that time, we were faced with the situation: Do we continue to run the club or do we lock up?" one of the defendants, in the bread profession, recounted large-scale auditors of the tax office.
With his colleague and co-defendant, he then decided to take over this task.
With all the risks.
"We've shotted money ahead, put down our life insurance as collateral at the bank," he said at the interrogation.
It had been agreed with the association: You can manage the money freely, only no debt may be incurred.
Furthermore, in the event of appropriate financial success, the disbursement of expense allowances had been decided retroactively until 1991.
Therefore, some of the expense allowances were not paid out until years later, namely from 2003, when there had been an increase in the account.
Therefore, flat-rate and non-detail debits also seemed to appear, the former chairman explained the processes.
The accounts and savings books managed the two defendants together, the second defendant said.
Why the payouts had not been disclosed to the club in detail, the judge asked.
The association was not interested in detail in the financial confession, important was only the freedom of debt, according to the answer.
When investigations for abuse of office began against the one functionary in 2011, an amount of 120,000 euros was transferred to the association's account.
These were reserves, the defendants said.
This securities account ran into the name of the second defendant and, according to his testimony, was not known to the association.
"Why?" the judge wanted to know.
For a long time, the respondent did not know an answer to this question.
Finally, he said that he believed that this was the best thing for the club.
If something had happened to him, no one would have known anything about this money, the prosecutor put in the room.
The co-defendant was informed, the official justified himself.
This referral has been phrased as "damage compensation."
What's damages?, judge and prosecutor wanted to know.
Then the first defendant stated that he disagreed with this wording, because the association had never suffered any damage.
But what am I supposed to do when the lawyer says, “You’re all off the hook.”
"Am I a legal representative?" he asked.
Furthermore, the functionary said that he had never received a penny of salaries, he only charged mileage money and diets.
On diets, he had charged the association 1,000 euros per month, but the actual effort was much higher, he added.
The defendants could not provide exact documents for the early years.
These were no longer kept after an audit, in which everything had been judged correctly, they explained.
Mileage money had also been received by the second defendant.
All fixes were made in cash, for which he had to drive from his place of residence Völkermarkt to the bank in Bleiburg, he explained in his statement.
The main hearing will continue on September 23 with the questioning of witnesses.
Hewlett-Packard cuts another 30,000 jobs
In addition to the three-year ongoing staff reductions, the computer giant will cut further jobs in splitting the company.
The job cuts at U.S. computer giant Hewlett-Packard (HP) don't come to an end.
As part of its restructuring, the U.S. technology group is planning the reduction of 25,000 to 30,000 jobs in its corporate customer division.
As a result, costs amounting to 2.7 billion dollars (2.4 billion euros) will be saved, the company said on Tuesday.
The move will lead to a special charge of around 2.7 billion dollars, which will be incurred from the fourth quarter.
The three-year reduction of 55,000 jobs has not yet been completed.
At the end of the 2013/14 financial year, HP still had around 302,000 employees.
After-hours trading, Hewlett-Packard stock gave up 2.3 percent.
The print-growing group from Palo Alto in California is currently splitting up: This year, the computer and printer business is to be bundled in a new company - separate from the more promising areas with computer servers and data stores for companies as well as other services.
Both divisions are to be listed on the stock market.
The world’s second-largest PC maker after Lenovo recently added that the world’s second-largest PC sales, as well as weaker demand from corporate customers after its services.
In the third fiscal quarter at the end of July, sales of PC and printers declined by 11.5 percent.
Sales fell 8 percent to just under 25.4 billion dollars.
As a result, revenues declined in 15 out of 16 quarters.
HP responds with a split and massive job cuts to the change in the industry.
The 76-year-old company led by Meg Whitman only reacted late to the fact that customers are increasingly buying smartphones and tablets instead of conventional computers and doing more and more business online.
Whitman's restructuring plans envisage a total reduction of around 55,000 jobs.
