Serbian police's press spokespersons also suffered injuries as a result of the use of tear gas and pepper spray.
Time of decision on interest rates for US Federal Reserve
The world observes when the U.S. Federal Reserve comes together in two days, on Wednesday and Thursday, to make the important decision on whether or not, for the first time in nearly a decade.
A poll among 80 economists polled by Reuters found that a little more than half last week thought the Fed would do so now think the Fed will persist a little longer and keep interest rates at the current level of 0-0.25 percent.
It's a historic event because it's been seven years since the Fed put interest rates on NULL.
"For the first time, we're approaching a situation where a major central bank may potentially leave the zero range and a process of normalizing interest rates," said Angel Ubide, a senior associate at the Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The U.S. economy is on a relatively good level of performance, trillions of dollars are added to the balance sheet and generate only low inflation.
However, the Fed cannot ignore the less rosy outlook for global conditions.
It has warned markets to adjust to an increase, but the signs suggest the chances against such a move are good.
The decision is to be announced at 20:00 CET on Thursday
Death shooter at Delta State University left apology letter
After shooting and killing his girlfriend in Mississippi on Monday morning - and before shooting and killing his colleague later in the day - Shannon Lamb wrote a note to say he was "suffering" at the first murder and wanted "he could undo it," authorities announced Tuesday.
Police in Gautier, Mississippi, said Lamb, a lecturer in geography and social science education at Delta State University, called the emergency number around 10:00 a.m
and said he swept at someone and killed someone.
Lamb told the employee of the emergency call center that the police had to send police to his home.
He said "I shot my wife last night" and he also mentioned that there was a "sweet dog" in the house, but who "won't bother anyone," but will probably be excited.
Police asked the caller by his name, but he didn't give him.
Lamb didn't give an explanation for his shots
As police entered the scene, they found the body of Amy Prentiss, 41, and a handwritten note from Lamb, 45, which said: "I'm so sorry, I wish I could undo it.
“I loved Amy, and she’s the only woman who ever loved me.”
According to the note's writing, Lamb drove 300 miles to Delta State University, where he killed Ethan Schmidt, a professor of history, before finally taking his own life on Monday evening, when cops surrounded him.
University President William LaForge said he didn't knew about a conflict between Lamb and Schmidt, but "of course there was something in Mr Lamb's imagination."
Despite reports on Monday that Lamb allegedly believed Prentiss also had a relationship with Schmidt, authorities said on Tuesday there was "no information, no evidence" for a "triangular relationship."
Prentiss has been dubbed a "good person" by Shawn O'Steen, who testified that they had been married for about seven.
The two were divorced 15 years ago, but remained friends.
O'Steen said he and Prentiss had a daughter who is 19 and she was "devastated."
He said Prentiss were "absolutely best friends" to her daughter.
Prentiss worked online from home, according to Linsday Knowles, who was identified by the Sun Herald as a close friend of Prentiss.
Knowles said Prentiss, who owned a dog named Lightning, had been meetinggling with Lamb for about three years.
"She's always wanted to help other people," Knowles said
Police said Lamb had called relatives at a certain time on Monday to tell them "he's not going to jail."
When cops caught up with him on Highway 1 near Greenville later that night, Lamb drove his car to the side, fled on foot, ran into the woods and killed himself with a gunshot.
Lamb received a doctorate from Delta State University in the spring of 2015, according to his time on the university's website.
