Speaking to SBS6, Moreno said: "I know how it feels, I know how hard such a thing can be.
I'm feeling very bad about it, I'm so sorry.
PSV player Andrés Guardado has also been taken to hospital - after a foul play by Chris Smalling.
Surely David Cameron knows The Lorax better?
It’s one thing that all parents will soon learn: if your child loves a book, it won’t be long before you know it almost by heart.
Unless you may be David Cameron.
When he was asked by a provider of childcare systems to name his favourite children's book, the Prime Minister opted for The Lorax, Dr. Seuss's 1971 environmental fable.
It has been a difficult choice, Cameron writes in a message on SuperCamp's website, continuing: "funny, moving, creative and with a powerful message, it's a book that I like to read to my kids, because there always seems to be a picture or message that we've missed so far."
All well and good, except that the following paragraph, in which Cameron supposedly describes the storyline of the book, instead reproduces a synopsis of the 2012 film version that added new characters and details to expand the fairly short original text.
"In the middle of the walled city of Thneed-Ville, where all nature has gone and even the air is a commodity, a boy named Ted, the heart of Audrey hopes to win the girl of his dreams by fulfilling their desire to see a real tree," one reads. However, a love interest was Dr. Seuss, whose proper name was Theodor Seuss's hostage and who died in 1991
As if that weren't suspicious enough yet: the text presented by Cameron reads in part like the summary of the Lorax film, which is presented on Internet Movie Database's website and so begins: "In the walled town of Thneed-Ville, where everything is artificial and even the air is a commodity, a boy named Ted hopes to win Audrey's heart, the girl of his dreams."
Although he admits to being a pragmatic father, it seems unlikely that Cameron's plot is so unfamiliar. Here an unnamed boy hears how the local environment is devastated by the money-greedy, tree-slashing, thneed-knitting once-ler despite the protests of the eponymous Lorax, a squatting, mustached creature "who speaks in the name of the trees
Most likely, such a relatively low PR task was delegated to a helper who didn't read The Lorax to a child 20 times in a row.
A Downing Street spokeswoman said the text was likely to have come from Cameron's constituency office.
SuperCamps confirmed that the text was delivered by Cameron's staff but gave no further immediate comments.
