The Brexit (Brexit = Britain's exit from the EU) could gain an influx if the economy falls into a slump by 2017
Daviv Cameron's infertile renegotiation strategy to keep Britain in the EU gets deeper into trouble with each passing day, even without the choice of genius Jeremy "Is it yes or no?" Corbyn as leader of Labour or the TUC, who quarreled fiercely because of Europe on Tuesday.
Foreigners also have their Corbyns and Nigel Farages.
A European diplomatic friend said the other day that some member states, as deeply entangled in various crises as the EU is and not always out of its own fault, Britain's self-created agenda with divided concessions and disturbing treaty changes, which they mostly don't want, are full.
In Brighton, the TUC decided at its annual meeting that it also doesn't like it if they endanger social protection.
We could also call it Dave and George's selfie agenda.
Prime ministers and finance ministers climb summits or bilateral meetings in foreign capitals, take a photo of themselves in front of a famous local monument, with or no local greats, then come home and say they attract allies for reforms.
It's always about me.
My European friend, who wants an outside-looking Britain inside the EU tent - "Don't leave us alone with France," says Berlin - and makes it clear that more and more short-sighted ministers and civil servants in some capitals have come to the point where they don't care if we stay or leave.
Germany's prudent chancellor Angela Merkel is an ally, but she's been in power for 10 years and wants to leave office before it looks bad for her, as has happened with the exaggerated long-term persevers such as Thatcher (11 years), Mitterrand (14) and Kohl (16).
France's François Hollande looks ahead to his re-election or substitution in 2017.
He's also a fading force.
Poland may soon have a nationalist right-wing government, Spain a left.
It's hard to imaginate that they're going to pull out a leg to help the selfie-brits whose energy should be better used to prevent Scotland's exit from the UK - something they don't want, either, because most have similar separatist tendencies.
Therefore, some such ministers may even intentionally plan unhelpful remarks, entirely in the SNP style, during Britain's 2017 referendum campaign to speed up the Brexit process, my friend suspects.
"The renegotiation was a bankrupty" and "Britain has gained nothing of value from the renegotiation."
Europeans know how predatory Fleet Street can be at its expense and what EU skeptics are the oligarchy of the press baroon, as it was during Tony Benn-inspired referendum in 1975, which supported our entry in 1973 with a 2:1 ratio.
You can weakly remember Boris Johnson, the arch-euro opportunist who made a name as an "EU straight banana" Brussels correspondent in the early 90's.
With all his craziness, Ed Miliband knew who his enemies were.
These included the medley of tax-fearing rogues, phone-hacking foreigners and the pseudo non-neighbors who own most of our major newspapers.
Actually, Tony Blair thought similarly about them, but found that if you're sitting in a cage with a hot gorilla, you're better distributing bananas to deflect him instead of going to lunch and dinner.
In fact, Miliband was more Blairians than Blair himself when he resisted Cameron's referendum vortex, because Blair had put the principle above the unpassed constitution of the EU, which was then rejected by French and Dutch voters in 2005, which thus opened him a back door.
The press has never forgiven him.
But the nice Jeremy Corbyn who gave me a friendly slap on my backs in Westminster on Monday - I told him he doesn't need to waste any more time for me - seems in jeopardy to repeat Neil Kinnock's boycott Murdoch strategy of the 80's, a mistake then and now.
It may take time, but he has to develop a coherent strategy to win the mediary, not with me, but certainly with Andrew Marr, whose BBC sofa he left on Sunday.
Corbyn's official position on Europe - I express it kindly - is in development.
He says that we should stay in a reformed EU.
Well, most people outside of Ukip's traditional blue-dye tendency and the wilder climes of Tory's small-Langeranism, arguably anyone can say yes to that.
It's what Hilary "A Benn, but not a Bennite" Benn said on radio and on television this week, when the Boy Scout in him struggled not to admit Corbyn's appointment of provoking John McDonnell as shadow chancellor was a bad idea.
It reminded me of the 70's, when his old father on television struggled to be disloyal towards his Labour cabinet colleagues without going too far and losing his cabinet job.
Hilary's fight was even more attractive.
But struggling to stay "under all circumstances" - Benn's expression - wasn't what McDonnell said, nor what JC told his comrades-in-arms and deputies, according to all reports at their Monday night's retreat.
Whatever Corbyn's toughy bottom-up image wants to convey - keep in mind that he's even touched me - news discipline remains important in the age of round-the-clock TV and, above all, in the swirl of social media, a breeding ground of paranoia and treason.
It is right not to give Cameron a free ticket to Europe, which allows him to take Labour's support in the referendum campaign a matter of course, as McDonnell said.
Simon Jenkins thinks that way.
But McDonnell and Corbyn's fundamental ambiguities on EU membership - in line with their Bennite siege economy, their anti-capitalist past - is no longer just a matter of esoteric discussion among left-wing dissidents, for whom narcissism of small-distinction is a central principle of dogmatic disputes.
As with his ambiguities on Nato - Tom Watson is filly convinced that JC isn't launching a campaign to quit - foreign diplomats will be required to try to find a sense for their superiors at home.
Why should Paris or Berlin waste political capital - they also have suspicious voters - to make concessions to the Britons, if this can all be pointless?
The U.S. is already quite disappointed by the weak military performance in Iraq, Libya and Syria, even with a cost-cushioning of the Tory government in power, which I was reminded ofrom at the launch on Monday of something British Influence Security Forum.
Experts criticise Public Health England report on e-cigarettes
Claims by a state-funded agency that e-cigarettes are less harmful than smoking originated from a meeting of 12 people, in part with ties to the tobacco industry, researchers said.
Experts writing in the British Medical Journal (BMJ) joined Lancet by criticising the evidence used by Public Health England (PHE) in their e-cigarette report.
PHE published the "principle report" last month, describing it as a "comprehensive review of the evidence."
Yet some researchers have questioned the resilience of the data, pointing to the links between some experts and the tobacco industry.
An editorial in the medical journal Lancet last month attacked the "extraordinarily windy basis" on which PHE justified its most important conclusion.
Two other researchers write in BMJ and have asked if the claims "build on stone or sand."
Martin McKee, Professor of European Public Health at the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine and Simon Capewell, Professor of Clinical Epidemiology at the University of Liverpool, said: "A fundamental principle of public health is that strategies should be based on evidence of efficacy."
They said the public expect that PHE's claim that "the current best estimate that e-cigarettes are about 95% less harmful than smoking" is based on a detailed review of evidence and modelling.
In fact, this statement comes from a single session of 12 people convened to develop a model for multi-criteria Decision Analysis (MCDA) analysis in order to result in their opinions on the damage of different nicotine-containing products; the results of the meeting were summarized in a research report.
McKee and Capewell said that a sponsor of the meeting was a company called EuroSwiss Health, whose managing director is said to have previously received funds (BAT) for an independent study.
He also signed BAT's references on public health in a sustainability report, they said.
One of the 12 attendees at the meeting stated to receive funds from an e-cigarette maker, but did not add the funding, according to reports previously obtained by tobacco company Philip Morris International.
The reasons for selecting members of the panel will not be delivered, but this includes several well-known e-cigarette champions, some of whom also state industry funding in the paper.
Some other attendees at this session aren't known for their know-how in tobacco control.
The meeting was also attended by PHE's Tobacco management.
The group's research "concernably admits" that there is a lack of "hard evidence for the damage of most products on most criteria," McKee and Capewell wrote.
However, none of these connections or restrictions are discussed in the PHE report.
McKee and Capewell said PHE's claims that "there's so far no evidence that e-cigarettes serve as a way to smoke for children or non-smokers" were premature.
Prof Kevin Fenton, director of health and well-being at PHE, said BMJ's claims had already been responded to before.
He said: "E-cigarettes are significantly less harmful than smoking."
One in two lifelong smokers die from their addiction.
All evidence suggests the health risks of e-cigarettes are low in comparison, but that we continue to have the long-term effects of studying.
PHE has a clear duty to inform the public about what the evidence proves and is not, especially when there is so much public confusion about the relative dangers compared to tobacco.
Nearly 80,000 people die annually from a disease related to smoking, and smoking costs the national health care system £two billion a year.
By clearly presenting current evidence that e-cigarettes are not without risk, but represent only a fraction of the damage caused by smoking, we fulfill our national jurisdiction.
BBC should abandon EastEnders, says former boss of Channel 4
Former Channel 4 chairman Luke Johnson has said the BBC should stop broadcasting programs such as EastEnders and The Voice. He said commercial rivals would "give their right arm" for hit shows such as Albert Square Soap.
Johnson said that the broadcasting fee should only be used to make the kind of programs that are not broadcast through other UK broadcasters.
He criticised the BBC for placing public-service programmes, probably seen by a smaller audience, into hard-fought time slots against their rivals' most popular shows and from broadcasters such as BBC4.
After the BBC director-general, Tony Hall, said the company wanted to do more in partnership with other organisations, Johnson said he had tried to get into contact with the BBC for six years in his time at Channel 4 without success.
"I'm having problems with the idea of BBC commissioning or broadcasting programmes that would also be clearly shown by unsubsidized rivals," Johnson told the upper house communications committee as part of his investigation into the BBC's future.
If you would like to keep the broadcasting fee, which I do not think is a good idea in the medium or in the long term, you should at least put your production on programs that others do not or commission.
They are broadcasting The Voice in a first-class window because they want to achieve the broadest possible support for their future.
The idea that no one else would show this is a joke, it's ridiculous, it's nonsense, of course.
Johnson said: "I don't see why a regressive tax funds programs that could be funded even without subsidies.
The Voice is one such program, EastEnders is another, admittedly both in-house productions.
Honestly, other channels would give her right arm for it.
I'm trying to see how this is supposed to be public television.
I don't think this would diminish the BBC at all.
Johnson, an entrepreneur for series who led Channel 4 from 2004 to 2010, said the BBC was to blame for the hunt for reviews "because of the ambitions of the people who work there, and because they believe this is the best way to secure long-term support."
His comments on the BBC's most popular broadcasts reflect on what the former head of Sky, Tony Ball, suggested in his MacTaggart lecture in Edinburgh in 2003 that the BBC should sell shows such as EastEnders.
Johnson said that the rather public-law niche programmes "should go to BBC4 where you pretty much can't measure the audience, but they perform their duties, and they can argue if they go on their hypocritical missions to justify £4 £ broadcasting fees, "Well, of course, we've created all these obscure programs that no one has seen."
They put them in a window where no one ever wanted to see them.
Commenting on the broadcasting fee, Johnson told the committee: "I urge you to find a rather regressive system in terms of terms and conditions on who can get the best value out of it.
Of course, you all think, we all think you're getting a great value here for £150 a year, but for people who's a lot of money for the £150, ask me if that's true, because they don't consume much from the BBC.
Former Sky and Channel 5 boss David Elstein, who also testified before the committee on Tuesday, called for the BBC to review its Reithian mission to "inform, educate and entertain."
He said little of the BBC's production would now be officially classified as an education - he said it was 1% of its TV production and 0.1% on the radio - and it should take an example at the public television stations in Australia and Canada change this into "informing, enlightening and entertaining."
Commenting on the issue of partnerships, Elstein said the BBC should do more in areas such as those of the Proms, of which he said only a handful will be shown on television, and make alliances with the commercial broadcasters to make everything available online.
'Sonogenetics' can control brain cells through sound waves
The video shows the nematodes changing direction at the moment they are irradiated with sound pulses.
Scientists have bred worms with genetically modified nervous systems that can be controlled by outbreaks of sound waves.
The tiny nematodes change the direction at the moment they are irradiated with sound pulses that have too high frequencies for human hearing to be heard.
The impulses work by turning on motoneuron cells, which are genetically modified to carry the membrane channels that respond to ultrasonic waves.
More on this topic: Researchers read and write brain activity with light
The researchers said the worms showed the power of a new procedure, called sonogenetics, in which ultrasound can be used to activate a range of brain, heart and muscle cells outside the body.
Sreekanth Chalasani, a researcher at the Salk Institute in La Jolla, California, told the Guardian that the procedure could one day replace deep brain stimulation, an invasive procedure that emits electrical impulses into people’s brains to treat the symptoms of Parkinson’s disease.
Threadworms don’t usually respond to ultrasound, but Chalasani found that they did when surrounded by a liquid with microscopic air bubbles.
The bubbles, he figured out, amplify the ultrasonic waves, which then enter the worms.
The amplified ultrasonic waves act on structures called TRP-4 ion channels and are found in the membranes of some of the worm cells.
The sound waves effect the opening of these ion channels and activate the cells to which they are assigned, according to a report Nature Communications.
In order to create ultrasound-controlled nematodes, Chalasani genetically modified the worms in such a way that some of their motoneurons carry TRP-4 ion channels.
If he applies ultrasound to the altered creatures, the sound waves are amplified by the microbubbles and transmitted into the worms, where they turn on the changed motor neurons.
The procedure has some similarities to Optogenetics, a groundbreaking tool that enables scientists to switch neurons on and off with pulses of light.
But Chalasani said Sonogenetics could have some advantages over this technique.
In contrast to light, which must be sent down at an optical fiber to the desired location in the brain, low-frequency ultrasound waves can pass tissue unhindered and can thus be sent into the brain from above on the skull.
"We believe that with gene therapy and a therapeutic virus, it is possible to make the targeted human neurons temporarily susceptible to the ultrasound signal in a clinical setting for certain neurological treatments," Chalasani said.
Other applications could focus on muscle cells and insulin-producing cells, he added.
Illegal torture equipment - right on your doorstep!
The ads shame Britain's arms trade
Amnesty International anti-weapons watch trade show video.
Terrible Murder Drones.
Ankle shattering leg shackles!
Cluster bombs.
And electric shock rods that cause excruciating pain but leave no trace!
The cheerful voice and the cartoon pictures leave no doubt that this ad for the world’s largest arms fair is a combative parody produced by Amnesty International to spotlight the British government’s readiness to scramble weapons to repressive regimes via the Defence and Security Equipment International Jamboree at the ExCeL Centre in London’s Docklands.
Related: DSEI arms fair: Authoritarian regiments flock to London
But a separate campaign also leads to many posters that can be seen at bus stops and on the subway trains that require a second look.
"Important announcement: Will you be driving DLR from 15 to 18 September?" (Docklands Light Railway) can be readed in the font types of the London Underground and the hallmark of the DLR line, which changes into the image of a tank.
In September, a swarm of arms dealers will travel with DLR ... Customers are asked to help stop the arms fair.
The 300 posters are by artists who exhibited their work at the Museum of Cruel Designs in Banksys Dismaland.
A collective called Special Patrol Group helped with the distribution in London and used "Ad Space Hack Packs," a £6-pack of Allen keys that it is claimed "gain access to around one-third of bus stop advertising space on the planet."
Transport for London doesn't find this poster "vandalism" funny.
One of the parody ads produced by Special Patrol Group.
According to Gavin Grindon, curator of Crueol Designs and a lecturer at the University of Essex, artists are becoming more critical and trying to bring political works to audiences outside galleries.
Many artists get to work with activists within protest movements and notice that they have to venture outside into the real world so that their art has an influence on society.
Another of the anti-weapons trading posters greets commuters on the subway.
The Amnesty video has been viewed more than 100,000 times in 24 hours via Facebook and originated through the advertising agency VCCP, which works non-profit with the graphic artist and voiceover artist, who also works for free.
VCCP creative director Matt Lever was amazed at the silence that prevailed around the advertisement for the arms fair - through a vacancy of the ExCeL calender - and thought, "Let's give them the campaign they're trying to avoid."
They played with the invention of a more realistic dodgy corporate advertising, but "by inflicting with bright colors and animation, it stays stronger."
"They've done a fantastic job by helping us raise awareness about the sale of illegal tortury equipment at the fair," says Tom Davies of Amnesty.
What we need right now is an intervention by the British government and the guarantee that torture equipment will not be traded illegally on our doorstep.
Perhaps the most creative art around the arms fair comes from the area within ExCeL, where exhibitors fade fantastically euphemistic products.
In a press release, Saab discloses an assignment by the U.S. Army over "the AT4CS RS shot down from the shoulder."
This includes a "uniquely shaped warhead that delivers outstanding after-effect effects inside the target."
Whatever that does, it won't be pleasant.
Eight golden rules for living in student residences
Eleven years ago as a self-forgotten 19-year-old, I moved into a dormitory, still in the belief in Beret's and moral relativism, but it's only now I remember the tense smills of those I've annoyed - and the numerous, long-winded complaints about others.
Most of what I've learned can be distilled into eight simple rules.
The entry into dormitories ranges from slightly nerve-wracking to absolutely frightening.
It's tempting to talk: people tell where you're from, if and where you took Sabbath year, where you're related to Corbyn, and why you don't "honestlyly don't love or hate Marmite because seriously, guys, it's just a yeast extract HAHAHA, I'm in my room."
Take a breathe.
And help others do the same thing - ask questions and show interest.
It steers the conversation and helps you figure out the people you like as opposed to those you wouldn't talk to, even if your eyelashes were on fire and those fire extinguishers were.
Leave people.
People's rooms often turn into beverage receptions, movie-night venues and communal meeting places, but they're also the places where people get dressed and get their underweards.
Related to this: Don’t remember to start a relationship at university
Introverts receive rough treatment at the university.
Your room should be a refuge, but many dormitories form a space-to-space stroll culture that is friendly and social and can lead to lifelong friendship.
This can be hell for someone trying to recover.
Nothing in culture allows you to say, “I like you, and I want our friendship to continue, but you have to go away so that I get back the energy I’ve used for interacting with you.”
Try to feel when a fellow student is exhausted by conviviality - and send him a text message instead of knocking.
Speaking of knocking, do it.
A guy in my dorms had the notion that an unlocked door was an invitation.
He's seen a few things.
Make a friendship through baking.
Since you're starting university at the height of the baking mania, knocking on people's doors to imagine with a plate full of brownies is less strange than the greeting of the 1950's "Welcome to the Neighborhood, I've baked you something. So, and now please smile when I ask you a few personal questions."
Offering home-made treats is a good way to make friendships.
Your roommates feel obligated to converse and may eventually associate your face with a tasty surprise.
Of course this depends on whether you're a decent baker.
Poorly mixed lumps made of flour, sugar and fat cause people to put themselves asleep when you knock.
Stealing food is a common crime in student residences.
Of course, stealing chicken is a theft, but is it taking a drop of milk for your tea too?
Verify this with the milk owner.
Chances are good that they say to serve themselves, even if they cook inside.
If someone steals your belongings, that's annoying, and you've got my sympathy - but try to deal with it in a more balanced way than my roommate who added a dish soap to her milk to punish thieves.
She's in prison now.
You shouldn't make any noise that keeps people from sleeping or working.
It's not just about being a decent person, but that you're literally one of the people sitting in the glass house.
A girl in my hallway asked her neighbors if they had something to do to make drum & bass a bit quieter, because it was three o'clock in the morning, and she had an exam in the morning.
They reacted to it with even louder music.
When she finally quit at 5 a.m., she kindly let her neighbors sleep for another two hours before plundering her own music, closing her door and embarking on a three-hour exam.
Wouldn’t it be romantic if your future spouse happened to move into the same dormitory?
Conversely, it wouldn't be abysmally embarrassed if you're keeping a housemate for your future spouse, approaching her, having to separate again and then having to see her every day and perhaps witnessing all their mutual friends pull over?
Of course, the heart goes its own ways - but if a hot roommate is definitely not your future spouse, at least try to tell your abdomen a bit of sanity.
In the exciting moment when you notice that someone has left their keys in their non-closed room, the limit between an ulk and bullying is finer than it seems.
A good prank is funny, but it only takes moments for him to become a boomerang.
Give a valuable and amusing lesson about safety by hiding - but never mess up anyone's room with toothpaste and shampoo.
Close their door and place their keys in a glass of water, which you then place in the freezer - but no more than twice their possessions on the ceiling.
Terraced houses for refugees, single parents, low-income earners
The real estate entrepreneur Markus Gildner builds terraced houses for people who don't have a lot of money in the Middle Franconian Eckental.
For example, for refugees - but also as a signal: "Not only refugees need housing, but also the single mother and the low-income earner."
"I show how you can still build cheaply and well today," the 44-year-old says in the interview
Thousands of refugees are going to enter the housing market
The entrepreneur Markus Gildner thinks he knows how cheaper housing can be created quickly: He builds terraced houses for refugees in a project.
Mr Gildner, many refugees are going to enter the housing market.
What solution do you offer?
Markus Gildner: The mass accommodation in containers or gymnasiums has no sustainability.
Because people stay here to a large extent.
In view of the flow of refugees, we also need sufficient housing in the coming decades.
A very mundane, three-storey terraced house is the solution.
Gildner: A terraced house needs the least space.
It makes integration easier.
It's different when you put people in apartment blocks.
Each of the six houses that are currently being built in the pilot project consists of three housing units for four asylum seekers each.
Each unit has two bedrooms, a bathroom, a kitchen and a lounge.
It may be that a lot of refugees will say it's peace again, I'm going back.
Then the houses can be easily rebuilt according to the respective needs.
What are the costs?
Gildner: The price for 160 square meters of living space per house amounts to 184 000 euros, excluding land.
That's manageable.
The construction took six months.
That's appropriate.
Did the building regulations complicate their plans?
Gildner: In terms of construction law, I went through my project correctly.
It is always particularly complicated by the impending Damokles sword special construction and the parking space ordinance.
Creeply.
It is still assumed who creates three apartments in a terraced house needs at least three pitches.
Only in the cities there is a lack of space.
Refugees and asylum seekers don't have cars.
How did local residents react to their townhouse project?
Gildner: There were some who accused me of creating luxury buildings for asylum seekers.
Which's definitely not true.
But don't tighten the competition for affordable housing?
Gildner: It’s true: We’ve had a construction boom in Germany in the last five years, but there have been built luxury apartments that hardly anyone can afford anymore.
My project has the message, even the normal people need housing.
Not only refugees, but also the single mother or low-income earner.
I show how you can still build cheap and well today.
That's what it's all about.
Markus Gildner (44) studied microsystems technology in Regensburg and initially dealt with environmental analyses.
iOS 9: How to get the new iPhone operating system when Apple update is released
The new operating system can be requested by going toward the settings app, tapping "Common" and then choosing "Software Update."
If it’s ready, the phone will give you the option to download, and then you’re up for installation.
Some have encountered problems after the installation because they receive an error message, which indicates that the "software update has failed".
To circumvent the issue, users are advised to either wait until the onslaught goes back or try to update through iTunes.
The phone itself walks you through the rest of the process.
This can potentially mean deleting apps to make room - but Apple has made that more unlikely by slacing the update. Likewise, it will be easier to do so when using a special tool to delete apps and then reinstall them once the process is completed.
It's worth making sure that your mobile phone is charged during the update or located near a charger, so the battery is charged.
If the charge is too low, Apple won’t allow the update to continue, as the phone may switch off halfway and this could lead to cancellation of the update.
