Only after that will it be decided whether Wiese is displayed or not.
Authorities of Panama destroy 4500 marijuana plants
The fertile soil and the rainforest-typical climate of the Isla del Rey are ideally suited for thriving marijuana plants.
For three days now, Panama's authorities have been tearing out the 4500 plants and burning them.
Men beat 17-year-old Afghan
Witnesses are looking for the police for an incident in Bad Soden-Salmünster: On Friday evening, around 23.45 clock, three young men were on foot from Bad Soden in the direction of Salmünster.
One from the group, a 17-year-old Afghan, according to the police followed his two friends at a distance of about five meters and shouted to them that one should wait for him.
At that time, the group was located at the height of the bft petrol station.
There were also three people on the premises of the gas station.
One of these men shouted at the youngster and punched him in the face with his fist.
The victim suffered a nasal bone fracture and bled heavily from the nose.
When his two friends rushed in and appeasingly talked to the thug, he initially left from his victim.
By his friends, the injured young man has now been taken home.
The perpetrator pursued the group with a car up to their apartment and drove there demonstratively several times.
When you didn't see the vehicle anymore, the injured man went in front of the building to smoke a cigarette.
There he was attacked again by the thug and another male person.
With the help of a baton, the victim's face and thighs were beaten.
The injured person had to be taken to a hospital for medical care.
So far, there is only a vague description of the perpetrators.
One of the men was about 1.80 metres tall.
The second racket is said to have been about 1.65 metres tall and was clothed in jeans and a brown-and-white shirt.
Police ask witnesses and whistleblowers to call 06181/100-123.
Dr. Frank Müller takes over Praxis Felstehausen and invests 310000 euros
Silvia Müller-Gongoll and Frank Müller (from the left) take over the practice of Karl-Heinrich and Ursel Felstehausen.
Velvet municipality mayor Bernd Bormann, business promoter Uta Seim-Schwartz and Asendorf's mayor Heinfried Kabbert are also pleased about this.
In many places in the countryside, doctors are missing - Asendorf, on the other hand, is lucky: Dr. Frank Müller takes over the practice Felstehausen on 1 October.
While Ursel Felstehausen is retiring, her husband Karl-Heinrich will continue to support his successor in the next few months with half a job.
After a conversion phase, Müller reopens the practice on 12 October, until then he invests 310000 euros in the premises at St.-Marcellusstraße 6.
It was sympathy at first sight, when the Felstehausens memorized Frank Müller and his wife Dr. Silvia Müller-Gongoll years ago, who is currently completing her specialist training in the Verden hospital and also wants to enter the practice next year.
"We've always had in mind that the Müllers would fit well into this practice," says Dr. Ursel Felstehausen, laughing.
“When the time came, I just called her.”
The Müller couple liked the idea to operate their own practice in Asendorf in the future.
"The proximity and the constant contact with the patients are very attractive for us," says 39-year-old Müller.
"We want to get to know and accompany the families over the years."
As an advantage, he also sees being his own boss soon.
And so one came to an agreement, the Müllers bought the house with the practice.
However, they will continue to live in Bruchhausen-Vilsen.
The two-time family man Frank Müller also grew up there, his medical studies in Hanover were followed by wards in several medical practices as well as in the hospitals in Sulingen, Nienburg and Rotenburg.
He is currently working as a senior physician in Sulingen, and in 2009 he was appointed senior emergency physician in the district of Diepholz and volunteered at the DRK-Readiness Bruchhausen-Vilsen.
From 19 September, the practice will be prepared for the new boss.
While the surgeon Dr. Karl-Heinrich Felstehausen works as a general practitioner, his wife Ursel (internist) specializes in gastroenterology.
The Müllers, both internists, want to settle down as GPs.
"Endoscopy therefore comes out," says 38-year-old Silvia Müller-Gongoll.
After the conversion, the rooms will look more modern.
We will offer more ultrasound examinations as well as long-term and load ECGs and need one consultation room more.
In addition, the computer will be completely renewed.
Müllers will receive a subsidy of a total of 50000 euros as part of a subsidy programme from the district (25000 euros) as well as from the velvet municipality (12500 euros) and the municipality (12500 euros).
"This shows how proud and happy we are that the practice will be continued," emphasizes mayor Bernd Bormann.
According to Silvia Müller-Gongoll, about 2000 patients come to the practice Felstehausen every quarter, in the North German average practice there are about 500.
There is another general practitioner's office in Asendorf.
A positive side effect of the practical takeover by Frank Müller: According to Ursel Felstehausen, the pharmacy remains in Asendorf.
If no successor had been found, she'd have left, too.
By the way, anyone who attends Müller’s office hours will continue to look primarily into familiar faces: take over the seven medical assistants and the cleaning staff from Felstehausens.
With their farewell, an era ends in Asendorf, after all, the physicians practiced there for 30 years.
"In the beginning we only had two rooms," Ursel Felstehausen recalls smirking.
She feels a little melancholy after a long time.
"But we continue to live in Hohenmoor and are sure to see a lot of patients," says the internist.
Her husband adds with a laugh: "We've got a 6000-square-foot plot."
"So far I've always been pushing myself in front of horticulture, now it could be different."
In addition, the couple has two grandchildren and likes to travel.
Croatia: 'We're letting the refugees through'
"Now the border checks are also being ramped up at our southern border," Interior Minister Johanna Mikl-Leitner said.
Checks on the Hungarian border remain in parallel.
It is still clear that anyone who wants to apply for asylum can do this "of course".
The care and safety of the people is in the foreground.
But it is also clear "that clear signals are needed," the minister said.
People need to know that we don't accept boundless migration flows, even across the Austro-Slovenian border.
A controlled approach is needed.
Croatia considering 'corridor' - Slovenia denied
The Croatian government had previously announced that Slovenia also wanted to allow incoming refugees to travel in the direction of Austria and Germany and possibly also erected corridors for the refugees.
However, Interior Minister Vesna Györkös Znidar did not see this in Laibach.
The idea of corridors is "absolutely unacceptable", Slovenia will intensify the monitoring of the border - especially Hungary.
The border with Croatia, on the other hand, has already been severely controlled as an external border of the Schengen area.
As the Minister emphasized, Slovenian does not want to lock down his borders for refugees.
"Each EU member has to fill his obligations, otherwise the system cannot work," Györkös Znidar stressed.
According to the Croatian government, the first refugees had already tried in the night hours of Tuesday to enter Eastern Croatia at the Tovarnik/Sid border crossing in order to dodge registration.
The Croatian police were able to pick them up in the nearby corn fields.
According to local mediaries, police vehicles with new refugees have been constantly arriving in Tovarnik, Croatia.
They are mainly Syrians and Afghans, including numerous women and children.
Ambulance services and the Red Cross are also already in use.
In the coming days, his country expects around 4000 refugees, Interior Minister Ranko Ostojic said.
In addition, buses from Presevo on the Macedonian-Serbian border, which had previously brought migrants to the Hungarian border, would be diverted to Croatia, Croatian and Serbian media reported on Wednesday.
However, the government in Belgrade denied that it was instructing the people to take certain routes: "We can, nor do we want to do so," it said.
According to the media reports, a first bus with around 50 refugees had arrived in the Serbian border town of Sid in the morning, in the course of the day more buses were expected.
Via Croatia, Slovenia and Austria to Germany
The occupants had been traveling all night from the Macedonian border, about 500 kilometers away, through Serbia.
"We've heared that Hungary closed, so the police told us to go here," said one of the refugees.
They wanted to travel through Croatia, Slovenia and Austria further to Germany.
Thus, it could now actually come to the postponement by observers already predicted the previous Balkan route Turkey-Greece-Macedonia-Serbia-Hungary to a slightly western route.
Croatia: "Extensive Possibilities for Border Control
Croatia's interior minister Ostojic had previously stressed that his country had prepared appropriate plans for possible exceptional situations in the event of a strong influx of refugees.
He did not give details, but he pointed out that, as a non-Schengen member, Croatia had "comprehensive possibilities for border control."
They carry out strict checks in order to qualify for membership in the Schengen area.
Slovenia has been a member of it since 2007, the EU partners have consistently given a good testimony to the country in terms of the standard of border controls.
On the Serbian side, about 300 refugees are still stuck behind the Hungarian border fence.
According to Belgrade media reports, they had spent the past night at the Horgos border crossing, which leads to Röszke.
According to the Hungarian authorities, the two border crossings Horgos and Backi Breg remain completely closed.
It is still unclear whether the remaining refugees now want to travel to the Croatian border.
Serbian Interior Minister Nebojsa Stefanovic tried to reassure Wednesday morning during a local appearance in Horgos: he considers the debates about alternative routes of refugees, after the Hungarian border is now closed, to be premature.
At the moment, it would not be a very high number of refugees.
It cannot yet be said whether they would continue their journey via Hungary, Croatia or another country.
Helpers advise migrants - dangerous - alternative routes
However, private Hungarian refugee aides have already begun to distribute maps to the refugees, on which alternative routes through Croatia are marked.
However, the border crossing between Serbia and Croatia is not safe: in parts of the border area there are still unvailed minefields 20 years after the end of the Croatian war.
On Wednesday afternoon, Croatian police announced that a team of mine clearers in the border region had been sent.
