Trials of 32 other defendants in the case were ongoing.
Most of the defendants are from Ukraine and Georgia.
The perpetrators are said to have acted in both U.S. and Eastern Europe.
Ecumenical Hospice Aid Mannheim has beened for 20 years
There is a lot of activity around Mannheim's Paradeplatz.
People run their errands, partly quick steps, sometimes leisurely strolling.
You drive to the two stations of the Mannheim car salon at Paradeplatz.
Stopping light trains.
Vibrant, bulging life with wonderful autumn sunshine and in the middle of it four large plaques each with the headline "Before I want to die"...
Ecumenical hospice aid drew attention to its 20-year anniversary with this action and is certainly confronted with mixed reactions.
"It's about life, that's why we've gone with the action in the middle of the city and among the people," says Josefine Lammer.
The head of ecumenical hospice aid is available to passers-by for questions and conversations as well as the volunteers.
While some passers-by spontaneously reaches for chalk and supplements the phrase around "my life," "reading," "driving with the camper through the U.S., "stop the war" or "don't suffer for a long time," others stop briefly, say sentences such as "I'm not ready yet," and then go on.
Again and again small discussions develop on the sidelines about the action itself, about life, the handling of dying.
Peter Hübinger is also on site right now.
"Only if I realize that at some time the end of this world comes for me, I can live responsibly," says the director of the Diakonisches Werk Mannheim, which together with the Caritas Association is the sponsor of the Ecumenical Hospice Aid.
Therefore, this public-facing action for the 20-year-old is good.
However, it is understandable that not everyone wants to communicate publicly on the boards, after all, it is a very personal matter.
The topic of death and dying is still fraught with taboos, according to the experience of Josefine Lammer, although the hospice movement in recent years has contributed a lot to people opening up.
An assessment shared by Angelika Godefroid.
The dying and grieving attendant is one of the honorary employees of the first hour.
Confronted with the topic through personal experience, she read a lot of literature and decided to get involved in euthanasia.
When Ecumenical Hospice Aid sought volunteers by appeal, she immediately contacted us and was present at the foundation in 1995.
Godefroid belongs to a team of 45 volunteers - 43 women and two men - which accompanies sufferers and relatives in private households, hospitals, nursing homes and in the hospice Sankt Vincent.
They're all carefully trained.
Angelika Godefroid can deal well with her task and the experiences and experiences made.
"I see life from another wait, think it's precious and appreciate the moment," she says.
The offer of the Outpatient Ecumenical Hospice Service is free of charge and regardless of denomination.
The financial support of the health insurance companies and donations from relatives and associations enables the training as well as further training and supervisions of the dying and grief attendants.
Every two years, training courses for new employees are offered.
The plaques described on Saturday are now exhibited in the house of the evangelical church (M1, 1a) and in the house of the Catholic Church (F2, 6).
Further personal requests can also be noted on postcards there.
FedEx result mixed, financial outlook for 2016 lowered
FedEx reports disappointing results for the last quarter, and the delivery giant lowered its earnings outlook to an entire year because of weaker demand for freight services and higher costs in its ground transport service.
The company also said it plans to hire about 55,000 seasonal workers for leave.
At that point last year, FedEx announced that the company plans to hire 50,000 seasonal workers for the holiday period.
The stock fell above 2.5 percent in premarket trading 45 minutes before Wednesday's opening.
FedEx Corp. said earning a profit between $10.40 and $10.90 in the fiscal year, which ends next May, 20 cents less than in the earlier forecast.
Analysts were expecting $10.84, according to a survey by FactSet.
The lower outlook comes despite the price hikes, which average 4.9 per cent and will take effect on January 4 and despite higher surcharges for soil shipments of heavy and large parcels levied from November.
The company said that it nevertheless expects earnings to increase year-on-year due to cost reductions, higher revenues, and because of growth in online commerce.
Chairman and CEO Fred Smith said that the company "solidly generates revenue when considering the weaker-than-expected economic conditions, especially in manufacturing and global trading."
Plans for holiday employment were announced a day later than that of rival UPS, which plans to hire 90,000 to 95,000 additional workers, about the same number as last year.
In the quarter that ended on August 31, FedEx earned $692 million, 6 per cent more than the previous year.
Earnings per share amounted to $2.42 per share, just behind the $2.44 per share that twelve analysts surveyed by Zacks Investment Research predicted.
Sales rose 5 percent to $12.3 billion.
Six analysts surveyed by Zacks expected $12.23 billion.
FedEx has seen a decline in international priority shipping in recent years, causing sales in its Express shipping unit to stagnate.
But the boom in online shopping has boosted results at FedEx Ground.
In the last quarter, soil transport sales rose 29 percent, but operating profit was basically flat because of higher costs for larger packages and self-insurance.
The express unit recorded higher revenues due to higher prices, while earnings declined because of higher labour costs in the freight business.
In pre-market trading, about an hour before opening, FedEx shares declined by $3.85, or $2.5%, to $150.15.
On Tuesday, they closed with 11 percent loss for the year and 17 per cent below a record high on June 11.
At their most anticipated meeting in the year, Federal Reserve strategists spend two days this week sitting around a 27-foot Honduras mahogany table to decide if it's time to raise the key interest rate for the first time in nearly a decade.
Threatening above them at one end of the ornate, two-storey boardroom there is a mural of the United States.
The card is a reminder that Fed chairwoman Janet L. Yellen and her colleagues are supposed to put the nation's economic interests before all other things.
The central bank has a two-fold order from Congress: maximizing U.S. employment and keeping prices stable here.
But since the great recession, the Fed leaders have acknowledged that there is an unwritten third mandate: financial stability.
And this third mandate could be the X-factor in a decision that is too narrow to predict. It will be announced on Thursday whether the U.S. economy is ready to be weaned from the unprecedented incentive by the near-zero short-term interest rate since the end of 2008.
Although most data from the economy shows that it is growing solidly, recent turmoil in global financial markets could make the already cautious Fed's representatives anxious to amplify this volatility by raising the interest rate on federal bonds - even by just a quarter percentage point.
"Obviously, the labor market and inflation will primarily be the most important thing they're observing," said Lindsey M. Piegza, chief economist at brokerage firm Stifel Nicolaus & Co.
"But I think if they were looking for a pretext not to raise the interest rate, the heightened uncertainty around the global market is enough justification to continue waiting," she said.
There's no consensus on what the Fed is going to do, which unsettles the financial market in itself.
About half of economists surveyed by Bloomberg recently predicted a rate hike this week.
Financial markets, however, expect the Fed's wait-and-see, with a key indicator indicating only a 30% chance of an increase.
This suggests a decline in the stock market if the Fed raises the sentence unless the strategists mitigate the blow by promising that a further increase is far away.
On Tuesday, the Dow Jones Industrial average jumped 228.89 points, or by 1.4% to 16,599.85.
The action was sparked by expectations that the Fed's policy will maintain the phrase this week, said John Lonski, chief economist at Moody's Capital Markets Research Group.
It would be a surprise if the Fed lifted the sentences at that point," he said.
If she does, "she'd have to go very far in her statement to calm the financial markets so they don't panic."
Other experts argued that a rate hike would reassure the financial markets because it would eliminate uncertainty about when the Fed is going to trade.
As recently as a month ago, the Fed seemed on track to raise the sentence by 0.25 percentage points this month, the first small step in a slow tightening of monetary conditions, which would test the strength of recovery in the U.S.
Yellen had said in the spring and early summer that she expected an increase in the sentence this year, despite complaints from liberals that more economic progress would be needed.
Although job creation slowered in August, improvement in the labour market this year was solid.
Unemployment rate fell to 5.1% last month, the lowest in more than seven years and close to the level the Fed sees as full employment.
Wage increases have shown signs of recovery.
And although inflation was constantly below the central bank's annual target of 2%, the Fed's representatives said the main reason was the temporary effect of significantly lower oil prices.
The economy "can handle higher interest rates and needs them because of tension in the labor market," said Ian Shepherdson, chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics.
But financial markets around the world shrugged at the end of last month after China devalued its currency.
Dow Jone's industrial average fell more than 16% below its peak in May when investors feared that a slowdown in China's economy would curtail global growth.
The market has rebounded somewhat this month, with the Dow now being nearly 10% below May's high.
For the Fed’s representatives, market turbulences influence the complex calculation of when to raise interest rates.
"If the events in the financial markets and the news about the weakness of China's economy hadn't occurred over the past few weeks, they'd tighten the reins in this session," said Stephen D. Oliner, a senior research fellow at UCLA Ziman Center for Real Estate and a former employee of the U.S. Federal Reserve.
Now he predicts that she will wait at least a month to get a better grip on the impact.
"There's a lot of uncertainty about what the impact on the U.S. economy will be, and there's almost no reliable data describing the developments in China," Oliner said.
The health of the global economy is none of the Fed's main concerns, so it shouldn't be technically involved in its decision-making.
But, in the end, the Fed's monetary authority has become in the world.
The central bank's influence has been enormous in recent years.
The mere hint from the then-head Ben S. Bernanke in 2013 that the Fed could "negate" its monetary expansion sparked confusion around the globe.
In a world where markets and economies are increasingly intertwined, the Fed’s representatives can be expected to not only monitor developments elsewhere, but also consult with other central banks and experts to gain a better understanding of the global economy.
Their decision-making is "more art than science," said Zachary Karabell, head of Envestnet Inc's global strategy for wealth management.
Jeffrey Lacker, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, VA, noted this month that "the Fed has a history of overreactions on financial market movements that are apparently not linked to the economic fundamentals."
Lacker, voting member of the Federal Open Market Committee who is campaigning for an interest rate hike, cited decisions in 1998/1999 as an example.
A financial crisis in Asia led the Fed to lower its key interest rate by 0.75 percentage points in three sessions "despite limited recognizable impacts on U.S. growth," Lacker said.
A year later, the Fed's representatives reversed these cuts.
The Fed should put aspects of financial stability first only during a serious crisis such as the 2008 market collapse, said Adam S. Posen, a former member of the Committee on the Bank of England's interest rate fixing.
Recent turbulence doesn't come close to this level, he said.
"She certainly doesn't want to get into the situation where she's afraid of a rate hike because the markets are getting upset," said Posen, president of Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The Federal Reserve Act still contains the double mandate and does not mention financial stability, and until that is changed, I don't think it should precede the time.
Don’t you need a great Thai market?
Try LAX-C in Chinatown.
Yeah, Chinatown
The market/camp was opened in 1997 and is owned by LAX-C Inc.
LAX-C is often referred to as Thai Costco and is a Thai wholesale market that also sells to the general public.
Yeah, he's in Chinatown, not Thai Town.
The market/camp is a central point of contact and offers a confusing number of fresh, frozen and dried goods, popular Thai and Asian snacks, and dishes, furniture, religious jewelry and Christmas decorations.
There is even an auto department if you need engine oil.
The shop is huge, and it takes over an hour to stroll through the aisles.
If the whole shortery makes you hungry, there is for you a meal in the LAX-C BBQ Express.
The kitchen (good and inexpensive) in this shop is close to the checkouts and has various takeaway options.
What's here: Do you need anything - anything - Thai?
LAX-C probably has it.
Products are reasonably priced and a number of them are sold in large quantities.
Fresh products include Asian specialties and regional Thai herbs, fruit and much more.
The meat counter is extensive, as is the fish counter - the staff behind the counter will clean and fillet the fish and even fry for you if desired.
A good part of the shop belongs to the massive freezing department. Here you will find frozen curry leaves, bitter melon and galgant, whole ducks, fish, beef blood and bile, swine-naturous intestines, fish balls, regional sausages, commercially prepared food and more.
Who shops here: restaurant owners and chefs, including individual families and individuals.
The wide corridors are suitable for shopping trolleys and forklifts.
You can find here: fresh Monthong Durian next to the cash register.
Where it's located: The store is located in an industrial area of Los Angeles on the edge of Chinatown and downtown - a few blocks away from Philippe The Original.
Parking: LAX-C has its own parking grounds, along with other shops and restaurants.
Even if the shop is well frequented, it is not difficult to find a parking space.
Hoyerswerda: Small life support for refugees
More and more refugees are arriving in Germany these days.
Giving them a warm welcome doesn't go without the commitment of countless volunteers.
Also in Hoyerswerda there are a number of people who unselfishly support asylum seekers in the city.
They include Petra and Klaus Heine.
Petra Heine is a teacher with heart and soul.
Even with her 74 lenzen, she still likes to teach.
Every Thursday she does it in the asylum seeker shelter on Dillinger Straße.
There she helps refugees from Syria, Kurdistan, Kosovo and Pakistan to learn the German language.
Of course, this is very different teaching than at a real school.
Brazil's pediatric society Rio Grande promotes nutrition for healthy breastfeeding
Brazilian pediatric organization SPRS is trying to enlighten women in a shocking way about the importance of good nutrition during breastfeeding. It does so with the presentation of images of babies sucking on boobs painted with depictions of unhealthy foods.
Breast milk consists of a variety of proteins, fats, vitamins and carbohydrates that give babies all the nutrients they need to build a strong immune system.
Due to the additional energy requirements, pregnant women and young mothers are advised to increase their daily calorie intake by about 500 calories a day.
However, new research suggests that higher calorie intake is not the only dietary adjustment that mothers should make.
A recent study led by Robert Waterland, a professor at Baylor College of Medicine in Texas, showed that a healthy diet of pregnant women actually affects the genes of her babies and protects them from the development of tumors.
Unfortunately, an unhealthy diet of the child can negatively affect the immune system.
Exemptions for the new Bernrieder sports hall
The municipal council Bernried approved the building application for the 2.3-million-euro sports hall.
And he made an exception.
The roof may become flatter for solar system.
The plans for the long-awaited gym building on the Bernrieder sports grounds are progressing step by step: In the most recent municipal council meeting, the developed building proposal was unanimously approved without major discussions.
Previously, among other things, the panel had passed an application for a construction "exception" or "liberation" from the valid development plan.
The "exception" was about permission for a special form with regard to the roof construction.
Accordingly, a "saddling roof with non-centry ridge and roof overhaul" is to be erected.
The background of the planning is the planned use of the roof area for solar systems: "As a result, we get a wonderfully large south roof together," explained Mayor Josef Steigenberger in the meeting.
The "liberation" was again about the roof inclination, which for energy reasons - unlike prescribed in the development plan - should be below ten degrees.
As a result, the volume of the hall is significantly reduced.
A "liberation" became necessary because the development plan with regard to the propensity of the roof does not allow any "exceptions" - whereby the Bernried municipal council usually proceeds rather restrictively in the case of liberation applications.
When it comes to gymnasium construction, however, this is not a recurring project: "We can't set a precedent," says Steigenberger.
The gym building at the sports center is a joint project of the municipality and the SV Bernried.
Around 2.3 million euros have been included in costs.
Grants are expected from school and grassroots sports funding.
In the next procedural step, the building application for the 18 x 36-meter hall will now be submitted to the district office for examination.
Peißenberg clinic moves to Schongau
Peißenberg hospital is to be closed sooner than planned - namely 2016.
At the same time, Dr. Wilhelm Fischer (66) will become the new medical director at Schongau Hospital.
The station and the majority of employees are moving from Peißenberg to Schongau.
This envisages the surprising plans of the hospital GmbH of the county, the district councillor Andrea Jochner-Weiß confirmed.
However, the Supervisory Board will make a decision on this, and it will meet next Wednesday, 23 September.
If the plans were to be implemented, all of the nearly 50 employees at the hospital in Peißenberg would continue to be employed.
The vast majority should move to Schongau with Fischer and the operinating internal station.
The rooms are available.
Employees who cannot go to Schongau should be offered a workplace in Weilheim, according to Jochner-Weiß.
Employees are aware of the plans.
The change was triggered by Dr. Andreas Eder, who has only been medical director of the Schongauer Klinik since the beginning of the year and is now moving to a private practice in the immediate vicinity of the hospital.
On the question of who should take the succession, according to Jochner-Weiß, the name "Fischer" came into play, who agreed to take over the management from 1 October and to move with his team to Schongau.
For the house in Schongau, this means in any case an "upgrading" and a widening of the offer in the area of "inner medicine," according to Jochner-Weiß.
Fischer is a specialist in pacemakers and defibrillators.
Schongau (180 beds) specializes in gastroenterological diseases at the Innere Station.
For the next two years, Fischer and his team, if the Supervisory Board agrees, will lead both houses, until next year the former hospital of Knappschaft, which still has 35 beds, is to be finally closed in Peißenberg after almost 140 years.
Until now, the county had planned this for 2018, when the current renovation at Weilheim hospital has been completed.
Councillor Jochner-Weiß, who is also chairman of the Supervisory Board, countered rumours that the House, which is owned by the Bundesknappschaft, would in future be used as accommodation for asylum seekers.
Jochner-Weiß could not say that there were several considerations of how to proceed with the property.
There is currently a short-term care of Caritas and a day psychiatry.
"Something better couldn't happen to us, such an opportunity doesn't come back," Fischer said about the quasi-relocation to the Lech, which had been received by the employees "positively and very understandingly."
There was no real perspective for the house, in which Fischer has been working as chief physician for over 30 years.
He had been asked by Schongauer colleagues to take over the post of medical director.
Peißenberg Hospital (once 100 beds) has been repeatedly threatened by closure in recent decades, departments have been closed, for example surgery and obstetricance.
