Hunting accident with two injured in Pratau
Two tractor drivers were seriously injured by gunfire during their corn harvest on Tuesday afternoon.
During harvesting on a cornfield near Pratau (district Wittenberg), several hunters were deployed, the police said.
After a shooting, a 63-year-old and a 22-year-old man suffered serious injuries and were taken to hospitals.
Exactly how the accident occurred was initially unclear.
Criminal investigators are investigating.
Missing 16-year-old from Rhineland-Palatinate victims of violent crime
A suspect has led the police to the corpse of 16-year-old from Rhineland-Palatinate, who has been missing since Saturday.
In interrogation, the 20-year-old admitted killing the youths and throwing her corpse into a canal in Rockenhausen.
A 16-year-old from Rhineland-Palatinate who has been missing since Saturday has fallen victim to a violent crime.
The body of the young woman was found on Tuesday evening in Rockenhausen near Kaiserslautern, prosecutors and police said on Wednesday.
A 20-year-old also arrested on Tuesday evening is a suspect, who confessed to the crime and led the investigators to the location of the body.
The 16-year-old had been reported missing on Sunday morning after failing to come home the previous evening after a visit to an autumn festival in Rockenhausen.
In the search for the young woman, investigators initially came across personal belongings of the victim.
During interrogations in the circle of friends and acquaintances of the misseed, there was finally a suspicion against the 20-year-old.
The alleged perpetrator then led the police to a canal in Rockenhausen, where the body was found.
According to the authorities, the arrestee admitted during the police interrogation to having killed the 16-year-old and placed the body in the canal.
The 20-year-old was to be presented to the magistrate on Wednesday, the investigators charged him with manslaughter.
Information about the closer circumstances of the act should bring the further investigation.
Refugees need shelters: Study warns of housing gap
The situation on the German housing market is tense.
More and more refugees are also looking for an affordable place to stay.
The pressure on the housing market is growing.
Social housing faces a challenge.
Distributive struggles on the German housing market have already begun.
With incoming refugees and job seekers from the EU, more and more people are pushing into a market that is already overloaded in many major cities.
They compete with low-income citizens for the last affordable housing.
To meet the demand for affordable housing, 400,000 new units would have to be built every year by 2020.
This is the conclusion of a study on behalf of the "Social Housing Alliance".
By comparison, 260,000 units will be completed in Germany this year.
This means that 140,000 apartments are missing.
Refugees are no longer obligated after the first three months of their stays to live as asylum seekers in an initial reception shelter.
Many then come across the housing market and line up into the long queues in front of the rental apartments.
According to the Federal Statistical Office, however, less than half of all asylum seekers lived for rent last year.
However, the negative development of the housing market is not only due to the increasing number of refugees.
The study also accounts for significant omissions in housing policy in recent years.
Germany is pushing ahead with a housing deficit that is getting bigger from year to year.
Around 770,000 residential units are currently missing in the metropolitan areas, complains Matthias Günther, author of the study.
Many professionals therefore necessarily lived in shared rooms in large cities.
Young adults involuntarily stayed longer at "Hotel Mama" because of the slump in the housing market.
In the major cities, the number of squares per capita has fallen again for the first time in recent years.
In order to solve the problem, the "Social Housing" alliance is proposing a restart.
New incentives are intended to boost social housing again.
Thus, the real estate transfer tax could be waived and the property tax for subsidized apartments could be temporarily suspended.
In addition, according to the alliance, attractive tax depreciation options are necessary.
The subsidy program for refugee shelters of the Staatsbank KfW is already well received by cities and municipalities.
A good week after the start, more than half of the approximately 300 million euros available had already been retrieved on Monday evening with 184 million euros.
Now the project could even be increased.
Refugees have free passage: Croatia and Slovenia set up corridor
There is practically no getting through after Hungary, as more and more refugees choose the way via Croatia.
Zagreb announces to grant transit via Slovenia to the north without hurdles.
However, the route is still dangerous.
Croatia wants to communicate with the neighbouring country Slovenia about the establishment of a corridor for refugees.
"I've spoken to Slovenia's interior minister, and if it's necessary, we're going to organize a corridor," Croatia's interior minster, Ranko Ostojic said.
Slovenia is located between Croatia and Austria and already belongs to the Schengen zone.
The EU country of Croatia is not yet a Schengen country.
After the foreclosure of the Serbian-Hungarian border, hundreds of refugees have set off for Croatia to get from there to Western Europe.
500 people had crossed the border at Tovarnik, reports the Croatian television channel RTL.
Hungary had sealed off its border for refugees the previous day.
New laws, which came into force on the same day, also make ordinary asylum procedures in Hungary virtually superfluous.
Croatia wants to let the refugees pass.
Croatian Prime Minister Zoran Milanovic said: "You can travel through, and we're preparing for this possibility."
These people are there, it's women, children and men who want to live and achieve something.
However, they are people who did not want to live in Croatia.
The Croatian police had picked up the first refugees on Serbia's border with the EU neighbouring Croatia in the morning.
According to media reports, most came from Syria, Iran and Afghanistan.
They came both by bus and with taxis to the Serbian border town of Sid.
Immediately after their arrival, they would have tried to get across the green border to Croatia on foot.
On the Croatian-Serbian border, the refugees do not encounter border fences.
But the bordering strip harbors other dangers: there are still sharp mines from the Yugoslav civil war in the 1990s.
Mine clearers should now quickly create a safe passage.
However, there are hardly any more refugees coming to Hungary after the closure of the border.
On Tuesday, police counted 366 picked-up people.
Shortly before goal closed on Monday, there had been 9380.
The new Hungarian laws classify the overcoming and damage of the border fence as an offence.
35 criminal cases were therefore initiated in the southern Hungarian border town of Szeged.
Convicts face imprisonments of up to five years and deportation from the country.
According to the immigration authorities, a total of 94 asylum applications were received on Tuesday in the newly established "transite zones" at the border, of which almost all - 93 - at the most important transition Röszke.
Applicants included 13 families with young children and two elderly people.
As of Wednesday morning, there were still 40 refugees.
In practice, however, no one has a chance to be granted asylum in Hungary anymore.
According to Hungary, Serbia is a "safe third country."
The rejected asylum seekers are to be deported there.
In front of the "transit zone" in Röszke, only a few hundred refugees gathered.
In Röszke, workers started cleaning up with it.
They collected the rubbish that tens of thousands of people had left on the side of the road in recent weeks.
Barriers have been dismantled and one of the reception camps in the border town has been rebuilt
No one wanted to comment on the goal of this conversion.
Austria reaches only a few refugees after closing the border between Serbia and Hungary.
The country has also stepped up its border controls.
Around 20,000 migrants were still in the country, according to government figures.
In Salzburg, about 1400 people are waiting for the onward journey to Germany.
The train service from there to Germany was halted in the morning on the instructions of German authorities.
In Freilassing, smaller groups of refugees regularly arrived on foot.
Mayor drove 'stranded' across the border
"We were busy cleaning up after Sunday's bridge festivality, when suddenly ten people with travel suitcases stood at the entrance of the tent," according to Wernstein's mayor Alois Stadler.
Tourists, they wanted to take the train to Passau.
However, because Germany had surprisingly suspended rail traffic with Austria on Sunday because of the flow of refugees, it was over for them in Wernstein," said Stadler, who immediately offered his help and chauffeured the stranded together with two other helpers and their cars across the border to nearby Passau.
Previously, we tasted the unexpected guests, with snacks and drinks.
We immediately said we'd take her by car to Passau.
"This was self-evident for us," Stadler said in the OÖN talk.
They were very grateful.
A man didn't mind handing over a generous donation - 100 euros for the fire department.
"We were very happy about that," Stadler said.
The stranded train passengers had switched from an ICE to a regional train towards Passau on Sunday evening in Wels, in order to try over the border after the short-term announcement that rail traffic to Germany had ceased.
However, the regional train was parked before the border in Wernstein am Inn.
In Wernstein, cross-borders are generally very popular.
The bridge festive combined with the Bavarian neighbors on Sunday, the day before a two-bridge run sporting - organized by Union Wernstein and Bavaria from Neukirchen.
The fact that Viktor Orbán is now sending more tens of thousands to us is a logical result of hypocritical politics:
Instead of immediately helping the establishment of structures that ensure humanity in the Röszke camp, our Federal Chanceller has officially and publicly put the attitude of the Hungarian government in the haze of National Socialism.
Sufferers of this policy are not only those Austrians who are now confronted with chaotic conditions in the border areas.
Refugees, too, will have to realize that the "praised country," which has invited and welcomed them, is nothing but another refugee camp.
It will be a major challenge, in view of the ongoing flow of refugees and the approaching winter, to establish humane conditions for those seeking protection with us.
And it will be an even bigger one - maintaining peace, security and stability in Austria.
Konrad looking for 35,000 winter-proof quarters
Konrad has his hands full with refugee accommodation.
Christian Konrad is used to his word having weight.
The government has deployed the former powerful Raiffeisen banker to set up quarters for the thousands of refugees.
Konrad has already managed the chaos at the initial reception centre in Traiskirchen.
"We don't have any more homeless people in Traiskirchen," he said of his first success.
