The Interior Ministry, on the other hand, spoke of a "crisisi
In total, Konrad expects a requirement of 85,000 quarters this year.
In order to meet the number, 35,000 shelters still have to be found in the next few weeks - 20,000 are to be provided by the states, 15,000 by the Confederation.
If there are more refugees, we can do it.
"But it's not a barrel without a bottom," Konrad said.
He has already had discussions with the state governors.
These would have assured him to create quarters for the coming refugees.
"It's going well in Upper Austria," Ferry Maier said.
The former VP mandator supports Konrad.
Tomorrow there will be a meeting with Cardinal Christoph Schönborn to clarify how the church will help.
The asylum coordinator also puts great expectations into the ORF action "Helping like us".
Private individuals can easily offer accommodation on the platform.
In addition, one hopes for hotels that are vacant in winter.
Konrad and Maier yesterday criticized the standards for accommodation as "very high."
In times of need, it could not be that quartiers have to maintain a certain height of the towel rails or size of the skylights.
"Emergency standards" would have to apply for a fixed-term period.
Konrad justifies with his experience that, in contrast to the Ministry of the Interior, he is more successful in accommodation.
I'm an elderly gentleman who doesn't readily say untruth in his face.
Persuasive power is his most important weapon.
Konrad and Maier do not incur any costs for the republic, as they themselves explained.
They want to increase their number of employees to three to four.
Their own neighborhood search has not yet been successful.
Konrad wanted to set up a container at Stephansplatz as an office.
However, there appear to be problems with approval.
Linz calls for a better division
For the time being, the city of Linz is not planning to open urban sports halls and other buildings, such as Tips-Arena and Design Center to accommodate refugees.
This is currently not necessary, since those affected can currently be accommodated without these measures, says Mayor Klaus Luger (SP).
