Brulljesmacher from Kallstadt
The Palatinate ancestors of Donald Trump came from a place whose inhabitants are cited show-offs.
Some also associate this trait with the billionaire who wants to become U.S. president.
Donald Trump (69) is always good for excitement.
Whether he expresses himself xenophobically, tackles journalists rude or reveals knowledge gaps in international crisis politics - the candidate for the US presidential office vigorously stirs up the election campaign.
And scores well.
This is also registered in Kallstadt in Palatinate, where the grandparents of the real estate billionaire come from.
The New Yorker with the foehn hairstyle is familiar to many in the winemaking village on the Weinstraße.
Some in the 1200-inhabitant place are over -twenty corners related to it.
His grandfather came from Kallstadt in the Palatinate: Donald Trump, here in campaign mode.
Kallstadt's Simone Wendel made a film about Trump.
And what do villagers think about his goal of becoming the most powerful man in the world?
"I think they're kind of exciting, but they'ren't so deeply impressed in their Kallstadt style either," says Simone Wendel.
Born in Kallstadt, she has made an amusing documentary about the place and its famous offspring, which in addition to Trump also includes the ketchup family Heinz.
Trump, who is not known for modesty, also speaks in "Kings of Kallstadt" (2014) - so with the statement that he would certainly also be successful if he lived in Kallstadt.
He didn't make it to the king of hearts there.
Although there is respect for the performance of the family, there also tends to think that Trump is "alreadily a bit of a big mouth," says Wendel.
Coincidentally, the nickname that Kallstadt has in the vicinity was "Die Brulljesmacher" - to High German: the show-off, she says.
So Trump comes out of the village of show-offs.
The Trump success story began in 1885, when the later grandfather emigrated and opened a restaurant for gold diggers in the U.S.
The real estate group was the cornerstone of land purchases in New York.
But the life of the grandparents was not free of tragedy: their desire to return home permanently failed on the Bavarian state, to which the Palatinate belonged at that time.
He did not accept Trump because he once ran away without a permit, the director of the Institute of Palatinate history and folklore, Roland Paul, had declared the film launch.
Trump is the grandson of an "illegal expatriate."
This contrasts with the sometimes marked statements of the descendant.
He had repeatedly spoken derogatoryly about Mexican immigrants in the U.S. and demanded the construction of a border wall to deter illegal immigrants.
"Just as he squeaks, that's not so sympathetic," says Hans-Joachim Bender, a distant relative of Trump ("Grandma was a born Trump, the grandpa was born Heinz").
The retired winemaker sees the American rather distant, "he's always so breawing."
Does he think Trump's coming?
"I can't imagine that," the 72-year-old says.
"He's never taken care of Kallstadt."
The retiree Veronika Schramm mentions, like many Kallstadters, that a descendant of the Heinz family donated something for the church organ during a visit, which was generally well received.
"I can't imagine that from Donald," the 68-year-old says.
She thinks it's better if he doesn't win the election, because "he has such radical views, I don't know if that's good."
If those who were in charge at the time of his grandfather in the United States had been like him, "he would never have arrived there."
"There are more interesting topics than him," says winemaker's daughter Sarah Bühler.
And as president?
"It's not my president," she said.
“The grapes are ripened even without President Trump.”
Wendel's film, for which Trump has long been interviewed, has seen 12 000 moviegoers, even in the U.S
