4 people killed after police shoot rubber bullets at protesters in Nepal
Four people, including a 4-year-old boy, were killed in southern Nepal after police shot rubber bullets into a crowd demonstrating against the proposed new constitution, an official said Wednesday.
Violence erupted on Tuesday night in Rupandehi district, about 175 miles west of the capital Kathmandu.
Bishnu Prasad Dhakal, senior district official, said more than 1,000 protesters had gathered outside a police station, with some of them throwing rocks and incendiary bombs.
Mr Dhakal said the police used tear gas and then shot into the air before resorting to rubber bullets.
20 police officers and five civilians were injured, he said.
It was not immediately clear why the rubber projectiles used, which are known to kill, but which are traditionally used as a less deadly alternative, apparently produced such a high death rate.
The altercation was the last in weeks of violent protests in Nepal's southern plains, where members of the Madhesi ethnic group say new provinces formed under the proposed new constitution would reduce their political influence.
There were also protests in western Nepal from the ethnic group of Tharus who want a state of their own.
At least 40 people, including 11 police officers, have been in violent clashes since the 10.
August killed when the four major political parties signed an agreement to divide the country into provinces - an important step towards a new constitution, but a very contentious issue in Nepal that has been centrally governed for centuries.
Working on a new constitution has been on its way since 2008, but legislators accelerated the process after the devastating earthquake that killed thousands of people in April.
Legislators voted this week on certain clauses in the draft constitution in Kathmandu, and officials have said the new constitution could come into force on Sunday.
Construction area planned in the city
The property at Emder Jugendzentrum Alte Post is to be redeveloped.
The city is currently preparing everything for marketing the 4000-square-metre area.
A mixture of commerce, commerce and housing is planned - but there are no concrete offers yet.
Emden - The city of Emden wants to market a 4000-square-meter plot in the immediate vicinity of the youth center Alte
