The city of Emden had already bought part of the property, which is located on the Ringstraße / Corner Große Straße, a few months ago and demolished the building located on it.
Project Hieve: Details are on the table
The plans for the holiday home settlement on the Small Sea near Emden take shape.
The investor, for the first time, publicly presented plans for the project.
A number of residents are still worried - they fear the sell-off of their recreational refuge
For months had been negotiated behind closed doors, pondered, debated and drawn.
Now the plans for a new holiday home settlement on the Small Sea near Emden are on the table.
The interested party, the medium-sized Emder company "System-Bau", publicly presented its plans for the first time on Monday evening in the urban development committee of the Emder Council.
Originally, this item had been on the non-public part of the agenda.
Under the pressure of the project critics and the FDP faction, who accused the investor and the administration of secrecy, Ralf Behrends of the system-construction management went into the offensive: "We don't have an answer to all questions yet," he said before the council members and more than 50 visitors.
But it’s good to be able to meet all rumours now.
The project, which costs around ten to twelve million euros, has been under discussion for months.
Fierce reactions among sea stall owners who had come into the meeting evoked the pictures and new details about the desired hotel construction.
Roberto Schulz, sea stall owner and member of the boarder in the advocacy group of residents, Pro Hieve, complains: "This will be a giant block, which surpasses all other buildings".
In addition, like several other project critics, he fears that traffic would increase sharply due to the intended size of the plant: "Whole busloads have to come, that this pays off," says Schulz
However, what exactly is happening on the one-hectare area of the former restaurant is still open.
There is no operator yet.
"First, we have to have a conclusive concept," said Ralf Behrends.
Because this was still missing, the development plans were still variable.
