"It's impossible for anyone to come to Europe" - Dalai Lama - RT-News
Europe can't take in all the refugees, the Dalai Lama said, adding that the real solution to the refugee crisis lies in the Middle East.
The Tibetan-Buddhist leader urged to end the violence in the refugees' home countries.
The Dalai Lama addressed the problem of the ongoing refugee crisis at the start of his ten-day visit to the United Kingdom in his speech on the occasion of the opening of the Dalai Lama Centre for Compassion in Oxford, dedicated to ethics studies.
He demanded that under the circumstances the interests of humanity should be placed before the interests of the individual nations or even continents.
He also stressed that Western European countries' response to the refugee crisis was admirable, but not appropriate to the situation.
"It's impossible that everyone can come to Europe," he said in his speech.
Even though the Dalai Lama praised Germany's and Austria's efforts to deal with the crisis, he stressed that it was only a temporary solution.
"Taking care of several thousand refugees is wonderful, but in the meantime you have to think about long-term solutions on how real peace and real development, mainly through education, can be created for these Muslim countries," he stressed.
Ultimately, we have to think about how to reduce the killing in their countries.
We need to reduce the use of force.
"The use of force has never solved these problems," he added.
The Dalai Lama addressed the issue of violence and also commented on George Bush’s actions after the 9/11 terrorist attack, highlighting that the U.S. violent response led to a chain of uncontrollable events.
After 9/11, I expressed my condolences in a letter to President Bush, and I told him the way to solve this problem is nonviolence.
"I know his motives were good, but he used violence, and that caused unexpected consequences," the Dalai Lama said.
Four-fold increase in use of hand sanitizer by children in the last 4 years
Poison control centers in the U.S. report a nearly 400 percent increase in the number of children under the age of twelve who swallow highly alcoholic hand sanitizers, according to the Georgia Poison Center.
According to Dr Gaylord Lopez, director of the Georgia Poison Center, cases of proceeds of hand sanitizers among children underaged 12 who report poison control centers increased from 3,266 in 2010 to 16,117 in 2014.
"Children are coming to these products more and more frequently, and unfortunately a percentage of these end up in the emergency room," Lopez told CNN.
He said that some children drink disinfectant intentionally, while some do it to impress their friends or because of a test of courage on social media.
Videos on YouTube show how teenagers drink disinfectant only because of a cheap experience.
Teenagers allegedly mixed disinfectants with alcoholic mouthwash to get a harder drink.
Younger children can be lured by attractive scents of disinfectants.
"A child doesn't think this is bad for it," Lopez said.
Many of the more attractive (hand sanitizers) are those that are perfumed.
There is disinfectant with strawberry, grape and orange scent, which are very appealing for children.
Hand sanitizer contains somewhere between 45 and 95 percent alcohol.
Especially in toddlers, even a few splashes can lead to alcohol poisoning.
"It's highly concentrated alcohol," Dr. Stephen Thornton, medical director of the Poison Emergency Call Center at the University of Kansa Hospital, told broadcaster Fox 4 in Kansa City.
So, you wouldn't leave a sip of whisky standing around, but people leave these hand sanitizers within reach of children, and if children get at it, then it's a quick way to consume a lot of alcohol.
Nhaijah Russell, a six-year-old girl who had recently taken four splashes of hand sanitizer fragrant for strawberry sanitisfactory at school, was taken to an emergency room for treatment.
Her blood alcohol concentration was 179, twice higher than the threshold legally considered drunk in an adult, said Dr Chris Ritchey, an emergency doctor who treated her at Gwinnett Medical Center outside Atlanta.
Nhaijah lashed her words and was unable to go when she arrived in the emergency room.
Doctors monitored her overnight in a separate children's hospital for signs of brain trauma as the girl had fallen through the alcohol and bumped her head, Ritchey said.
"That's been very scary," Ortoria Scott, Nhaijah's mother, told CNN.
It could have been deadly for my child.
Lopez has advised parents and teachers to use non-alcoholic products or disinfectant wipes and to store hand sanitizers outside the child's reach.
In addition to alcohol poisoning, some disinfectants were linked to deaths.
In 2013, two women in Ontario women died after swallowing hand sanitizers containing a toxic, undeclared ingredient.
Employees of health authorities suspect that the product contained methanol, a deadly solution, rather than ethyl alcohol, which was noted as an active ingredient.
In January, three fourth-grade students in New York state planned to poison their "mean" teacher by laying out anti-bacterial products around the classroom, according to a police report.
The teacher is highly allergic to hand sanitizers and banished her from her room.
Police viewed the thwarted plan as "chatter" and referred disciplinary action to the school authority.
