Why I'll witness an execution in Oklahoma
A little later today, excluding with a late legal intervention, I will watch as a man is brought to death.
If it happens, and I hope it doesn't happen, I'll sit in a spectator gallery in the death chamber in the Oklahoma State Penitentiary when Richard Glossip is executed by the poison syringe.
He asked me to be one of the four official witnesses when he died.
The invitation took place for the first time in January about his best friend, and even though I was shocked, I agreed.
As the day approached, I began to be afraid.
So, why should I do it?
I agreed because I thought it would be a great way to tell the whole story about the death penalty.
It would provide a unique perspective.
It's true that journalists are allowed to see executions in the U.S., and since there are often more applications than places available, a macabre lottery takes place to decide who is elected.
Oklahoma has reduced the number of media seats from 12 to five.
Whoever is selected will observe it emotionlessly.
I can't do this.
I'm going to sit next to Richard Glossip's closest friend, with whom I've developed my own relationship over the months with phone calls and a two-and-a-half-hour visit.
I like this guy.
We often laugh a lot when we speak.
The prison authorities say I can't be a witness and a journalist, so I won't be allowed to take a notebook and pen to record what I see.
I'll just have to try to remember all the details.
An Oklahoma journalist accused me on Twitter of giving up my impartiality.
Here's the lesson.
Tell the story of the detainee as he wants, and you'll get access.
I've complained to her.
She acknowledged that she "could have phrased it better," but she still thinks it's wrong what I'm doing.
Richard Glossip had problem with some of the things I've said and written.
He doesn't tell me what I have to write and I wouldn't, even if he did.
I've told him that he can remove me from his list of witnesses at any
But he wants an international reporter present to write there.
If he dies, he thinks public relations will help the movement against the death penalty.
Kim Bellware, a Huffington Post reporter, will sit beside me.
If you want to know more about the case, please listen to the series of podcasts I've made.
I don't want to get back to all the details here.
I can't say for surely Richard Glossip is innocent.
His actions after the assassination of Barry Van Treese would make him guilty of being a beneficiary.
But I believe there is a strong likelihood that he is not guilty of murder.
I certainly believe that there is not enough evidence to justify a death sentence.
I believe his execution is wrong.
