Wayne speaks of exciting football.
He've been comparing his game on Saturday night to ours on Friday night and how exciting their game has been," Bellamy said.
I recall that in 2010 St George was criticized as a boring team, and Wayne said he's going to take care of a shit.
But now he has a team that can play and that we see as exciting, especially his young players with their speed.
That's the balling game you want to play when you've had that kind of player.
He made this statement in 2010, now he wants everyone to play his kind of football - not every team has two players like Anthony Milford and Ben Hunt so they can play like these.
I'm building stronger confidence because they're saying these things because they're a bit worried about having to play against us.
Six weeks ago they probably didn't think of us when it came to the big games.
Bellamy also pointed out that the game Storm against Roosters scored a higher result than Bronco's game against Cowboys.
Bronco's game was arguably a good game, I haven't watched it yet, but what was the result? 16 : 12 ?
Our game was 20:18, there were two more tries in our game, but is that an exciting game?
Or are taking breaks and no points anything exciting?
For me, it's the game plans.
If they mention us, it means we'll still encounter them at one stage.
The Storm team is vacant this weekend as they have won the right to receive either North Queensland Cowboys or the Cronulla Sharks next Saturday night in the pre-finals at AAMI Park. Ticket sales will start next Tuesday morning.
Serbian with €200,000 bail after drug charge
A man who may face a life-live sentence for his attempt to take possession of 40 kilograms of cocaine smuggled into Queensland has been released on bail of $200,000.
Marko Maksimovic, 29, was one of the five men arrested last month after federal police found 100 kg of the drug on the yacht Solay, which came from South America via Vanuatu and docked in Coomera in August.
Maksimovic was watched meeting his co-defendants at the marina over the course of the morning and later at Café Coomera Roadhouse, where police found 40 kilograms of the drug in a Toyota Echo.
Despite a potentially life sentence for attempting to possess a commercial amount of cocaine, Maksimovic's lawyer argued that there was no evidence that he was trying to own the entire crowd and that might have been in the cafe for a harmless reason.
The Supreme Court judge, Peter Flanagan, was concerned about the risk of escalation at Maksimovic.
He has clear relations with Serbia.
He often travels to Serbia," he explained to the court on Wednesday.
During the criminal case, he lied to the police about his whereabouts.
Maksimovic's mother and stepdad had jointly offered bail of 200,000 dollars as collateral to achieve his release.
"After reading the affidavits of the two persons, it is clear that the requirement of a security of 200,000 dollars represents a real financial burden," Judge Flanagan said.
But that alone won't be enough to stop him from not appearing, he warned.
Maksimovic has been released on bail under the conditions that he surrender his Australian and Serbian passports, reports to the police on a daily basis and adheres to a curfew from 18:00-06:00.
Accident on A5 near Karlsruhe: Driver's cab tears apart
In a rear-end collision with three trucks on Autobahn 5 near Karlsruhe, a truck driver was seriously injured on Tuesday evening.
Two of the three trucks involved had braked in front of a permanent construction site in the direction of Basel.
The third driver noticed it too late and tried in vain to swerve to the left at the last moment, the police said.
The driver's cab of the culprit tore apart completely.
The driver was taken to a hospital seriously injured.
The A5 between Bruchsal and Karlsruhe-Nord was completely closed for six hours until 2 p.m.
There was a loss of 122,000 euros.
Stevan Hogg used phone from girls to lure paedophiles to send pictures
A paedophile used an 11-year-old girl's phone to request images of child abuse.
Stevan Hogg took the girl's phone as it fell asleep, logging into an online messaging service from which he received a series of indecent images of children sent to him by another user.
The girl later woke up and discovered that the phone was missing and found it in the hand of the sleeping Hogg.
She looked through it, found the confusing messages, and immediately alerted her mother.
Police took care and saw the five images, then analyzed computers that were found at the address and discovered "relevant" web searches that used terms to find indecent images of children.
Hogg then claimed to officers that he did it, as it would lead to "pedophiles on the Internet" and he then "pretended to be a police officer to scare them."
Prosecutor Eilidh Robertson told Dundee Sheriff Court: "he said it would be an advertisement - that he was addicted to scaring people.
He admitted that the searches on the computer were carried out by him, but said it happened because he wanted to claim to be a police officer to scare paedophiles.
He was asked for his conversations with the unidentified person who sent him the pictures and whom he asked to send even more photos.
He said he did it to lure them.
He said he was sick in the head when he was drinking, and said he then wanted to see pictures of rape and murder.
Ms Robertson added: "When the girl found the messages on the phone, she called her mother and was shaking and crying.
She then also saw the indecent pictures on the phone and confronted the defendant with it.
The girl was interviewed and said the defendant used computers and Playstation to talk to girls who looked much younger than him.
Hogg, 23, Ward Road, Dundee, pleaded guilty within the meaning of the indictment to have taken indecent images of children on June 14, last year, breached the conditions on January 25 of this year, and not to have appeared in trial for a hearing on March 24 of that year.
Defensive lawyer Gregor Sim asked that Hogg not appear in the register of sexual offenders because one could argue that "no significant sexual element" could be found in his offense.
Sheriff Alistair Carmichael postponed the verdict until October to receive background reports on social work.
Hogg was granted bail in this case, but was held in pre-trial detention for hearings on other pending cases.
He was added to the list of sexual offenders even before the date of the verdict.
Smugglers dodge small border crossings to Germany
An increasing number of refugees are being taken up away from the major checkpoints on the German-Austrian border.
Many smugglers, for fear of discovery and arrest, increasingly allowed people at smaller border crossings
on the Austrian side out, a spokesman for the federal police said.
More than 1000 refugees have been picked up along the Inn border rise in the counties of Passau and Rottal-Inn since Tuesday.
They had reached the German side on foot via bridges and dam weirs.
There they are received by federal police officers and forwarded to registration.
According to Federal Police data, significantly more refugees arrived at Munich's main train station on Wednesday compared to the beginning of the week.
By 8.00 a.m. alone, around 700 people had arrived in the state capital, a spokesman said in the morning.
After the reintroduction of checks on the German-Austrian border, 1759 refugees had reached the city on Tuesday.
Stuttgart: Excursions of tropical storm "Henry" expected in the southwest
Meteorologists warn of storm gusts in the south-west from Wednesday afternoon.
Due to a low-pressure area over Western Europe, warm sea air is currently flowing into the country, the German Weather Service (DWD) said in Stuttgart.
The hefty low is the foothills of the tropical storm "Henry", which in recent days has moved from the Bermuda Islands across the Atlantic.
Violent storms and thunderstorms are expected, especially in Baden.
According to DWD, gusts of 60 to 90 kilometers per hour are still to be expected until Thursday morning in the Black Forest, the Swabian Alb and the Lake Constance district.
On Friday, the sun can be seen again.
The crowd does it: Every two years, the car manufacturers at the IAA show their novelties.
In 2015, there was mainly movement among the compact cars, which are currently selling particularly well.
Cars from the compact class are currently particularly in demand: According to the Federal Motor Transport Authority (KBA), this segment accounted for around 28.1 percent of new registrations in August alone.
This is also reflected at the IAA (Audience Days 17-27 September).
Between all the futuristic concept cars and new upper-class and luxury cars, many new compact models will be presented this year in particular.
One of the biggest premieres for the German car industry is Opel Astra, which will stand at dealerships in the autumn.
"The Astra is an extremely important model for us," Opel CEO Karl-Thomas Neumann said at the fair.
It belongs to the core of the brand.
In order for the volume model to sell well, the new Astra should not be much more expensive.
Opel is envisaging entry below the biggest competitor VW Golf, which starts at 17 650 euros.
The current Astra costs from 16 990 euros.
The Astra is available with a new 1.4-litre engine with 74 kW/100 hp.
In addition, the Hessians offer further diesel and gasoline engines from 70 kW/95 hp to 147 kW//200 hp, including three-cylinder petrol engines for the first time.
Unlike the most important competitors VW Golf and Ford Focus, the Opel Astra is initially only available as a five-door.
As a second variant, the station wagon will follow in the spring, which can already be seen at the IAA.
And in addition to the Astra at the IAA, what else is being used on new compact cars?
Renault Mégane: Renault sends the fourth generation of its Astra competitor Mégane into the race.
At the fair, the Mégane can be seen exclusively as a five-door in a conventional and an especially sporting GT version.
He goes on sale early next year, then also as a station wagon.
Infiniti: The Infiniti Q30 is completely new in the segment.
With the new model, the Nissan offshoot wants to find its position in the fast-growing segment.
The compact Infiniti with its curved lines fits fully into the design line of the brand.
The basic model will get a petrol engine with an output of at least 90 kW/122 hp.
But there is also a diesel with 80 kW/109 hp or 125 kW/170 hp on offer.
Suzuki Baleno: New in the segment is the Suzuki Baleno, which celebrates its world premiere in Frankfurt.
The model is offered with petrol engines, including a newly developed 1.0-litre turbo engine and an output of 82 kW/111 hp.
According to the manufacturer's specifications, the maximum torque is 170 Newton meters (Nm).
Sales launch of the Baleno in Europe is spring 2016.
DS 4: Not completely new, but completely renewed is the compact class model DS 4, which can be seen at the IAA.
On the outside, the designers of the noble Citroën offshoot mainly laid hands on the front section: Here, the radiator grille was redrawn and the changed headlights were equipped with LED technology.
In the interior, the infotainment system has been modernized, the number of knobs has been reduced by a touchscreen and, for the first time, Apple's CarPlay has been integrated
Added to this are a series of revisions and new variants such as the refreshed Mercedes A-Class or the Peugeot 308 GTI at the IAA.
JVA Berlin-Tegel: Five square meters of cell are inhumane
The Federal Constitutional Court has given the right to an ex-detainee of the Tegel prison, who was housed for months in too small a cell.
Due to violating their human dignity, detainees are generally entitled to financial compensation if they are housed in too-small cells.
This was decided by the Federal Constitutional Court in a decision published on Wednesday.
According to the judges, those affected can even hope for money if they were only locked up in too tight spaces a few days (Az.: 1 BvR 1127/14).
Thus, an ex-detainee was successful with his constitutional complaint in Karlsruhe: He was housed in the prison Berlin-Tegel between June and November 2009 in a single cell with around five square meters of floor space; the toilet was not separated spatially.
Afterwards, the man, who according to his lawyer is now back on the loose, had been transferred to a larger cell.
His official liability lawsuit because of the unworthy prison conditions against the state of Berlin failed.
But the constitutional judges overturned the verdict of the Berlin Court of Appeal and dismissed the case for re-examination.
You see the man's human dignity violated.
Kammergericht now decides on the amount of compensation
Even though, for legal reasons from the period in question, only a few days in prison are decisive for the compensation, there is a right to compensation, it said.
Otherwise, "an atrophiation of the legal protection of the personality would have to be feared."
The Court of Appeal must now clarify the amount of monetary compensation.
Constitutional judges have often strengthened the rights of prisoners in the past.
So they didn't concede damages to an inmate until April because he was locked naked in a cell.
According to a 2011 decision, the multiple occupancy of a cell without a separate toilet is also inhumane.
In addition, Karlsruhe on Wednesday also confirmed the Berlin Constitutional Court, which had certified an inmate in a parallel case in November 2009 an inhumane accommodation.
The then plaintiff was housed daily between 15 to 21 hours a day in a similar cell as in the Karlsruhe case for three months.
Berlin's prisons had also hit the headlines in recent weeks because of a lack of staff.
Prisoners are therefore trapped again and again for 23 hours, the aggressiveness increases, even brawls are said to have already occurred because of the tense situation.
According to information from the Judicial Press Office, 21 positions in Tegel are unfilled due to a lack of young talent, 19 are to be eliminated in 2016.
Angela Merkel and refugee policy: "We can do it"
Immigration of refugees is an enormous challenge - the chancellor is determined to accept it.
What's up to Germany?
According to forecasts by the Federal Ministry of the Interior, around 800 000 asylum seekers will come to Germany this year, according to Vice-Chancellor Sigmar Gabriel (SPD), Germany is even able to accept one million refugees.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) has demonstrated determination in the face of the unusual challenge: "I say again and again: we can do this, and we can do it".
The following overview provides an overview of what is coming to Germany.
According to different forecasts, between 350000 (estimate of the Federal Ministry of Construction) and 400000 (estimate of the Pestel-Institut) apartments are used annually - for the next five years.
That's almost twice as much as is being built so far (250000 apartments).
However, the lack of housing is dramatic, but above all in the case of affordable rental properties: 60000 to 80000 social housing falls out of the rental price commitment every year, and refugees will also apply for these particularly cheap apartments.
Federal Minister Barbara Hendricks (SPD) therefore wants to double the subsidy, from currently 518 million to one billion euros.
According to the Pestel Institute, this is far too little: 80 000 social apartments would have to be built annually and the federal government would have to invest a subsidy of 6.4 billion per year.
Scientists and the Federal Minister of Construction agree that there must also be more tax advantages for investments in housing construction.
According to estimates by the Institute for Labour Market and Occupational Research, it can be assumed that 55 percent of refugees in Germany can be employed in the long term.
However, the refugees in Germany would not be systematically asked about their qualifications in order to help them get started, criticizes Claudia Walter, project manager for integration and education at the Bertelsmann Foundation.
For the integration of refugees, however, it is crucial whether they find work in Germany.
Work is the key for social contacts, for appreciation in the host society, but also for the self-esteem of immigrants, says migration researcher Dietrich Thränhardt.
In Germany, according to Thränhardt, there has been a political turnaround: from a ban on work for refugees to the recognition of work.
While there used to be a ban on asylum seekers of up to five years old, this was reduced to three months in November 2014, as well as the residency requirement, which restricts asylum seekers to one place of residence.
In society - also because of the shortage of skilled workers - the majority has prevailed that refugees should be allowed access to the labour market more quickly.
A major hurdle for access to the labour market is a lack of knowledge of German.
So far, there is not yet a sufficient supply of general and job-related language courses.
Research also shows that workplaces are most often communicated through personal contacts.
According to Thränhardt, the establishment of networks is therefore the king’s road to work integration.
The Berlin school administration has found that only a few refugee parents give their children to daycares, an estimated ten to 15 percent: "For the uprooted parents, it is first of all very important to keep the family together," says Ilja Koschembar, spokesperson for youth and family in the Berlin Senate administration.
In order to convince the family nevertheless of the advantages of the day-care center, the administration distributes handouts in different languages.
Should the demand for educators increase sharply due to the large number of refugees, the Berlin Senate would try to increase the training capacities - for example, by allowing more technical schools.
In the countries, pupils without knowledge of German are prepared for lessons in welcome classes.
However, they are not always taught by fully trained teachers, but, as in Berlin, also by teachers who only have a qualification in German as a second language.
There is no shortage of them, says Beate Stoffers of the Berlin school administration.
The Deutsches Studentenwerk estimates that about 20 percent of refugees want to study in Germany.
In principle, no federal state prohibits asylum seekers or tolerated the study - the only exception so far was Berlin, but Interior Senator Frank Henkel (CDU) has now given up his stance.
Refugees willing to study could be facilitated access to the university.
Refugees with a residence permit or toleration will no longer receive benefits under the Asylum Seeker Benefits Act when they start their studies.
In principle, the latter does not receive a Bafög, since they do not fall under the circle of persons entitled to the allowance.
So far, tolerated refugees had to have lived in Germany for four years in order to be entitled to Bafög, from 1 January 2016 this margin is shortened to 15 months.
However, this funding gap must also be closed, Steffen Krach, Berlin’s state secretary for science, and the Deutsche Studentenwerk demand.
The students should get Bafög immediately.
Islam will play a more important role in the future, because many refugees are Muslims, and for many religion is an important part of its identity.
The state should successively grant Islam the same rights as the churches have, and develop the existing state-church-right into a new state-religions relationship.
It would also be important to establish an Islamic welfare association, which takes care of the new arrivals professionally.
Rights also include obligations.
For example, the representatives of Islam must agree on clear contact persons and be prepared to cooperate with the state in many areas and give it certain possibilities of control.
This also includes a willingness to act courageously against religious fundamentalism.
The chairman of the Central Council of Muslims, Aiman Mazyek, expressed concern on Thursday that religious conflicts could also be carried into Germany.
Those who wanted to carry out such conflicts in Germany "immediately forfeit their right to stay here, in Germany," he said on Bavarian Radio.
He proposed to translate the Basic Law into Arabic.
In the future, integration will be much more of an issue for the entire society than it is today.
This will also have to change the majority society, the dismantling of discrimination and racism will become more urgent.
As a result of European decisions, Germany has had an anti-discrimination body and a General Equality Act for almost a decade.
But it learns very slowly, as the NSU murder series showed, in which police and authorities systematically ignored the racist motive.
Equal opportunities for everyone: What migrant organizations and the interest groups of non-white Germans have long demanded and what international agreements such as anti-racism convention oblige Germany must become a reality in the next few years, if the country is to remain peaceful and its new citizens should use their opportunities - for the benefit of everyone.
In general, Germany is capable of providing medical care to the previously expected refugees, says Medical President Frank Ulrich Montgomery.
"As far as man power and capacities are concerned, we can do it," he told Tagesspiegel.
However, additional money has to flow into the system.
In order to avoid shortages, Montgomery calls for all arrivals to hand out health cards right at registration.
Services rendered could later be billed with the federal states.
And in the long term, it is highly questionable to feed off so many people with the limited catalogue of benefits that the Asylum Seeker Benefits Act provides for.
Otherwise, doctors are particularly concerned with language barriers.
According to Berlin-based health scientist Thea Borde, migrants have so far been enlightened worse about their illnesses than other patients.
Not only in clinics, but also in medical practices, interpreting costs would have to be covered, she demands.
However, many refugees would also be helped with more general medical contact points in the emergency ambulances.
And with a different diagnostic look.
Among refugee children, for example, there are significantly more untreated disabilities, and the adolescents are concerned with HIV education.
With the partly other clinical pictures, however, German doctors would be clear from their training, Montgomery thinks.
"Parasite infestation or tuberculosis are not so difficult to detect", and as far as the shortage of doctors is concerned, the refugees may even provide relief in sight.
The Syrians have a highly developed education system, several refugees are even doctors.
However, in order to practice, they would have to prove their qualifications and possibly take exams again.
According to estimates by the Federal Chamber of Psychotherapists (BPtK), at least half of refugees arriving in Germany are mentally ill: most suffer from post-traumatic stress disorder or depression.
Refugee children are particularly vulnerable, explains Dietrich Munz, president of BPtK.
A recently published study by the Technical University of Munich proves that one in five Syrian refugee children arrives traumatized in Germany.
Those suffering from post-traumatic stress disorder suffer, among other things, from so-called flashbacks with shortness of breath, dizziness, heart racing and fear of death.
Other symptoms include sleep and concentration disorders, severe terrificy and emotional deafness.
In order to prevent the symptoms from becoming chronic, early treatment is necessary, Munz explained.
So far, however, only four percent of mentally sick refugees receive psychotherapy.
Although mentally ill asylum seekers in the social welfare offices can submit an application for psychotherapy, processing usually takes several months.
In addition, in the social welfare offices, specialists or doctors who are not trained for mental illnesses would usually decide whether a therapy is necessary or not.
This often leads to miscalculations, Munz says.
Manege invites you to dream
With the new staging "Passion", the Circus Probst draws on the traditions of circus art.
The aim is to captivate every age group of fascinating acrobatics, animal dressages and unique entertainment.
Now Circus Probst invites you to dream in the manege on the Ziegelwasen.
“Our passion is the circus.”
"I can't imagine living without my circus family," says Stephanie Probst, an animal teacher and junior chef.
"Passion" - under this motto is the current manegen production.
The circus has been in the hands of Probst family for more than 33 years.
The gift for enthusiasm is in the family's blood.
"It is important to us to get people away from their everyday life and take them to a completely different world for two hours," emphasizes the 27-year-old junior boss.
"Manegenccocktail with cosmopolitan city flair," is how director Reinhard Probst calls his concept.
50 cities on the almost 3 000 kilometre long tour can now experience this flair.
The circus’s program convinces with talented artists from all over the world.
Awarded at the international circus festival in Monte Carlo, the Russian Stoliarov Clown Family captivates the audience with wit and charm.
The Castillo Brothers understand their craft, too.
With the heroes of the silent films, the Cuban duo wants to record loosely.
Ease and agilitance - with these words they identify.
Her humorous art on the mast is supposed to take women's hearts by storm.
In addition to numerous salmon salves, the acrobatics of Circus Probst is also fascinated with dexterity and articulation.
Balancing with the unicycle on a ball?
There's no problem for He Yuan.
Snake people fascinate and frighten at the same time.
Wang Lin from China is familiar with the oldest form of acrobatics like few others and fascinates with her agility.
60 employees from twelve nations offer diversity in all respects.
"The animals belong to our family," insists Stephanie's mother, Brigitte Probst.
Maladministrations in keeping circus animals are all too often an issue at various animal welfare organizations.
Stephanie Probst also comments on this: "If our animals are not well, we are not doing well either".
Around 80 animals from five different continents enjoy each caress.
The "four-legged artists", as they are affectionately called, are read from the eyes of every wish if possible.
We want to work with the animals and have fun with them.
If the animal doesn’t feel comfortable, it doesn’t want to go into the manege either.
So the top priority is to take good care of the animals.
The Probst family is particularly proud of their Zebroid Jumbo, a mixture of a Shetland pony stallion and zebra mare.
The two animals have lived together in an enclosure for eight years and nothing happened.
"But then, suddenly, our little jumbo was there," Brigitte Probst recounts.
The Zebroid is not yet ready for the manege, but it is the unique mascot of Circus Probst.
There should be something for every visitor.
"One thing is certain," says Stephanie Probst: "Whether five or 1 200 spectators, we always do our best and are there with all our heart."
Circus Probst invites you to its performances daily at 5pm and 8pm from 17 to 20 September.
On Sunday, the programme begins at 11 a.m. and 4 p.m.
Thursday is family day, for the performance at 5 p.m. the prices are discounted.
In addition, the animal show is open every day from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.
Zimbabwe's Robert Mugabe delivers false speech in parliament
Mr Mugabe, Africa's oldest leader, fell from a podium down the stairs earlier this year.
He remained unharmed, but the video of the case quickly spread via social media.
Parliament's opening was also clouded by claims by opposition MPs that they had received anonymous death threats and that they were warned not to accompany Mr Mugabe's address with booing.
Last month they had booed him and interrupted him during his State of the Union address in parliament - this is the speech he reiterated on Tuesday.
Movement for Democratic Change (MDC) Innocent Gonese said seven opposition politicians have received text messages on their mobile phones that warned them not to interfere with Mr. Mugabe's address.
"The news comes from a number that doesn't appear, but it's titled 'Death,'" he told journalists after Mugabe's speech.
It warns the affected members of parliament that immunity will end in parliament, and as soon as they leave parliament, their parliamentary immunity is no longer effective.
The chief spokesman said Morgan Tsvangirai-led party was "concerned" about the dangers to politicians.
Hewlett-Packard will cut up to 30,000 jobs
Hewlett-Packard is expected to cut 25,000 to 30,000 jobs as part of the restructurings and savings efforts in the company's services.
HP will split into two listed companies later this year, separating the computer and printer business from its faster-growing corporate hardware and service operations.
The expected job cut will result in a burden of over 2.7 billion-dollars from the fourth-quarter, HP said in a statement.
"These restructuring measures will enable a more competitive, more sustainable cost structure for the new Hewlett-Packard enterprise," said Meg Whitman, HP chairman and chief executive designed to lead the unit after the split.
"Hewlett Packard Enterprise will be smaller and more focused than HP is today, and we have a broad and immersed portfolio of stores that help companies transition to a new style of business," Whitman said.
As a stand-alone company, we are better positioned than ever before to meet the changing needs of our customers around the world.
Hewlett Packard Enterprise has more than $50bn of annual sales and "will focus on delivering previously unrivaled, integrated technology solutions for companies," according to a statement from the company.
It divides a company that was founded in the 1930s by the graduates of Stanford University Bill Hewlett and Dave Packard in order to make electrical installations and whose Palo Alto Garage was called "the birthplace of Silicon Valley."
HP has undergone a massive restructuring to handle the abandonment of traditional PCs towards mobile devices.
HP's measure, the world's second-largest PC maker and one of the largest U.S. tech companies, is the latest in this area based on the belief that tightly-focused companies deliver better performance.
Arsenal captain Mikel Arteta says team-mates must be behind injured Jack Wilshere before the game against Dynamo Zagreb
Wenger emphasized to Wilshere that it is only September and if his rehabilitation goes according to plan, there is no reason why he can’t play again before Christmas.
This upbeat scenario already means Wilshere will surely miss at least the next four games for England and given his history, his involvement in the European Championship next summer is certainly still uncertain.
The big shame on this current problem is that Wilshere had severely ended last season after a separate ankle injury and then regained back to his old momentum during the pre-season.
"This type of injury doesn't threaten careers," Wenger said.
It's a bone that didn't heal well.
That's not a bigger matter.
It was just an accident.
It wasn't related to his ankles or the injuries he'd had before.
That may be true from a purely medical point of view, but like Wenger also acknowledged, there is the associated danger of further injuries whenever a player tries to return in the middle of the season.
An unpleasant pattern also develops.
Premier Injuries Ltd's investigation has recorded 23 of Wilshere's illnesses since its debut six years ago.
He has also played 157 league games in the last five seasons in just 65 of Arsenals.
The broader picture for Arsenal is that in six of eight seasons since 2007/08 they have been one of the two Premier League clubs to record the most days of injury.
Wenger accepts the potential of a vicious circle, but Arsenal believe an improvement in the injury rate will continue during last season.
"Sometimes when you come back from an injury, you'll have a prone period of time, a time when you're having to strengthen your body and gain combat power," Wenger said.
Jack is young enough to overcome that.
I hope his body stabilizes, I'm confident he'll do it and he'll go through a career of the kind he deserves.
Arsen starts his Champions League season on Wednesday night against Dinamo Zagreb ahead of Saturday's games against Chelsea and against Tottenham Hotspur next Wednesday, and Wenger faces a rotation within the team.
Héctor Bellerín and Aaron Ramsey didn't travel to Croatia, which mean Mathieu Debuchy and Arteta are likely to start.
Wenger can also start with Kieran Gibbs and Olivier Giroud instead of Nacho Monreal and Theo Walcott.
"I'm trying to keep the balance and giving a little breather to the players who need it," Wenger said.
Even though Arsenal are clear favourites to advance through Group F, the Maksimir Stadium in Zagreb is among the playgrounds with the biggest intimidation effect in Europe.
Dynamo is also unbeaten after the "invincible" campaign launched last season.
The stadium was shaken by an earthquake on Tuesday evening, with 3.2 being measured on the Richter scale.
This year it was the 10th in Zagreb.
Wenger is of the opinion that Arsenal drew the lesson from last season with the Champions League surrender against Monaco and remains driven by the distant hope of leading the club to his first European Cup triumph with his management.
"This propulsion is immense," Wenger said.
It's never worked out at Arsenal.
We've been very close to it in 2006, but on the other side, I've been in business long enough to be realistic.
We're ambitious, but we're not dreamers.
FriendsFest: the comedy show that teaches us serious lessons on men's friendships
The absence of similar depictions in shows is evident, especially with regard to how the need for men to open up has now become even more desperate than ever before, as proving suicide rates among young men at a 15-year high.
In 2013, it was the main cause of death among men aged 20 to 45 in the UK, and it is three times more common in men than women.
In an effort to address the issue, the Campaign Against Living Miserably (CALM) announced 2015 as the year of the man with the aim of questioning the culture preventing men from seeking help when they need it.
Friendship is at the heart part of the fight to overcome this challenge.
A recent study of more than 2,000 high-school students with depressive symptoms found that if the sufferer had a sufficiently high percentage of friends with "healthy mood," his chances of recovery doubled.
For those who aren't depressed, a circle of mentally healthy friends halves the risks of mental problems.
But of course you have to have friends so that a friendship can work.
In the age of social media-led communication, with its emphasis on style over substance, the immediacy of the response is often more important than a meaningful contact.
Our digital lives have doubled the need to appear strong, fun-loving and successful, even if in reality isolation dominates.
This superficial success is dominated by what psychologists call extrinsic values; money, image, social status, everything at the expense of intrinsic values - our inner longing for personal development and friendship, which has a much deeper effect on our mental health.
And as a video game inventor and billionaire recently explained, money really can’t buy happiness.
The beauty of Friends was that this focus was turned upside down.
Each character was defined not by his work, but by his personality shining through interaction as a friend.
The humour of Joey and Chandler's frequent hugs, moments when watching football in cozy armchairs and Ross's longing for Rachel - all of this came from the knowledge that men can definitely refer to it, even if they often hold back to fully exploring their feelings.
As a telephone chaplain explained to me, "my listening role for people who feel depressed and who are suicidal has really clearly shown me how even the slightest interactions of friends can make a difference."
The men who contact us often don't feel able to talk to friends.
The old phrase "I'lbe coping with it" has an enormous impact, such as the feeling that it is perceived as a weakness when talking openly about problems."
Friends is one of the shows that comes to mind when I try to offer more long-term coping mechanisms.
She clearly presents the small moments of male friendships.
As much as my objective outsider status over the phone may also help people in moments of desperation, the long-term support comes from a personal network of family and friends.
And so if you’re visiting Comedy Central FriendsFest this weekend with the backdrop completely redesigned in celebration of the day, why not take your friends with you for a drink at Central Perk.
Or, even better, if you see someone sitting around alone in Starbucks, give them a smile and say hello.
You never know what difference your friendship could make.
Friends will be broadcast on Comedy Central from start to finish - the entire 236 episodes - for #FriendsFest
Di Maria meets to help PSG crush Malmö
Angel Di Maria celebrates with Blaise Matuidi and Edinson Cavani after his hit against Malmö.
Di Maria scores on his debut in the Champions League on Tuesday, helping PSG move past Malmö.
Here's the match report and the reaction to the game.
Angel Di Maria and Edinson Cavani keeped course when Paris Saint-Germain kicked off his Champions League season with an extremely comfortable 2-0 win against Malmö at the Prinzenparkstadion on Tuesday.
Di Maria scored less than four minutes after the start of his Champions League debut as a PSG player who launched the French champions, but afterwards they missed a number of occasions before finally increasing their lead when Cavani scored a header goal in the 61st minute.
With Real Madrid and Schachtjor Donetsk also in Group A, Paris needs a strong start against the Swedish champions as they not only seek qualification for the knockout rounds, but an improvement on the way to the quarter-finals in each of the last three campaigns.
But while they got the points, it wasn't a class game from Laurent Blanc's team with Zlatan Ibrahimovic's missed chances in front of goal. He was later substituted in the game against his hometown team and against the club with which he began his illustrious career as early as 1999.
"It's not easy to win at home or away in the Champions League. Therefore, it's a good start to win 2:0, cause problems for our opponents and look back relaxed, even if there are certain things we still have to perfect over the course of the season," Blanc said.
"That's a good sign.
We wanted to win and we won, even though I'm sorry we didn't score one or two more goals."
Ibrahimovic returned to the Paris team after his injury in one of three changes, who were able to hold a 2:2 draw on their own pitch against Borduard in the First Division at the weekend, but he was stabbed by Di Maria in the fourth minute.
The Argentine winger has been dubbed the man who can take PSG to the next stage in Europe when he was taken over by Manchester United last month, and he hinted at why, as he ran in Marco Verratti's pass behind the Malmö defense, before finishing past Johan Wiland nicely into the long corner of the net.
However, if anyone thought PSG would score a big win was disappointed.
Malmö, which Celtic beaten in a play-off to reach that tier, competed with nine national players on the field and was a heavy opponent with a five-man defence and two lined up deep in midfield.
But except for a shot from Nikola Djurdjic, who went well past Kevin Trapp's post in the 34th minute, they offered little in attack.
Instead, PSG themselves sometimes gave up, with some mispasses and poor finishes, not least from Ibrahimovic, who smashed on no less than five attempts alone in the first half and was then abandoned after Cavani deployed him after the restart.
Nevertheless, he proved much more skill when he brought his teammates into the game, and it was a pass from Ibrahimovic, whom Di Maria turned into a shot that was steered past the post by Wiland in the 52nd minute ...
The second goal finally fell shortly after the hour mark thanks to Cavani. The Uruguayan scored his sixth goal of the season after Ibrahimovic fished up a left-half flank from Maxwell.
That ended all the thoughts that Malmö could get back into the game, and only an excellent intervention by Wiland from the shortest distance to stop David Luiz could keep the end result low before substitute Ezequiel Lavezzi was deprived of a goal at the end.
Malmö's coach, Age Hareide, later admitted that PSG played a class above his team, and the Norwegian said: "Our last Champions League game was in December last year.
Since then, we've only played in the Swedish league that doesn't have the same level.
The qualifying games aren’t enough.”
Hats off Paris.
It was a great game of them!
Glencore raises $2.5 billion through stock sales
Swiss-based mining giant Glencore, which has been affected by collapsing commodity prices, raised $2.5billion on Wednesday through a share sale as part of its huge debt repayment plan.
The London-registered Glencore company said in a statement that it selled £1.6 billion worth of new shares to pay off its own debt.
The company, which has lost 57 per cent of its market value this year, is grappling with falling commodity prices as China's economic downturn depresses demand and has a devastating impact on markets.
Issuance of subscription rights brought Glencore's share price in trading early Wednesday morning a rapid rise to the top of the London stock market.
The stock jumped 2.77 percent to 131.60 pence on the FTSE 100 index, which opened 0.69 percent higher.
Glencore had last week announced the $2.5-billion-share sale as part of the broader plans to pay off around a third of its $30 billion in debt.
The company sold the new holdings to 125 pence per share, amounting to a 2.4 percent discount on Tuesday's closing price.
There were 1.3 billion shares worth up to 9.99 percent of the group.
Concerns regarding long-term inhibited growth in China have cut iron ore prices by about half, coal, copper and other commodity prices have declined by 20 to 40 percent.
Refugees arrive in Weimar
On Tuesday noon, 49 people reach the city administration in Weimar after a mostly long escape.
Refugees are from Syria, Albania, Afghanistan, Macedonia and Somalia.
The youngest arrival is a six-month-old girl from Syria.
The White House confirms Xi Jinping's visit to Washington
Chinese President Xi Jinping will make a state visit to the U.S. at the end of the month, the White House confirmed in a statement Tuesday.
The visit, Xi's first official in Washington, will take place on September 25 and retort President Barack Obama's trip to Beijing last november, the statement said.
U.S. President and First Lady Michelle Obama will also be the host of Xi and his wife Peng Liyuan at a state diner this evening after the two leaders held bilateral talks.
"President Xi's visit will provide an opportunity to expand American-Chinese cooperation on a range of global, regional and bilateral issues of common interest, but at the same time give President Obama and President Xi the opportunity to constructively address areas of disagreement," the statement adds.
XI, whose visit was initially announced in February, is expected to meet several U.S. technology leaders in Seattle on his way to Washington before moving on to New York City for the U.N. General Assembly in September.
Obama and Xi are expected to address a number of contentious issues, including Washington's concerns regarding China's alleged cyber espionage and its increasing self-confidence in the disputed waters of the South China Sea.
440-horsepower sports cars borrowed and driven to scrap
A 29-year-old wanted to fulfill a dream and borrowed a Ford Mustang.
On the rain-soaked highway then came the nasty Awakening.
At a company in Gotha, you can fulfill the dream and once borrow and drive a V8 US sports car Ford Mustang Boss 302 with 440 hp.
That's how a 29-year-old from Gotha County did.
On the A 71 near Arnstadt, the dream was then out and over.
The 29-year-old lost control of the sports car, got into a slingshot on a wet carriageway and drove into the guardrail.
The sports car suffered total damage, no one was injured.
The property damage is estimated at EUR 40,000.
Xavi: Pep Guardiola prevented 2008 move to FC Bayern
World and European champion Xavi has been playing for the al-Sadd Sport Club in Qatar since the summer of 2015
FC Bayern were obviously on the verge of the signing a few years ago from Spain's superstar Xavi, who played for FC Barcelona at the time.
Eight times Spanish champions, three cup winners, four Champions League winners, two times club world champions, twice European champions, once world champion - Xavi's title list is breathtaking.
In the summer, the now 35-year-old left FC Barcelona after 24 years and moved to al-Sadd Sport Club in Qatar.
But in an interview with Marca, Xavi now reveals that he was already facing a farewell from Barcelona before the 2008 European Championships.
“I was told Barça wanted to sell me.”
"Madrid won everything back then, we didn't win anything," he recounts.
And that's what FC Bayern registered as well.
Xavi: "There was an offer from Bayern."
"Rummenigge wanted me."
As a reminder: In the summer of 2008, Jürgen Klinsmann took over FC Bayern.
Guardiola was the reason Xavi didn't switch
However, the change did not happen.
And the decisive man for this works today for the German record champion: Pep Guardiola.
Xavi explains why: "Barça signed Guardiola and I played a great European Championship."
"Pep told me I wouldn't change that he couldn't imagine the squad without me."
"He got me with that."
Who knows how Bayern's 2008/2009 season would have gone with Xavi.
Xavi stood on the turf for Barca in the quarter-finals of the Champions League against Bayern over the entire distance, knocking out the Reds (4:0, 1:1) and celebrating the king-class triumph in the end.
Matt Damon downplays diversity in filmmaking
Matt Damon confronted African-American filmmaker Effie Brown during a discussion about diversity and said she was not needed behind the scenes.
During an episode of Sunday Night by HBO's Project Greenlight, the experienced actor Brown interrupted as she emphasized the need of alternative perspectives in relation to a black protagonist in the project's chosen script.
"If we're talking about diversity, then you're doing it in casting the film and not casting the show," Damon assured.
Brown reacted shocked, with insulted disbelief and shouted "Hoo! Wow. Okay," as The Martian's commentaries solidified.
Damon and Brown joined fellow producers Ben Affleck and the Farrelly brothers to discuss the show finalists.
"I'd like urge people to think about it - whoever will be the director - how they treat the character of Harmony," Brown said at first.
The only black person, and she's a hooker beaten by her white pimp.
Dear White People's producer campaigned for finalist Leo Angelos and Kristen Brancaccio for the directing rights to the film; Damon retorted that her directing "could give us something we don't want in the end."
After the discussion aired, social media exploded with disapproval about Damon's comments.
Twitter user Mr Pooni expressed his anger over the clip by writing "Matt Damon talks about the only black person in the room, so he can explain diversity to her - that's SO WHITE that it hurts."
The tweet earned more than 5,000 retweets and 4,000 likes since its release.
Damon's comments come at a time when accurate racial representation in the media is at the forefront of social discussion.
After teenager Michael Brown was killed by a white police officer in 2014, the Black Lives Matter movement gained effect nationwide, urging legislators and authorities to halt discriminatory practices against people of color.
In February, singer Prince made a nod to the movement during the nominated albums of the year at the Grammy Awards.
"Albums still matter," (albums still have their meaning) he said.
Like books and black lives, albums still have their meaning.
More recently, leaders of the Black Lives Matter movement have released a statement in response to Fox news personality Elisabeth Hasselbeck's question about whether the group should be seen as a hate group.
"The Black Lives Matter network is a love group," the leaders said.
Damon has recently been announced on a list of entertainers, producers and media moguls who are invited to visit the pope to discuss the representation of the Catholic Church in the West.
Whether he accepted the invitation or has already met the pope is unclear.
'American Ninja Warrior' crowns first champion
Isaac Caldiero became the first athlete to win the American Ninja Warrior on Monday evening, taking home the $1 Million Grand Prix.
The competition show, which has just completed its 7th season, had never experienced a competition participant who could complete all the stages of his obstacle course.
During the season finale, two men, Caldiero and cinematographer Geoff Britten, made it to the final.
The last task included climbing on a 75-foot climbing rope in 30 seconds.
Britten completed the task within the time he was the first athlete to complete the strenuous Phase 4 of American Ninja Warrior. However, Caldiero completed this challenge a full three seconds faster, making him the winner.
Caldiero spoke to E!News after the hard-won victory, still in shock over winning the €1 million prize.
"I mean it's amazing to think about, I've never earned more than $10,000 or had for life," Isaac E! said of his pile of money.
As suddenly .. as I can't even imagine how it is... the future is unknown.
Leading producer Kent Weed sat down with The Hollywood Reporter to discuss the finale and why Britten, even though he was the first ever to finish the course, won't receive any prize money.
According to the rules, the money goes to the fastest person.
"If there's more than one finalist, it's the one with the fastest time," Weed said.
How much the money is a wonderful prize and changes life, he receives the great gratification.
I don't think he harbors any grudges about it.
He's just so happy to have done what he's done.
He received masses of awards from fans.
He's such a great guy and a family man.
He's a hero for his children and his wife.
Geoff said to me, "If I'm to be beaten by someone, I'm glad it's Isaac."
And I think Isaac would've felt the same if they'd swapped roles.
America Ninja Warrior has been extended for an Eighth season, filminging will begin in the spring of 2016.
