Hundreds of refugees are to be accommodated in
A train with hundreds of refugees is on its way to Hesse.
His arrival was expected for the night on Wednesday.
The people who arrived there are then to be accommodated in emergency shelters in Wiesbaden, as the city Wiesbaden announced on Tuesday evening.
She reckoned with 450 to 750 incoming refugees.
Several sports halls of the Hessian state capital had been prepared for this case in recent days.
Up to 1000 refugees can stay there temporarily.
They will then be redistributed to other municipalities.
In the past ten days alone, 7500 people had arrived in Hesse, according to the Giessen government office responsible for the initial reception.
Frankfurt and Hanau have also set up emergency shelters in sports halls because of the large number of refugees.
Ischinger: German military measures in Syria an option
The head of the Munich Security Conference, Wolfgang Ischinger, considers military measures with German participation in Syria to be a possibility.
"I believe the decisive reflection on military action options must not take place without Germany," he said.
He accused European states of "collectively looking away."
Four years ago, one was glad that the chalice of a military participation in Germany passed by.
"Now the conflict lands on our doorstep," he said on Deutschlandfunk radio.
The former German ambassador to Washington also called for a massive increase in international aid on the ground for the refugees.
Austria controlled at southern and eastern borders
After Germany, Austria has also reintroduced checks on its southern and eastern borders.
Austrian Interior Minister Johanna Mikl-Leitner said on Tuesday evening on the Austrian TV channel ORF that no one would be sent back to Hungary even now.
Germany also did not send refugees back to Austria.
"No, that's what's agreed," she explained.
On the question of an agreement whereby Germany could accept a certain number of refugees from Austria per day, Mikl-Leitner said there had already been talks in this regard between the Director-General for Public Security and representatives of Germany.
Concrete figures she did not mention and referred to talks on Wednesday in Berlin.
In recent days, tens of thousands of refugees had come from Hungary to Austria via the so-called Balkan route.
Their goal is usually Germany.
Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) and her Austrian colleague Werner Faymann, together with several other EU leaders, requested a special summit for next week.
Germany had begun temporary border checks on Sunday.
The EU countries have so far failed to agree on a common approach in the crisis.
The EU interior ministers want to make a new start next Tuesday (22 September) at a special meeting for the binding distribution of a further 120 000 refugees.
In view of the strong number of refugees in Germany, the Confederation wants to support the Länder more strongly in the initial reception and distribution of people.
The Confederation will manage this together with the countries, Merkel announced late on Tuesday evening after almost four hours of deliberations with the prime ministers in the chancellery in Berlin.
Merkel underlined that those people who needed protection would also receive it.
However, those who do not have a prospect to stay could not stay in Germany.
This view had been impressively shared in the round.
The chancellor stressed that the state as a whole must now cope with a "huge effort."
Thousands of desperate refugees, meanwhile, are stuck on the Balkan
Hungary had closed its 175-kilometre-long border with Serbia on Tuesday night.
A few hours later, the government in Budapest announced that it would also seal off the border with a fence.
This neighboring country belongs to the EU differently than Serbia.
Hungary wants to prevent smugglers from seeking alternative routes via Romania, Hungary's Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto told the planned new border fence.
The Romanian government immediately condemned the plan.
There were initial arrests of people cutting the fence.
In Turkey, meanwhile, eyewitness reports said thousands of other migrants were on their way to the Greek border.
"Cassini" spacecraft finds ocean on Saturn's moon Enceladus
Under the ice tank of Saturn's moon Enceladus, a global ocean sloshes.
That's what researchers conclude from observations of the "Cassini" spacecraft.
Accordingly, the moon tumbles slightly on its orbit around the ring planet.
Although this wobbling movement is only small, it is still too big for a continuously fixed celestial body, according to a statement from Cornell University in Ithaca (U.S. state of New York) on Tuesday.
The researchers present their analyses in the journal "Icarus".
For a long time it had been known that there must be liquid water under the ice sheet of Enceladus.
For example, "Cassini" had already discovered ice volcanoes at the south pole of Saturn's moon in 2006, which spew water vapor and ice particles and thus feed the so-called e-ring of Saturn.
At least at the South Pole there must be an underground sea, the astronomers concluded at that time.
However, the expansion of the hidden ocean has not been clarified.
Rama is missing several weeks in 1860 Munich
The 1860 Munich has to do without offensive player Valdet Rama for about a month.
An investigation has revealed an adductor injury to the 27-year-old, the club said.
Rama complained of pain earlier this week.
According to Münchner Löwen, the diagnosis means "probably four weeks of forced pause."
"It's hard for us, Rama's a bitter loss."
"Of course we have to think now," athletic director Necat Aygün told the Bild newspaper with regard to the possible commitment of a club-less professional.
As a training guest, however, the Sixties initially presented a defensive man.
The 30-year-old Hrvoje Cale was most recently active in Belgium and had previously also been under contract with VfL Wolfsburg.
“The Croat currently has the opportunity to prove his skills.”
Goal-scorer Rubin Okotie may return to team training after his concussion next week.
Already on Thursday, the Austrian national player is to get into running training.
Okotie had injured himself in the game at Fortuna Düsseldorf (0:3) in a violent clash on the head.
Contrary to initial fears, however, he was left without a serious injury.
No diet fruit - mangos contain a lot of sugar
Mangos are exotic and juicy-sweet.
But if you want to pay attention to your slender line, you are often advised against them.
Because mangos contain a relatively large amount of sugar - especially sucrose.
Nevertheless, the exotic fruits are healthy: B vitamins as well as vitamins E and C are stuck in them. Above all, mangos contain a lot of vitamin A and its precursor beta-carotene.
Those who eat the fruits thus support eyesight, the immune system and metabolic processes in the body.
When buying, mangos should not be too soft or too firm and smell pleasant.
The shell color alone doesn't say anything about how ripe the fruit is - so green mangoes can also taste very delicious.
They should be stored at room temperature and consumed within a few days.
Then, of course, they taste pure or in sweet dishes.
A mango, however, also gives savoury dishes a special touch.
It softens the slightly bitter taste of some leaf salads such as chicoree and radiccio.
And a mangochutney goes well with fish, poultry, lamb and game.
The U.S. parcel service UPS is once again hiring up to 95000 volunteers for the Christmas business in the U.S.
The company has already begun recruiting seasonal workers, UPS said Tuesday.
Drivers in particular are being searched for.
As early as last year, UPS had boosted its workforce by 95000 temporary workers for the holiday season.
Faced with the booming online business, UPS and its competitor FedEx have been making precautions earlier since last year to be prepared for Christmas shopping.
In 2013, delayed deliveries had brought harsh criticism to the parcel services.
First foehnstorm, then distinctive cold front
Next night, in the valleys of the north side of the Alps, the Föhn breaks through, as Meteoschweiz announced on Tuesday.
On Wednesday and at night to Thursday, the foehn blows partly with storm strength through the Föhn valleys.
After blow-drying on Thursday morning, a striking cold front crosses us, bringing heavy rains, gusts of wind and a drop in temperature.
As MeteoNews writes in a statement, Switzerland is currently on the front of an extensive low-pressure area over the British Isles in a strong south-west current.
On the north side of the Alps, in the night to Wednesday, the hairdryer breaks through the valleys.
As a result, until Thursday morning, the foehn blows in the foehn valleys with gusts of 60 to 100 km/h, occasionally and especially in Urnerland partly with over 100 km/h.
The Föhn can occasionally penetrate into the adjacent Mittelland, and even hurricane gusts of over 120 km/h are possible on the mountains.
While in the north on Wednesday stormy hair dryer causes summer temperatures of 23 to 27 degrees, the moisture on the Alpine southern slope begins to jam.
This is where rain begins, which lasts until Thursday evening.
In total, south of the Alps in the next 48 hours, more than 100 liters of rain per square per square meter must be expected, after large amounts of rain have already fallen in the last few days.
On Thursday morning, the hair dryer runs out of air, as a result of which a cold front with strong, sometimes thunderous rain rolls from west to east over Switzerland.
Together with the cold front, the wind refreshes sharply to stormy, and the temperatures decline significantly.
The following days in the north present themselves autumnally with 15 to 20 degrees, in the south, however, it will be sunnier on weekends with up to 25 degrees.
Rome now has a Martin-Luther place
Almost 500 years after the Reformation, there is now a Martin Luther Square in Rome.
In the presence of city president Ignazio Marino, a hitherto nameless place in the park on the Oppio hill was christened the name of the German reformer on Wednesday.
On "Piazza Martin Lutero" he is now honored as "German theologian of the Reformation."
The park is located in the middle of old Rome next to the Colosseum.
The naming had been requested by the working group of evangelical churches in Rome.
Jens-Martin Kruse, pastor of the Evangelical Lutheran Congregation in Rome, reminded on Wednesday that the Protestants in the city of the popes for centuries as a minority could often live their faith only in secret.
With the square it becomes visible in a symbolic way that their tradition of the Christian faith is welcome in Rome.
Martin Luther (1483-1546) spent a few months in Rome as a young Augustinian monk at the end of 1510/11/11.
At that time, he perceived the city as a depraved scapegoat.
According to historians, his negative impressions may have strengthened him in his subsequent struggle against Rome.
Luther had then initiated the division of the Western church with his theses against the indulgence trade.
Hammer artist invested three years in personal interpretation of the Bible
Jesus with the (gilded) crown of thorns is one of the main works that Otmar Alt has painted for his Bible.
Klaus Altepost has written the introduction and updated Bible texts.
At the beginning was the word?
No, not at the Otmar-Alt-Bible, which has now appeared with magnificent pictures.
In the beginning was the picture for Otmar Alt, who formulated his very personal interpretations of biblical stories on paper and canvas in 150 motifs.
The 75-year-old has preserved his "language" with colorful colors and imaginative shapes - which is by no means synonymous with "funny" images.
On the contrary: adapted to the respective topics, this can seem quite bleak.
Otmar Alt has been working on his Bible project for about three years, for which he has made a subjective-artistic selection from the many thematic areas of the "book of all books".
Cycles have emerged that deal with topics such as crossroads, plagues, commandments or Our Father.
With this, he spontaneously fascinated several partners: This applies to Luther-Verlag with its theological orientation as well as to Kettler-Verlag with its focus on art books.
And there is above all the author Klaus Altepost, who has dealt with the respective Bible passages on the basis of painting.
He has interpreted the Bible passages selected by Otmar Alt and thus "translated" into today, into current contexts.
These texts are like verbal mirrors of Alt's painting.
They are embedded between the original Bible quotation of Martin Luther and a sentence by the artist, who formulates his very personal thoughts on the respective situation here.
This jewelry cover has golden letters.
Thus, the 75-year-old allows twice a deep insight into his soul, his emotions.
That's a high level of intimacy.
Emotions were ultimately the trigger for the Bible project: Otmar Alt, born in Wernigerode in 1940 and raised in Berlin, comes from a family that belonged to the strict Protestant Herrnhuter Order.
"That's when education happened with the cane," Otmar Alt recalls.
"I have tried to work this out in order to possibly get along again with the Lord Father."
His second concern is to help art to be given more importance again in a fast-paced time: "The perception of visual worlds today is characterized by advertising, and it is noisy.
Symbolism is supplanted.
Artists have a different desire and bliss to show things.
Pictures always have something mysterious and are not decorative.
"In this regard, I'm a dinosaur."
Otmar Alt wants viewers to "fall into" the pictures, let them take effect, discover and feel what power they have: "The picture seeks its counterpart".
Klaus Altepost confessed on Tuesday at the presentation of the Otmar-Alt-Bible in the Otmar-Alt-Foundation that "he has always" been a lover of Otmar Alt's art: "I like how he understands life with his paintings".
Alt did not pay attention to fully grasping the Bible, but selected the topics that were also important to him.
"This was not a cooperation on an equal footing," says Altepost: "This is about the pictures, the text merely explains."
For Hans Möhler from Luther-Verlag, this work is an invitation to "deepen his life and find freedom".
Otmar Alt's images speak for Möhler both the (biblical) language of love and that of responsibility.
A part of the originals - paper and canval paintings - will be presented for the first time in an exhibition that will open at the Otmar-Alt-Foundation on 15 November.
Start of construction for BMX-Bahn is approaching
Plans made, found space, companies commissioned, earth mounds modeled for a circuit: A year later, almost everything was ready - but even before the official opening of the BMX track, this was over again.
Failures in politics and administration caused annoyance, especially among local residents who didn't want to condone the railway.
So deported hills, new place wanted - now with formal planning and application for a building permit.
The approval will be received in October, the first local councillor Sabine Mosebach-Bock could now communicate in the children's and youth committee.
"I hope this year still make things visible happen," she said.
It should then begin without "further commemorative pause" with the work at the former indoor pool.
DFB intensifies commitment to refugees
The DFB is expanding its commitment to refugees until 2019, the Bundesliga sends a clear signal at the weekend.
Football responds to the worsening situation in Germany and assures policymakers of its help in addressing the problems ahead.
"I consider this to be our country's biggest challenge since reunification."
"In principle, no one can say, I don't care, I've got nothing to do with it."
"Everyone must be interested in getting to grips with this issue," DFB boss Wolfgang Niersbach said.
At the annual meeting with Chancellor Angela Merkel in Berlin on September 23, he wants to discuss the refugee situation in Germany intensively.
"I'm at the chancellery next Wednesday and I'll meet Minister of State Ms Özoğuz and the chancellor there."
"That's where the issue will certainly play a central role," he announced.
Football can and wants to help.
However, politics has to set the decisive course.
The German Football Association has therefore expanded its refugee initiative "1:0 for a welcome" by four years.
"Through the Egidius Braun Foundation, we have ensured that this programme will continue until 2019," Niersbach explained on the sidelines of the UEFA meeting in St Julian's/Malta.
Some federal leaguers also support this programme.
Already this weekend, the 36 professional clubs set a clear sign if they will wear the imprint "We help - #refugeeswelcome" on the left jersey sleeve instead of the usual sponsor logo (Hermes).
"We also want to make it clear that everyone should help where and how they can," Leverkusen's head of sports Rudi Völler told the "Bild" newspaper (Tuesday) and described the project as a matter of course.
"The refugee drama goes to everyone, no one is allowed to look away."
"We football leagues can, no, we have to take advantage of our radiance, our influence and our opportunities to help the many refugees who have already suffered so incredibly," Hertha manager Michael Preetz said.
Hoffenheim's managing director Peter Rettig spoke of a "celebrity sign of the league."
For the European Championship qualifying match against Hungary this Friday, the women's national team invites 100 refugees to Halle/Saale.
Niersbach described the support of refugees as "a great joint work of German football."
The fact that all 36 licensing clubs had started their own projects in their locations is great.
It is just as great that small and smallest clubs are active there.
The message "1:0 for a welcome," which we've spent, is lived in football.
"That's of enormous importance," he said.
This year and next, together with the DFB selection and the federal government, the association will provide 300 000 euros each for the integration of refugees.
"The clubs have already retrieved the entire sum for 2015," Niersbach reported.
Reports: SAP CEO loses an eye in accident
The chief executive of Europe's largest software company, Bill McDermott, has reportedly lost his left eye due to a fall in early July.
In the accident on the stairs at his brother's house in the USA, he fell after midnight on a water glass, which he had carried in the left hand, reports the "Wirtschaftswoche" on Wednesday.
"He's almost bled out, passed out, then dragged himself out onto the street - that's what he told us last week," the "WiWo" cites SAP co-founder Hasso Plattner as saying.
McDermott, however, can carry out his work.
"At the beginning of October, we will meet before the Supervisory Board meeting in Walldorf," Plattner said.
The 54-year-old manager was present at the meeting and "fully able to work."
McDermott announced in an interview with the "Süddeutsche Zeitung" (Thursday edition) that he will come back to Germany in October and continue to lead the software company.
"I'm fully there for SAP, fully in my job."
"Of course, I have been in regular contact with the Supervisory Board and its chairman Hasso Plattner all the time," McDermott told SZ.
McDermott usually commutes between Germany and the USA, but since the summer he has not been in the German SAP headquarters in Walldorf.
It is important for him to go public with such a personal story, the manager of the "SZ" said: "Something like this can happen to everyone."
"It's important to get back up when you're down."
