He is therefore just as unequivoked as a reserve driver for the two regular pilots Fernando Alonso and Jenson Button at the night race in Singapore at the weekend, just like a week later at the Japanese Grand Prix.
Football: BVB without Reus against Krasnodar: Weidenfeller in goal
At Borussia Dortmund, all hopes for a comeback by Marco Reus in the Europa League's first group game have finally shattered.
The national player will also be missing the leader of the Bundesliga in the duel with FK Krasnodar on Thursday (19.00 o'clock / Sky).
"Unfortunately, we can't fall back on him," Thomas Tuchel said Wednesday.
Whether Reus will be there again on Sunday's top game against Leverkusen, the BVB coach left open: "We expect a return from him to training on Friday".
In view of the high burden on his professionals, Tuchel will not only change his regular team at the Reus position.
That's how he announced a change on the goalkeeping position.
That's why Roman Weidenfeller occupies the place of regular keeper Roman Bürki.
"He's training at an absolute top level," the football teacher praised the world champion.
Whether Weidenfeller will be involved in all Europa League games in the future, Tuchel, however, left open.
He will most likely also play the second game.
Midfielder Julian Weigl thinks midfielder Julian Weigl is understandable: "Marco is not so wrong."
"I think we have a good chance of picking off the title."
Robobe cars: Government wants pioneering role for Germany
According to the will of the German government, Germany should be a technological pioneer in changing the car.
On Wednesday, the Federal Cabinet adopted a strategy paper from the House of Transport Minister Alexander Dobrindt (CSU), which includes investments in the expansion of digital infrastructure.
Dobrindt spoke of a "new age of mobility" in Frankfurt on the sidelines of the IAA car fair on Wednesday.
Automated and connected driving will increase road safety and cause less congestion, Dobrindt said.
He also wants to discuss the issue with his counterparts from the G7 countries, who will also meet with industry representatives at the IAA.
Among other things, the strategy paper of the Ministry of Transport emphasizes the importance of mobile broadband expansion and a speedy switch to digital radio.
In order to test new technology, a test track is currently being built on Autobahn 9 in Bavaria.
In order for the driver "no additional liability risks," the federal government wants to "examine the legal framework and, where necessary, adapt it to the new developments."
For example, Germany intends internationally to ensure that the permissible maximum speed for self-driving cars is increased from 10 to 130 kilometers per hour.
The German government also wants to campaign for an amendment to the Vienna Convention, which so far only envisages people as drivers.
In automated driving, the car increasingly takes the lead, the driver becomes more and more a passenger.
The technology is mainly based on sensors and cameras that capture the environment and process the data in a matter of seconds.
According to a study presented by the Ministry of Economic Affairs, highly automated driving on motorways will be technically possible by 2020.
Nevertheless, driverless vehicles on public roads "are only to be expected far after 2020".
The authors of the study estimate the added value in the field of driver assistance systems and the highly automated driving functions at the location Germany at around 8.4 billion euros for 2025.
"The digital networking of the passenger car can lead to more safety and efficiency in road traffic," said SPD transport politician Sören Bartol.
However, the question of liability for accidents and the safety of the personal data of the drivers must be clarified unequivocally.
However, the Germans still see self-driving cars skeptically - much more than French or Americans.
A survey by the Forsa-Institut on behalf of the testing organization Dekra, presented on Wednesday on Wednesday, concludes that only eight percent of Germans believe that autonomously driving cars will prevail in the next ten years, 32 percent don’t expect it until 20 years, 31 percent don’t believe it at all.
In France, 21 percent of the respondents expect autonomously driving cars by 2025 and 33 percent in the United States.
Anyway, motorists in Germany aren’t ready to rely entirely on a self-driving vehicle.
According to a survey by digital association Bitkom, only seven percent would agree to hand over the control on all roads throughout.
On the motorway, this could at least be 15 percent in flowing traffic, in traffic jam 45 percent.
A good one in four would leave control to the car under no circumstances.
Boll cancels team and double start at table tennis European Championships
According to the current status of the title fights from 25 September to 4 October, the Düsseldorfer only wants to pitch individually.
The final decision is due no later than 22 September.
"It's a huge loss for our team."
"The boys found out in the morning."
"However, we are strong enough to win the title even without Timo," German coach Jörg Roßkopf said during the European Championship course in Düsseldorf.
Boll was on the flight to a long-term approved sponsor appointment in China at the time.
The 34-year-old German champion is expected back on Friday.
"Withous Timo, it's a disadvantage, we're not such a clear favorite now," explained individual European champion Dimitrij Ovtcharov.
However, the Hamelner, who tops the seeding list in Yekaterinburg, also reminded us of the 2013 European Championships in Schwechat.
"At that time Timo was also missing, and we became European champions."
"That's what we should orient ourselves on," the world-ranked fifth explained.
