In the Champions League game at PSV Eindhoven, the defender was hit by Héctor Moreno during a tackling and replaced after a long treatment.
The first diagnosis in the 20-year-old team-mate of Bastian Schweinsteiger indicated a shin fracture.
Gaultier feels too old for an enfant terrible
It is a mixture of a very large fashion show and a wax-figure horror cabinet: the designer Jean Paul Gaultier (63) presented an exhibition about his work in Munich on Wednesday.
The highlights: mannequins that look terrifyingly human thanks to video projections.
They carry the spectacular creations of the French designer, who, among other things, became the big star of the fashion scene with Madonna's stage outfits - and by putting men in skirts.
"I can't say anymore today that I'm the enfant terrible of haute couture because I have white hair."
"It used to be bleached, now it's real," Gaultier said.
For the visitors of the exhibition "From The Sidewalk To The Catwalk", which can be seen from September 18 in the art hall of the Hypo-Kulturstiftung, it is fortunate that the fashion designer has changed his initially disapprovant attitude to a show about his person.
Actually, he was against it, because: "For me, an exhibition sounded a bit after death," he said.
If yoe dies, you're coming to the museum.
The impressive collection had already made a stop in Montreal, Canada and Paris.
"It's about so much more than just beautiful dresses," said the director of the Kunsthalle, Roger Diederen.
It’s about tolerance, acceptance and the vision of an open society.
The exhibits give an insight into the hard-to-see work of the man who has written fashion history without ever having completed an apprenticeship.
Chronologically, the show is not constructed, but it is an excellently choreographed Gesamtkunstwerk, for which a lot of effort has been carried out: In order to style the dolls, for example, a stylist has been hired specifically.
According to curator Thierry-Maxime Loriot, the creators have carted 14 tons of material in Munich.
Shortly before the start of the Oktoberfest, there was a special delicacy: a Lederhosen outfit with corset.
Heidi meets Madonna.
He's always been interested in strong women ("women are smarter than men") and for people who are a bit different, Gaultier said.
Gaultier's old Teddy can also be seen in the exhibition.
It stands for the designer’s first tentatively fashionable attempts to walk.
As a kid (he wasn't a good student and bad in football) he put on his teddy bras and dresses, Gaultier said.
Playing with a doll, his parents wouldn't have allowed him.
He later taught himself how to design fashion himself.
He stole fashion magazines and looked at the designs of other designers, as Gaultier recounted in Munich.
Later, of course, I bought them.
He could not pay the models for his first own fashion show.
They didn't get anything, but were allowed to keep the clothes.
Jewellery instead of gardine - Plauen lace as fashion niche
It used to be on the long embroidery machines in and around Plauen to make meters.
The embroidered fabrics and tips were created in large quantities for customers, who, for example, processed them into blankets and curtains.
This alone no longer works today, explains Andreas Reinhardt, managing director of Modespitze Plauen.
Instead, the fashion is important again.
In the early days of the Plauener Spitze, fashion played a bigger role, was then ousted by the home textile industry - and is now rediscovered as a niche.
Reinhardt cites some examples: lace in jewelry, accessories such as bags and regional costumes.
In general, the so-called vintage fashion, which is based on past stylistic epochs, forms an increasing order potential.
Those who do not serve niches in the textile industry in Germany are no longer on the market.
With us, they already account for half of all orders.
Modespitze Plauen, together with nine other companies, is a member of the Plauener Spitze and Embroidery industry association.
Only they are allowed to wear the protected label.
In the last 15 years, about 80 percent of sales have broken away.
There are almost no more private specialist shops and also wholesalers.
In this way, our products hardly reach the customer anymore.
The mass-produced goods are nowadays produced much cheaperly in the Asian region and distributed almost exclusively via chains.
In addition, the export is weakening, currently to Russia and the USA.
Sufficient reason for new ways.
The industry association is also trying to score points through environmental awareness, as Cordula Bauer from the stick pearl in Falkenstein explains: "Since this year, we've been carrying out a seal of quality, which shows that we're producing ecologically and socially compatible".
Reinhardt adds: "This also refers to the materials used".
Graduate designer Ute Schmidt teaches at the Schneeberg branch office of the West Saxon University of Applied Sciences Zwickau - at the Faculty of Applied Arts - and specializes in embroideries and tips.
In order to counteract the competition from the Asian region, you have to find high-quality and innovative products.
It takes time and courage.
There is an area of tension between the freshest ideas of the designers and the companies, which above all have to think economically," says Schmidt.
Therefore, an existing design is often varied only slightly in order to maintain a customer base.
A few years ago, textile designer Kati Reuter revived the historic snowball tip.
In some places, the machine embroiders small beads.
Plauen goldsmith Bianca Hallebach-Krausse was inspired by this: "Before, I couldn't do anything with traditional Plauen lace".
Now she's using the snowball tip for her jewelry.
In an elaborate process, she embosses the pattern in silver.
"Many people in the region identify with the theme Plauener Spitze - young and old," says Reuter.
It also delivers to the USA, Australia and Finland - especially to customers whose ancestors have lived in Plauen and the surrounding area and who want a memorabilia.
In doing so, I see my jewelry as a modern ambassador of the Plauener Spitze.
Disney plans new 'Mary Poppins' movie
You don't want to touch legends, but Disney still ventures into a new movie of his classic "Mary Poppins".
According to "Entertainment Weekly", however, it is not about a remake, but about a kind of sequel that is set 20 years after the first film with the magic nanny.
It's about nanny's experiences with the Banks family in times of global economic crisis in England.
Who Poppins plays more than 50 years after Julie Andrews is still unclear.
Rob Marshall, "Into the Woods"
Xi pushes to open the economy more broadly to the world
The economy needs to open more broadly on the outside in order to promote growth, President Xi Jinping told a group tasked with steering the reform on Tuesday.
"China should be committed to attract foreign investment and know-how and improve strategies for business start-ups," he said in an address at the 16th meeting of the Central Leadership Group for deepening comprehensive reform.
Promoting start-ups and advancing reforms will bring about new impetus and vitality and provide new space for economic growth, Xi said.
The leadership group has adopted a number of directives, including a negative list to regulate market access, depletion of border control policy, promotion of state-owned enterprises (SOE), private capital, and overhaul of the resident reporting system.
According to the statement, a negative list is created that identifies sectors and companies that are out of the question for investments.
The system is gradually being tested and improved by test programs.
The initiative is important because it gives the market a greater role in allocating resources, ensuring a rule-of-law business environment and making the market more open, the statement said.
The government will also loosen checks over the powers conferred on the companies and ensure that companies decide how to conduct their businesses.
The statement also said that China will allow border areas to explore new models of cross-border economic cooperation and new mechanisms to promote regional growth.
As part of an offer to open state-controlled sectors, more areas will be opened up for private investment.
"The government will not change its policy towards foreign investment and will protect the legitimate interests of debt-financed companies and provide better services to them," the statement said.
In addition, it is made easier for foreigners to apply for a permanent residence permit or "green card" by optimizing the requirements and facilitating the application process.
The right of the lawyers to carry out their activities, and to employ professional judges and prosecutors, was also discussed at a meeting.
Also attending the meeting were Premier Li Keqiang and key leaders like Liu Yunshan and Zhang Gaoli, according to a statement released after the meeting.
EU to double mercury limit in predatory fish
The European Commission wants to double the limits for mercury in large predatory fish and thus give consumers a significantly higher dose of the nerve.
The consumer organization Foodwatch, which has a corresponding working paper of the EU, sharply criticized the plans: "Risks and side effects of failed industrial and environmental policy are passed on with full force to pregnant women and toddlers," explained Matthias Wolfschmidt of Foodwatch.
According to Wolfschmidt, the limits of the nerve agent for large fish at the end of the food chain, such as shark or swordfish, are to be raised for "economic policy reasons" from one to two milligrams of mercury per kilogram of fish.
However, large predators are already so heavily polluted with mercury that, on the basis of the still applicable limit values, about 50 percent of catches may not be sold.
After doubling the limit values, only 14.5 percent would then be unsaleable.
In exchange for this easing, according to Foodwatch, the EU wants to exacerbate the limit values for other fish from the current 0.5 milligram to 0.1 milligrams of mercury per kilogram.
Foodwatch, however, described this as "trick" because smaller peace fish, such as carp, are usually burdened so low that they already meet the planned highs today.
The lowering of small fish is therefore "a perfidious distraction that alone helps the economy," Wolfschmidt explained.
Mercury is a highly toxic heavy metal for humans, which accumulates greatly through the food chain, especially in predatory fish.
The federal government therefore warns that "already small amounts, especially in unbornins, can cause damage to the nervous system".
With regard to the applicable maximum values, however, the Federal Environment Ministry declares on its homepage that a "health endangerment of the general population" is not to be expected.
Mercury enters the environment mainly through coal combustion.
According to written information from the Federal Environment Ministry, the German coal-fired power plants emit more than six tons of mercury per year, two-thirds of the total amount issued in Germany.
The concentration of mercury in fishes, for example in Elbe, Rhine and Danube, was "permanently and comprehensively exceeded," the "Spiegel" had cited from the paper in March.
Last year, Europe-wide warnings about mercury in fish were one of the most common reported health risks in the RASFF EU warning system.
Foodwatch calls on consumers to protest against the EU's plans at: www.quecksilber-aktion.foodwatch.de
